 We also have definitions surrounding empty properties that include what are classed as second homes as well. So, the definitions can be confused in some senses: a long-term empty property is an empty property that is empty and unfurnished, whereas a second home is an empty property but is furnished. So, that can essentially distort figures as well, and our priorities lie with getting long-term empty properties back into use.

 What we often come across as well is that not all properties are accounted for. The previous panel touched on it. Properties that are taken off the rating system are the most difficult properties to deal with, and they are not made available to empty property officers. That information is not available to us, which makes it difficult for us to establish which are empty properties and which ones we prioritise.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Osian Jones', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=4388&l=en', NULL, 195.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty properties; empty property; evidence session; inquiry; second home; second homes', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.375425', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775200', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In contrast to last year, because we’re not looking at Senedd reform between this year and next year, it isn’t quite such an extreme calculation. It would still be extremely painful. We don’t build in contingencies. We already over-programme our project fund and have to manage the budget dynamically from year to year. You’ll notice that in the last year, of course, we did not exceed our target of coming within 1.5 per cent of the operational budget, which is always a bit nail-biting at the end of the financial year. We make sure that best public value is obtained from every last £1 of that budget.

 In terms of the Government’s budget, then we’re in the same position as other bodies. Should that not be passed, then, as you will be familiar, we return to 75 per cent of the previous year’s budget, and then that reverting to 95 per cent if a solution is still not found. I think, at that stage, very drastic decisions would have to be made, which I expect we would be coming to talk to the Finance Committee about. It wouldn''t be possible to carry on with business as usual under those circumstances.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16319', '2026-10-05', 'Manon Bonner', '', '', '', '', 'Public Accounts and Public Administration Committee', '2. Scrutiny of Accounts - Senedd Commission 2025-26: evidence session', '16319', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16319?startPos=211&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-07T13:41:42.645352', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16319&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775740', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
