 Your point with regard to people moving and perhaps being missed during that period is something that we''re very aware of. There are different data sources that are used and that are updated. The register, the data, is being updated regularly as an open register anyway. But, perhaps, Michael, you could expand a little on the specific point about someone moving home and perhaps falling between the cracks.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '4. Scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning on the Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=9318&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:26.108798', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774394', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I''d like to take a moment to respond to some of the other comments that have been made, starting off with the leader of Welsh Labour. For clarity, Suella Braverman was born in London and is a British citizen, so would be entitled to social housing under this policy. Peter—[ Interruption .] Peter, as I''ve just clarified, EU citizens are not impacted by this policy when they have settled status, although I must say it is hard to take lessons from the Tories about seriousness on immigration. Kiera, the right to buy—[ Interruption .] The right to buy gives people the chance to own their own home, one of the greatest equalisers of wealth this country has ever seen. Now, can it be done differently? Of course it can be done differently. But selling one social house can give you the equity to build three more. To simply say that we will not allow—[ Interruption .] To simply say that we will not allow working families the opportunity to get on the housing ladder and own their own home is nonsensical. Jayne, it is not divisive to say that the Welsh Government should put the Welsh people first. And to the leader of the Welsh Greens, there is nothing more ugly and hateful than your party declaring that believing that the state of Israel can exist is racist.

 Of course, reforming allocations alone is not the whole answer. We need to build more homes, but here, too, Wales is being failed. Minister, you spoke about the importance of house building. However, we have exchanged very recently frustrations with the planning system. Planning decisions take months and sometimes years, holding up investment and house building. Rather than acting now to relieve the pressure on planning departments, the Welsh Government has embarked on a three-year review of the national planning framework. Wales cannot afford another three years adrift. The backlog is already severe. The Bevan Foundation estimates that Wales is short of some 9,600 homes. That is the equivalent of building another Llandudno, another Bangor, nearly building a whole other Rhyl. These homes were needed yesterday, before we even start to begin to meet today''s targets.

 That is why Reform wants practical planning reforms, making it easier to create desperately needed homes above high-street shops, seriously restricting the conversion of hotels into HMOs and relieving the burden on overwhelmed planning and housing teams. But while we build for the future, we must also be honest about the homes that we have now and who should have first call on them. This motion proposes a sensible and proportionate measure. It will help ensure that the scarce resource of social homes are available first to the people with the strongest claim on them. This is a principle that this Senedd should be able to support, and I urge Members to do so. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Tom Montgomery', '', '12183', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=17819&l=en', NULL, 154.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'hmos_and_standards; housing_supply; planning_system; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Private rented sector', 'Sector bodies; Opposition housing leads', '', 'HMOs; house building; planning system; right to buy; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.564241', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776970', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
