 So, electoral registration officers have had the power for, I think it''s over 10 years now, to already use data that''s held by the local authority for the purpose of electoral registration or verification. Some of them haven''t been using that as much as they could''ve done. Some of them have been using it really well, and there is a broader opportunity here for local authorities to see the benefit of sharing data. So, I know that Newport, for example, in their pilots, already used a lot of data sharing, which meant that the council had a lot of better insights about their local population, and other local authorities were building that as part of the pilots. So, there is a mixed picture already.

 I think part of the continuous improvement that we''ll see with this registration process in the future would be seeing the opportunity for better quality data, better comparable data sets in the future, and maybe introducing things like UPRNs, where they are appropriate.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Michael Kay', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '4. Scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning on the Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=10475&l=en', NULL, 126.5, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; social_housing; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', 'Housing minister', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; RSLs; council tax; registered social landlords; social housing', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:26.376402', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774527', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 And so you mentioned these cases that take 10 years to pursue. Well, we need 10-year time frames for action plans from Welsh Government, a sort of long-term direction for this, which then enables local authorities and presses local authorities to maintain that funding and resource stream and to continue reporting. And when reporting, think about how you''re measuring the activities of local authorities and not just counting properties brought back into use—because that can favour short-termist approaches, favouring counting empties—but the actual actions that local authorities are doing to bring empty properties back into use. You can also include cross-cutting themes, such as data sharing between departments and a corporate responsibility to encourage different council departments to work collaboratively in order to bring the empties back into use, because with 25,000 empties, you''ve got 25,000 different solutions.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Dr Henry Dawson', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 1', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=1482&l=en', NULL, 155.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty properties; empty property; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:24.990150', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774717', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Can I thank Louise Emery? Her knowledge, experience and years of promoting this industry, not just with words but with direct action, has been remarkable. We both share the view that the tourism sector is a key pillar to the economy in Wales, but Louise has gone further and she has shone a light on how this place, this Government, can work proactively with the industry. As we''ve heard, this industry can be key to us tackling issues such as youth unemployment. Particularly with all of the talk of those not in education, employment or training, you would be hard pressed to find an industry more experienced in providing youth employment opportunities than the tourism sector.

 Louise has shown also how on these benches in Reform we recognise the importance of sustainable tourism. We recognise that this, like any other industry, can have an impact on the area that they operate in. However, to address this we need targeted local action, working with local stakeholders. We don''t need nationwide regulations or more taxation—an approach that amounts to taking a sledgehammer to the issue.

 I''d also like to thank my colleague John Clark for highlighting what the industry itself is telling us. They have still not seen a return to pre-pandemic visitor numbers and the challenge that this industry has in attracting visitors here against our international competitors in Europe is stark. With all that in mind, we must accept that surely our best way forward is to work with the industry to break down cost barriers, so businesses can better compete with international markets. A visitor levy won''t do that. As already highlighted, most overnight stays are already subject to VAT, and, in comparison to our European rivals, we are already taxing overnight visitors at a much higher rate. Applying a visitor levy on top of VAT will simply be implementing a tax on top of a tax. Additionally, this approach will hit working and low-income families hardest. It''ll have little impact on the high-end tourism across Wales, but will dramatically hammer more affordable holiday options and smaller businesses. Governments of the past have had the approach of trying to tax or regulate this industry into growth. That will never work.

 I''d also thank Steven Rodaway as well. You''re absolutely right. The industry has spoken. The 182-day rule is too restrictive and it is damaging the industry and the people that work within this sector.

 And I want to turn to the amendment that the Government has put before us. In the amendment, it says that they will keep the 182-day rule under review. Members, we''ve heard today about the pressures the industry is under and we understand the importance of this industry to every corner of Wales. Saying that you''re going to keep it ''under review'' is not good enough. The industry is struggling. It needs our support, not a Government holding response. Also, what I will mention is it has been brought up today that the 182-day rule would help address the housing crisis in Wales. It hasn''t. We still have a housing crisis. We have a housing crisis because we''ve had decades of a Welsh Government failing to build enough homes. Please don''t try and scapegoat that to the tourism industry. If you want to address the housing crisis in Wales, get on and build more homes.

 Then the amendment says that the visitor levy is about giving local councils a choice in introducing the levy to help fund improvements to the tourism sector locally. This is a false narrative, Members. As someone who has spent the last four years in local government, I know first-hand the pressures that councils are facing. Any moneys from this levy will not go to tourism investment. If you put this power in local councils'' hands to levy additional taxation on local businesses, they will use it to ease their financial pressures, mostly in education and social care, not pressures caused by the tourism industry. Additionally, as the Government should well know, there are already provisions in the local government funding formula to support councils to deal with tourism-related pressures. Councils with high numbers of tourists already receive an uplift in funding. So, if you''re concerned about councils managing tourism-related pressures, you want councils to invest in the tourism sector locally, or want to protect council services, then fund councils properly. Do not scapegoat this again to the tourism sector for Cardiff Bay funding failures.

 Members, we have heard that the sector is a vital component of our economy in Wales, and we''ve heard how this slate of legislation is burdening it. We have the opportunity to tell the industry that we stand with them. Members, reject the amendment and support our motion.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Tom Montgomery', '', '12183', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=15002&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'Sector bodies', '', 'visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:17.086362', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767100', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 And it is a first step, as I mentioned, as we start to think about how we can go further with the town centre taskforce that has been set up in terms of developing the high street and supporting the high street as the heart of a number of communities. But also the challenge you set forward is a challenge that is about the system as a whole, of course. You speak particularly about city centres, which have large shops and have important anchor stores in the middle of those cities. It is important, of course, that we think about striking the right balance in terms of supporting the smaller businesses, which is a continuous call coming from Members in this Chamber, but with an eye to not disadvantaging the larger businesses too much. So striking that balance is important. In terms of publishing the exact multipliers that will be operational for 2027, that will have to wait until we have the budget for next year, when that is introduced. That is when that will become operational, will become known. 

 And then on the point about the full reform of this system that is needed, I think, as a Senedd—. I hear it from different parties and from different Members: rather than making small changes here and there and sometimes making them in a reactive way, reacting to whatever issue is affecting the economy, we need to look at this area as a whole, in its entirety. I would hope, as we discuss and test out more of the ideas relating to business rates, we will come to a consensus across this Chamber with regard to the political appetite that there is to address this work. I would be happy to be part of that discussion.

 On these cliff edges you mentioned—when £51,000 is the limit and if you''re one side or the other of that, it''s a 30 per cent difference in how much tax you pay—this is one of the considerations certainly that needs to be looked at in the future. So, there are further changes that I think the Government could look into making, with support from this Chamber, and then there is a broader question about addressing and reforming the system as a whole. I''m sure that the discussion on that will continue.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=10258&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates; non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.126148', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769214', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Cabinet Minister, you also said today that this national conversation will help co-ordinate a range of plans and strategies in the programme for government. But can you explain exactly what that means? Are other Government policies dependent on the outcome of this conversation, and if so, which ones?

 Lastly, Cabinet Minister, you say that you want this to be an open, inclusive and meaningful conversation, but today, we''ve heard about just three events across the whole of Wales—in Cardiff, Aberystwyth and Llandudno. So, what reassurance can you provide, Minister, that communities in every part of Wales will have a meaningful opportunity to be heard? And will you commit to publishing what people tell the Government and what policy changes actually come out of this conversation?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Vikki Howells', '', '5107', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=8780&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'planning system; programme for government', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:59.030410', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772996', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The programme for government commitment is clear. We want to ensure that integrated care is delivered closer to home, with prevention in particular at its core, to support earlier diagnosis, reduce admissions and delays in discharge. We have many ideas on how, in general, the delivery can be improved in rural areas, but health officials, I know, are engaging with the health board and Pembrokeshire County Council specifically on the development of that integrated centre in Fishguard.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=783&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:18.705678', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770725', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Removing profit from the care sector is an issue that we worked on during the co-operation agreement, and that legislation is now in place and we''re moving towards implementation during this Senedd term. The previous Government did sign a statement in 2025. As a new Government, we do support the principle of creating a care system that is more focused on families, is based on rights, is trauma informed, is locally rooted and is ambitious for all children, whilst removing profit from childcare and ensuring that no young person leaves care and goes into homelessness also. I do very much hope, given the fact that you will have heard this, Deputy Minister, that there will be an opportunity for you and the Member to discuss this issue further.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16313', '2026-10-06', 'Heledd Fychan', '', '426', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', '16313', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16313?startPos=7257&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'homelessness; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'co-operation agreement; homelessness', 0, '2026-10-09T13:35:10.146544', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16313&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776072', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
