Published 16/07/2026 | Reading Time minutes

As Brexit approached, over 30 new forums were set up for the UK and the EU to take decisions on their relationship, including on matters which are devolved to and/or affect Wales. The forums were set up under two treaties:

Focusing on the 23 intergovernmental forums set up under the TCA, this article presents updated analysis of Welsh Government attendance at these meetings, where important decisions that affect Wales are taken. It finds that there has been a strong pattern of Welsh Government attendance across meetings, with a few notable exceptions.

Explainers on these arrangements and their history can be found in a series of drop-down menus at the end of this article.

Attendance by Welsh Government is recorded in meeting minutes . Figure 1 uses a colour code to denote attendance (green), non-attendance (red), or where attendance is not specified (yellow). As attendance is at the UK Government’s discretion, it may be that the Welsh Government was not invited to attend a particular meeting. Information on whether or not a devolved government was invited is not published.

Since January 2021, there have been 113 meetings. The Welsh Government was at 80 of these (70.8%) but didn’t attend 22 (19.5%). For the remaining 11 meetings, their attendance was not confirmed.

Figure 1: Welsh Government attendance at meetings held under the UK-EU Trade and Cooperation Agreement (2021-2026)

Source: Meetings of the EU-UK Partnership Council and Specialised Committees under the Trade and Cooperation Agreement (European Commission)

While there is a strong pattern of Welsh Government attendance at meetings, there are notable exceptions for meetings on air transport, law enforcement and judicial cooperation, VAT and intellectual property.

Academic research by Royles, Rowe and Minto concluded that, while devolved government involvement in TCA meetings has improved over time, participation from Scotland and Northern Ireland has been greater than that from Wales. This they attribute to “their more enhanced devolved competences”, pointing to greater attendance at the meetings listed above on air transport, law enforcement and judicial cooperation, VAT and intellectual property. Their research formed the basis of evidence submitted to Sixth Senedd committees in 2024.

Although the Welsh Government can act in an international capacity , it cannot enter into legally binding commitments. Only the UK Government can do this for the UK, and does so on behalf of the four nations.

The Welsh Government’s role in this process is important because UK-EU agreements either effect Wales or fall in devolved competence. The Welsh Ministers and the Senedd are responsible for putting these arrangements in place under the Government of Wales Act 2006.

The parts of the TCA that fall in devolved areas include fisheries, health security, the economy, the environment and some aspects of trade. Reserved matters, like trade or law enforcement and judicial cooperation, also have a significant impact on cross-border cooperation.

29 new joint forums oversee the TCA which are shown in Figure 2. The main forum is the Partnership Council, which is supported by committees and working groups. The participation of civil society is also provided for, as well as parliamentary cooperation between the UK and European parliaments.', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/representing-wales-the-welsh-government-at-eu-meetings/', NULL, '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd Research', '', '', '', NULL, 15.0, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'cooperation agreement', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:48.050537', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The spending review also looked at five challenges or opportunities facing public services in Wales. We looked at housing, elements of health, we looked within education, economy and transport; so, transport, for example, looked at buses. We brought together analysis and evidence, along with independent policy thought. So, the work was led by Welsh Treasury but also colleagues outside those policy areas, so that we had a real cross-Government approach. So, we''ve got a good suite of evidence and information and recommendations there for Ministers to look at in the new year. But, of course, the new Government will want to look at different things as well, and there''s a very clear emphasis on maximising our funding, the effectiveness of our funding—that''s bound to be a feature of the next spending review as well.

 I think also, just to build on your previous question about the longer term budget—. So, the UK Government will reset our budget next year in the next UK spending review. So, we''ll have an updated three-year resource settlement and four-year capital, which will take us beyond this Senedd term—so, 2030-31 and 2031-32. Again, there''s an opportunity, bringing together the spending review work in Wales as well as the UK one, to give another longer term settlement if that''s what Ministers decide to do. We''d be happy to come back and speak to the committee as that work evolves in the new year if that''s of interest.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Emma Watkins', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=7242&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.640481', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774270', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think as well it''s the unintended consequences in terms of costs that automatic voter registration brings. As Sandra said, their pilot identified 12,000 electors that weren''t on the register, but they didn''t have enough data to automatically register them. The system that exists within the UK means that, once you''ve identified somebody, you need to send an invitation to register within 28 days, you then need to send two follow-ups and potentially door-knock those properties. It''s those costs as well. While, obviously, it''s the electoral registration officer''s duty to look for people and get them on the register, it''s the scale at which automatic voter registration builds that picture, and that''s going to be an ongoing cost throughout time as well.

 I think it''s also the knock-on consequences for the annual canvass proces. Because this is being done just on the local government register and does not affect the parliamentary register, it prevents you using the more cost-effective routes that the canvass offers you. Most properties now go down route 1, which doesn''t require a response to that sort of communication. When you''ve got an automatically registered person and you don''t know whether or not they can be registered for the parliamentary register if they haven''t actively gone and registered themselves for that, it then leads to them having to go down route 2, which requires a response, which requires a chasing cycle. So again, it''s the knock-on consequences of funding and different electoral registration processes throughout the year, which also requires funding, as well as the ongoing support and maintenance for delivering automatic voter registration.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Clare Sim', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=7112&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.720043', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769986', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 And then there are things, as I''ve said, that we can do through legislation. Actively exploring different measures for regulating increases in rent, for example, effectively is really important. We can look at what''s happening in Scotland. There are learnings from Catalonia too. It''s important that we look at what works well, and also look at potential drawbacks. But significant steps need considerable policy development, evidence gathering, and cross-party and cross-sector engagement, which is why we have outlined our ambition to bring forward that second renters Bill as part of a two-phase legislative approach.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=2338&l=en', NULL, 110.0, 'High', 'digest', 'private_rented_sector; housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Private rented sector', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'renters; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:25.146332', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769022', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, before I bring today''s proceedings to a close, I have the result of the secret ballot for Chair of the legislation Committee. The number of votes were: for Cai Parry-Jones 15, Cristiana Emsley 52, abstentions seven, and spoiled ballots zero. So, I declare that Cristiana Emsley is elected Chair of the Legislation committee. Congratulations.

 And that brings today''s proceedings to a close. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Kerry Ferguson', 'Deputy Presiding Officer', '12177', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Statement by the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health: Public and Preventative Health', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=14287&l=en', NULL, 33.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:59.804721', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773088', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 To give you a full picture, the areas of strength included various things. They talked about effective meetings, good papers. They talked about good support from the board secretariat. They spoke about leadership from the chair, an effective governance framework and engagement with staff. We place a lot of emphasis on transparency and staff engagement—we have staff coming in as observers at those meetings—and a good attitude towards risk management. So, those were strengths.

 Certainly, there were things that we want to develop. I''ve referred to one already, with the diversity of membership. One specific point that I would draw to the committee''s attention is that we have one member who is appointed by the auditor general, and it''s a strange system. The person is an excellent person, but it''s a strange system. And perhaps, if we were to look at a change in legislation, that might be something that we could look at.

 They feel that the board needs to ensure that the auditor has a succession plan for senior staff. I was part of the changes that we''ve already heard about, and, at the time, I was speaking regularly to Adrian about his plans, and I have those same discussions with Catherine.

 Also, the board has an interesting role, and sometimes a complex one, in terms of Catherine''s responsibilities as the auditor general on the one hand, and chief executive on the other. Our responsibilities then vary. The advisory role within the auditor general role is something that we''ve tried to clarify in terms of what ''advisory'' means—because the independence of the auditor general is very important—and how we ensure that.

 We have also ensured that the meetings are shorter and more slick, more effective. We review the governance arrangements annually and we are continuing to engage with staff. Personally, I try to visit every office at least twice a year to speak to staff, and as I said, we publish papers where we can. There are some that we can''t, but we publish papers to staff, the minutes and so forth, and we welcome staff as observers. So, yes, there''s always room for improvement, but we have plans in place to move things forward.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Dr Ian Rees', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=2809&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.315514', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774185', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
