This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=986&MId=16303', '2026-10-12', '', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '', '16303', '', '2026-10-12', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Legislation Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-18T07:16:21.259843', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 One thing I would say is that it''s not as simple as just expanding the legislative drafting office. The Government as a whole contributes to the process. So, you have a number of people involved, ranging from the policy officials and economists looking at the financial issues, and lawyers obviously providing advice on the legal context and ensuring that what is done is done correctly within the legal framework. So, it''s a multidisciplinary process. At the end of that process, we come along to actually draft the words on the page, but there is quite a bit more to it than that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dylan Hughes', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=717&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.165073', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770374', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The Minister agreed that flooding is devastating, but says that dredging was cut back because experts now understand flooding better, because of rules protecting nature, so it''s only done on a case-by-case basis where evidence supports it. The relevant committee''s own research also said that there''s no evidence that less dredging is a major cause of more flooding across Wales. Flooding has come up in several past petitions, and the committee last time flagged it as a key issue for this Senedd.

 So, the options available to us: we could write to the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee to invite that committee to consider the issue as part of its work programme for the seventh Senedd, and revisit the petition following receipt of that committee''s response. Then we can undertake further work to seek further evidence and better understand the risks and benefits ourselves.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16259', '2026-09-24', 'Joe Martin', '', '12146', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. New Petitions', '16259', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16259?startPos=1623&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T12:02:31.132262', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16259&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772192', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, I''ve asked the Bill committee to be ready—and they''ve agreed—to go to speak to every party and, if I have the time, I will be participating too, on several occasions, I hope, in order to include everyone in the process. And that''s how I see things working. As my colleague says, of course, there are some Bills that we''re talking about now where, well, it''s likely, I hope, that there would be consensus.

 I hope there''ll be consensus on the tribunals, but to arrive at that consensus, we need to have that relationship, the conversation and the discussion, and for that to be a meaningful discussion—not a tick-box exercise, but to ensure that we listen to what the parties are saying, and so, in my view, develop better legislation anyway.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=1724&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.335197', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770377', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Let''s be clear on the implications for additional spending on NHS waiting lists. We support these proposals. They are, after all, the continuation of the successful policy that the last Welsh Government adopted. You do not need a supplementary budget in place to spend in this area. You can carry on the investment now and regularise that spend later in the year. Fearmongering that this money is at risk simply does not bear informed scrutiny.

 And as to the suggestion that if the supplementary budget failed, there would be no money given to ALN at a later date, if that''s accurate then it''s pernicious, it''s vindictive and it''s going to lead to widespread industrial action. The supplementary budget before us has £0.25 billion of unallocated financial resources. I''ve outlined before, the Chair of the Finance Committee has repeated it: that''s not normal. You could use £100 million, as we''ve requested for ALN, and still have a substantial cushion to manage in-year spending pressures.

 Llywydd, we genuinely tried to warn the Government that they were headed for the rocks. My advice now is just as genuine: withdraw the supplementary budget today, negotiate with the teaching unions to resolve this dispute you''ve blundered into, and then bring back a supplementary budget that actually starts to meet the asks of pupils and teachers. When you do, we''ll be here waiting, ready to vote for it. Diolch yn fawr.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=24201&l=en', NULL, 49.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', '', 'Finance Committee; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.612649', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767897', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Councils need both the data and the powers to act. Last December, you tabled an amendment that removed section 35 from the Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation (Wales) Bill. You said social housing was—and you have said it—a universal resource. That provision would have allowed councils to decide who does and does not qualify for social housing in their area—the same kind of power English councils use to apply residency tests. 

 Will you now reintroduce that power for Welsh councils, so that social housing in Wales goes to the people of that country, and who have been waiting for years for social homes?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Francesca O''Brien', '', '12190', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=6126&l=en', NULL, 132.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'housing_supply; homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation; homelessness; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.566113', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776720', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The Deputy First Minister will provide an update to the Senedd today on the process of rolling out the childcare offer, as well as information on the cost modelling. The additional investment through the supplementary budget is going to allow us to expand thousands of additional funded childcare places, focusing immediately on expanding childcare for two-year-olds. This is always something that happens in phases: the 12.5 hours, the 20 hours, and then onwards to the youngest children. That''s how it''s planned, to increase the financial envelope and the workforce at the same time. But we''re clear that this transformational change requires a childcare sector that is strong and sustainable, a skilled workforce, and a budget that works for providers as well as parents.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=3302&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.481769', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767536', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
