 We do do a lot of digging underneath the results as well. We really analyse the free text comments that come out. We triangulate what we''re getting with further engaging with staff, and this year, I think, we''ve invested in a new tool to give us an even better run at our staff survey figures. 

 So, I think that, coupled with really strong engagement with our trade union side and so on, gives us an opportunity—with challenge from the board on the results as well—to really dig underneath those results and get to grips with what it''s telling us about our organisation, whether there are any gaps and so on. Obviously, this is going to be crucial. I think we''re live with the survey now, or in a few days, at least, and, obviously, I''ll watch those very closely. It will be the first ones, partly, as I''ve come into post; nobody wants them to slip when they''ve taken up a new organisation. But that will be a really important baseline as we come into this, as we are through this transitional year, and as we work and engage with staff on our new strategy going forward. And, inevitably, that will lead to some change for colleagues, and having a really robust way of running the staff survey will be part of how we will track whether we''re getting the right buy-in to where we''re going. 

 The new provider also enables us to do pulse surveying as well, so we''ll be able to do targeted pulse surveys around particular things. So, I am satisfied coming in—No. 1, I think they''re a great set of scores, and No. 2, I think we''ve got some really good tools and techniques to help us really understand and engage our staff base.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Catherine Mealing-Jones', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=1018&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:50.911693', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774291', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 One thing we have highlighted is that, in the last Senedd term, there was a commitment to have a Welsh housing survey. Now, that, at the moment, is looking at housing need. So, it''s to help us with how many houses we need to build, but also looking at property conditions. If that''s going to be a census-style survey, why couldn''t it be helping us with the, ''Well, where didn''t we get responses to that survey?'' Rather than relying on UK-wide census data every 10 years, could we be getting the mantle, because, ultimately, our council tax data is there for taxation purposes, not for the use that we''re trying to say, in terms of enforcement and incentives and all that sort of thing. So, I think we need to start thinking differently about the data that we can collect.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Wendy Dearden', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '5. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 3', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=14684&l=en', NULL, 195.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; council tax; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry; second homes', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:26.567648', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775391', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
