 As part of that, there are two groups that work in tandem, with one member crossing from one to the other. One is an AI governance group and the other is an AI opportunities group. That helps us to manage how AI is used. Translation services are something that is a key element for us here, and seeing how we can potentially use AI to help with some of those issues. An initial test with it wasn''t great when you check how it works, so it''s work in progress, but, as you know from AI, it learns. So, it''s how do we do that as well.

 But it keeps coming back to being controlled and evidence led. There are examples where AI is being used in committees, and looking at evidence that comes to committees and being able to summarise some of that evidence, especially when you have a large amount. So, using the tool, but obviously keeping a view of that—. The human being then keeps the overall control over it, making sure that it is a tool to be used, rather than—. But that gives you a flavour of the type of work where AI is happening in the Senedd.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=3703&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.474372', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777557', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Speaking of uncovering the truth, First Minister, I''ve got a couple of quotes to read to you today:

 ''Reform would look at the nation of sanctuary and say Welsh people come first when prioritising council housing, and that is really, really important to me.''

 Let me read you my second quote:

 ''Millions are being wasted on the tree-planting project, which is absolutely incredible, that we are sending money in Wales to Uganda.''

 First Minister, these were things said by a Senedd Member that now sits on your benches. Do you agree with these comments, ''yes'' or ''no''?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Helen Jenner', '', '12153', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=1042&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'council housing', 0, '2026-09-23T11:54:34.347859', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770727', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But it can be challenging to maintain up-to-date records with that, in particular because at times the data collection for them is very reliant on self-reporting with council tax resources. And then we''ve also got fairly limited and variable communication between empty property officers and council tax enforcement teams, which maintain these records, in order to update them on what''s happening with individual properties. Properties that could be occupied as residential, such as flats over business units, often also fall outside of the definitions. So, the Land Registry data only captures a proportion of the ownership of the housing stock. We have similar information deficits, so it''s something that you''re trying to capture that is constantly in flux, and often quite difficult to capture due to the variety involved.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Dr Henry Dawson', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 1', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=160&l=en', NULL, 155.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; council tax; empty properties; empty property; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:24.799458', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775137', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In a past life, I was vice-chair of planning on Wrexham council, and the issues of HMOs have been a hot potato for more than a decade—not for the reasons stated by the people over there, but because an overconcentration of HMOs can impact a community in terms of overdevelopment, highway matters and waste disposal.

 Houses in multiple occupation are a small but integral part of the housing mix. They provide accommodation for people who can''t afford a house or a flat, and there are loads of reasons for that. Let''s hope that no-one here loses their job or income. Let''s hope that no-one here has a health issue. Let''s hope that no-one here has a messy divorce, a breakdown in the family or, God forbid, is forced to flee domestic violence or coercive control. These are all issues that can lead people to look for a housing option that suits them temporarily.

 I won''t go into other issues, because Kiera Marshall and Peter Fox have very eloquently mentioned them, but I''ll mention one: students living away from home are living in HMOs. Does the Member want to prevent all foreign students from having accommodation? Do you? Perhaps you can talk to the vice-chancellors about what that would do to our Welsh universities.

 As it happens, the owner of the HMO next to me is a responsible landlord, but there are unscrupulous landlords who are cramming in as many people as possible to maximise profits. That''s the real issue with HMOs. In the wrong hands and without sufficient oversight, they become dumping grounds for people with very real problems of substance misuse and mental health issues, but without any support. You don''t care about that, obviously. You''re just concerned about the people who look and sound different to you.

 People in my town are paying £87.50 a week for a shared room. There are eight other people in the house, with no communal space, apart from a family kitchen and a small back garden. That''s not the fault of tenants, it''s the fault of those unscrupulous landlords who fail to treat tenants as human beings. And these are working people, not on benefits. They''re nurses, they''re care workers, they''re people I hear getting up at 6 o''clock in the morning to go to the industrial estate.

 I said earlier that this focus on a certain type of housing was odd. I think we know why—people have told us why very eloquently. The track record of the party involved is all about seeking to divide and rule, fuelled by hatred, not compassion. Since the UK Government moved from housing asylum seekers and refugees in hotels—I believe there''s one hotel in the whole of Wales now—those who were busy rioting, burning and hating outside those hotels have looked for a new focus, and they''ve found it, haven''t they, in HMOs.

 Like those unscrupulous landlords, they don''t see the people who live in HMOs as human beings; they see them as inferior, as problems. What we''ve seen today is people deliberately conflating foreign nationals with asylum seekers, deliberately conflating people on benefits with foreign nationals. It''s a mess of a debate. It''s scaremongering and demonising for clickbait and social media. It''s not a serious proposition; it virtue signals their hatred for people who look different, who sound different. It could be written in crayon.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Marc Jones', '', '350', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=16602&l=en', NULL, 159.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'private_rented_sector; hmos_and_standards; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', 'Opposition housing leads; LGHP Committee members', '', 'HMO; HMOs; houses in multiple occupation; landlord; landlords; multiple occupation; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.459094', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776959', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 With regard to the local government sector, we have more challenges—I think the committee may be briefed on that already—and we only received 16 of the 32 local government body accounts that we needed to receive in. And to be perfectly honest, the quality of those accounts was not in all cases where it needed to be, and there were a larger number of audit issues arising. If there are audit issues, it''s our responsibility to work with the audited body to bottom those out, which means that we don''t meet the deadline. Quality must come first, and we have to issue a safe opinion. And that would explain, therefore, why there was a slight reduction on the accounts delivery side during 2025-26.

 For this year, the picture is slightly better. I don''t know if you want me to go on about this year, but we have received 22 of those 32 sets of accounts that we need to deliver by the 30 June deadline. And we''re continuing to work closely with the sector to improve the quality and the timeliness of accounts delivery. Adrian, prior to him leaving, had written to the local government bodies, and I have written more latterly. So I think we''re doing a lot of work in this space. We''re holding good-practice events and working really closely with them. We''ve got very good relations with them, and there are signs things are improving.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Ann-Marie Harkin', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=581&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:50.816880', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774283', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Sorry, one last specific question from me before we bring other Members in on movement in provisions. Provisions are a slight dark art, I would have thought, in the accountancy world. There''s zero movement in provisions. What are the total current provisions? Is it £0 or are you just reflecting the movements in the provisions there as £0?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Sam Rowlands', '', '8734', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=475&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:36.963793', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777365', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
