 We''re going to start with the legislative programme, looking at the capacity, principles in terms of legislation, and then we''ll look at the Sewel convention, the programme to improve the accessibility of Welsh law, consolidation and codification, and then statutory instruments to finish with. So, there''s a lot to cover today. So, I would ask my fellow Members to be succinct with their questions and for us to have similarly succinct answers as well in order to get through the work we have to do today. So, I''m going to ask, first of all, for Kiera Marshall to start off with the questions. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=216&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee; legislative programme', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.065658', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770349', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 You have to also remember the fact that within the eastern side of England, livestock density is lower, and where we move west, the livestock density, including Wales—. Some of the highest, if not the highest, sheep density in Europe resides in Wales. But that westward spread of disease of BTV-3 is, as you rightly highlighted, a clear and present danger for us in Wales. We have over 200 BTV-3 cases confirmed today in Wales, and I anticipate that that number of confirmed BTV-3 cases will continue to rise over the coming days and weeks. The action that can be taken right now and over the coming days, weeks and months is to vaccinate for BTV-3.

 I''ve spoken at farmer meetings and veterinary meetings over recent weeks. We have had co-ordinated communications campaigns. Back in the spring, you''ll recall, we did an extensive bluetongue vaccination campaign through Farming Connect. Through these farmer meetings and bringing stakeholders together through the working group that I chair and under my office, we share that map that illustrates what has been seen in the south-west of England. The Animal and Plant Health Agency have published that map online—it''s freely available to all and everyone who would want to look at it.

 But what we have seen in the south-west of England, and now the north-west of England—if we just focus on those two areas specifically—has been an explosion of BTV-3 over the summer months. We''ve seen high livestock density and high temperatures, which accelerate the transmission cycle of this midge-borne disease. That BTV-3 seeded into the south-west of England last summer.

 There is that clear and present danger in Wales right now for BTV-3, but the action now for next year is that it''s crucial to vaccinate. Because if we take the lesson of the south-west of England, that is what we have to prepare ourselves against for Welsh farming, Welsh livestock health and welfare, and Welsh family businesses on farms. Because we can see that potential devastation coming towards us, and vaccination against BTV-3 is the only and best protection that vets and farmers can work together to deliver for the farmers of Wales, learning from the south-west of England.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Richard Irvine', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=5615&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; working group', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.050758', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772047', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 If we are to talk about the effectiveness of the board, the report that we had noted that the membership of the board was not very diverse, and you''re quite right to raise that. It''s a concern to us, but it is, to an extent, out of our hands. So, the message has been conveyed to this committee in the past, and the same message will be conveyed again in appointing the new member, namely the importance of diversity in board membership. We do recognise that and we are doing what we can to address it.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Dr Ian Rees', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=2693&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.282128', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774167', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Nobody in this Chamber wants to see people killed or seriously injured on Welsh roads, and I think anybody who says that is disgraceful. The question is whether making 20 mph the default across thousands of miles of roads is the right way to achieve the objective of decreasing road traffic collisions. Three years after the policy came into force, we now have more evidence to examine. As Jason said, 91 people were killed on Welsh roads in 2025, 9 per cent higher than 2024. More significantly, Department for Transport figures from last year show the highest number of deaths on Welsh roads since 2019.

 I''m aware that the increase in deaths was not necessarily on 20 mph roads, but the aim of an overall reduction in road traffic deaths has not been achieved. There is also a great deal of confusion. I regularly get stuck behind people going at 20 mph on roads with 30 mph or 40 mph limits. The confusion, I think, contributes to accidents. I literally had somebody last weekend on a 60 mph road going 20 mph. I overtook him, he put his hand on his horn and beeped me. I pulled up and I said, ''Are you okay?'' And he said, ''No. Do you know this is 20 mph?'' And I said, ''Mate, it''s not.'' And then we made friends and he was from outside the area. You can argue the details, but it''s undeniable that this policy has failed in its overall objective and it has come at enormous cost to the Welsh economy. 

 We also need an updated economic appraisal. We haven''t had one since 2022, perhaps because the Welsh Government wouldn''t like the results. But if they are confident in the policy, then let''s see the data. Presiding Officer, this should never have been about ideology; it should have been about what actually works. If we want safer roads, let''s target dangerous roads, improve road design and crack down on dangerous driving rather than imposing a blanket limit—[ Interruption .]—across the entire country. I apologise—default. Let''s end the anti-motorist agenda that punishes all road users. I urge all to vote with our motion to scrap this crazy failed scheme.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'David Mills', '', '12171', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Reform UK Debate: Default 20 mph speed limit', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=13675&l=en', NULL, 93.5, 'High', 'digest', 'rent_controls_and_affordability; taxation_of_property', 'Private rented sector; Tax & finance', '', '', 'rent control; tourism tax', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:49.384411', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773788', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Cabinet Minister, Citizens Advice Cymru has reported that fuel debt is the No. 1 debt issue facing people using the charity''s services in many areas across Wales, including my own constituency of Newport and Islwyn. We know the Plaid Cymru Government has vowed in its first 100 days to plan to review the existing Warm Homes scheme, but many people, including my constituents, want and need action now. So, with the price cap having just increased, and no prediction for it to come down any time soon within the autumn, what support does the Welsh Government intend to provide people who will be experiencing fuel poverty? Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Natasha Asghar', '', '336', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=1542&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency', 'Property & energy', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Warm Homes; fuel poverty', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:15.596636', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766828', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
