 If I could go on—. Should I go on, Chair, to notification periods, or does somebody else want to come in?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=4442&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.256218', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769948', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 You''re asking about progress. In terms of the money that we''ve already spent, you''ll know that there was £55 million in total allocated in the supplementary budget, which we have put to work in terms of expanding childcare and also with some capital grants to enable that to happen. So, 78.9 per cent of all two-year-olds are now offered childcare. Over 13,000 new two-year-olds have been offered a childcare place. Over 9,900 new two-year-olds attended their funded childcare, and over 2,000 of those children attended funded childcare in a Welsh-medium setting. 

 As I said, we are now expecting, and I''ve had assurances from local authorities—. Many Members in the Chamber will probably see this happening now—their local authorities putting out that offer to parents of that age group. That''s happening progressively in the way that we said it would. And then, by the end of the financial year, all two-year-olds in Wales, in every single local authority, will be offered 12.5 hours of funded childcare. I think there are quite a lot of statistics there for you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=1102&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'draft budget; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:47.798369', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773520', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But, again, issues related to post offices are not devolved to Welsh Ministers. As I mentioned in an earlier response, the UK Government''s commitment to post offices includes plans to retain the size of the network as it is, and that means looking for new businesses to offer those services when individual post offices close. But it also includes a commitment to locate post offices in town centres, where possible. Because, as I said, I agree—we know that post offices increase the number of visitors to the high street, as well as providing those crucial services.

 Our programme for government does include a commitment to look at those options to rejuvenate our town centres, and that links to our work in terms of the town-centre taskforce that we''ve established. So, I''m sure that ensuring that post offices can be part of vibrant high streets could be part of that work too.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=2391&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:47.973415', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773565', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Secondly, on child poverty, you have my absolute support for all the measures on child poverty that you''re introducing, and the universality of it. That is essential, for Cynnal and, obviously, the roll-out of universal free childcare as well. Just to put on record as well, it was me as a Liberal Democrat who made the deal with Labour for the 12.5 additional hours from Flying Start to be rolled out. That was a deal two years ago, and we want to see more. And I do want to just come back to your Deputy First Minister and myself. When we were previously on the back benches, we were like a stuck record, saying, ''Give us targets on child poverty, give us targets.'' So, I''m really interested: what are your targets and when will we hear about them? Diolch yn fawr iawn.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Jane Dodds', '', '4983', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=11649&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.177535', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771026', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 This consultation is one issue that I''m focused on, and I think that''s crucially important. But I''m also focusing on delivering the target of 20,000 new social homes by the end of this Senedd term. I welcome your commitment to increasing the social housing stock too. The right for everyone to have a home is at the heart of the work that I am doing in this Senedd. But take a step back now. The development plan and the national development framework also look at issues related to housing. It also looks at issues related to economic growth.

 My hope is, having listened and had those conversations, that we will have a new plan that will provide a realistic vision for the development of all parts of Wales. As you mentioned, that''s crucially important. The plan and framework must enable us to distribute wealth and growth to all parts of Wales, retaining the economic powerhouses of Cardiff and Newport, but also distributing that economic growth to north, mid and south-west Wales. So, we need meaningful regional strategies too, which will be part of the long-term vision.

 You mentioned reducing bureaucracy and planning processes. Well, bring your ideas forward. Everyone tells me that we need to accelerate the planning system, that we need to make things easier, and I agree, and I have some ideas as to how we can make progress in that area. I''m very happy to hear practical ideas as to how we deliver that, in practice, on the ground, so that we do remove some of these barriers that can hold developments back, including crucial housing developments.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=9416&l=en', NULL, 148.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'housing_supply; planning_system; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; planning system; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:59.129805', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772997', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We have also been having a conversation across Cabinet on this. So, we''ve established a cross-Cabinet taskforce—the first to be established is the one on child poverty—and I chaired that on Monday. That was part of our discussions: what kind of targets would be most effective? Because I think it''s important for the committee to understand and to acknowledge, perhaps, that there are different ways of measuring poverty. You can think about relative income poverty. That is something that is used very broadly, and of course it''s important, but that''s not the only measure. We also consider measures that evaluate hardship, things such as material deprivation. That''s something that the UK Government has been looking at as they develop their child poverty strategy.

 And then, of course, there is deep material poverty, rather—. I''m also in favour of thinking about wider evidence, that is, as I was saying, how things are felt. I remember reading reports, for example, by organisations such as the Bevan Foundation, that talked about the fact that there isn''t enough money in children''s pockets, for example, to be able to go to the theatre or that they can''t go to a friend''s birthday party because they don''t have the money to buy a birthday present. So, there are qualitative ways as well, I think, of measuring poverty.

 So, I think we have to recognise that there isn''t one individual measure that''s going to capture in its entirety families'' and children''s experiences of poverty. But, of course, I would say and acknowledge as well that we do need to set ambitious targets and, very importantly, those milestones along the way, to maintain that focus on our actions to ensure that they—. And, as I said, that''s across Government, to ensure that we focus our policies and that we know what works and what doesn''t work.

 And, of course, we are bringing forward some new measures, such as expanding the provision of free school meals, such as ensuring that we improve the way that Welsh benefits reach families, such as the Cynnal pilot, for example. So, we want to ensure that we can measure the impacts of those initiatives as well. So, as I said, we are doing this work. I would like to hear the view of the committee on that, if that''s something that you would want to look at in terms of what targets you believe would be most effective and appropriate. But, as I said, I do expect to consult on the new strategy and on the targets, of course, early on next year.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=382&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.597511', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771131', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 To get back to the very good point raised by Gwyn in relation to the community energy issues, I was lucky enough to visit Hwb y Gors in the Amman valley, where community-owned wind turbines have paid for the renovation of the community centre there. But it took 10 years to secure planning permission, and we need to make it much easier for our communities to be able to own community energy. So, Prif Weinidog, what will you and the Government do to make sure that we accelerate this, so that, actually, communities can own that community energy and ensure that they see the benefits and we stop the world being on fire? Diolch yn fawr iawn.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Jane Dodds', '', '4983', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=297&l=en', NULL, 121.0, 'High', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; planning permission', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:24.869229', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768985', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Natural Resources Wales have recognised this as well, and they support the announcement that we''ve made today. So, what we''re doing is moving the closed period forward a few weeks to allow farmers to be able to manage that slurry more effectively. It''ll still happen within clear parameters around how much you can apply at what intervals and under what conditions. It''s not some sort of free-for-all that some may wish to portray it as. So, it will still be done in a controlled manner, but it just gives the sector a bit of breathing space in order to mitigate the heightened risk that we''ve seen from having those slurry stores too full going into closed periods. So, it''s quite a pragmatic step, recognising that there is always a balance to be struck. And, you know, on this occasion, I think it is the right decision.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6919&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.244391', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772063', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Now, Kiera Marshall is right, there is a shortage of social housing in this country, but Reform would build tens of thousands more. But those homes should go to British—[ Interruption. ] No, thank you. Those homes should go to British people. There is no point in building thousands of new homes when net immigration is bringing in hundreds of thousands of people every year to fill them.

 The British state and the Welsh state do not exist to provide social housing to the rest of the world, they exist to serve the interests of the British and Welsh people, and putting the people of Britain and Wales first necessarily means putting the rest of the world second. It isn''t racist or xenophobic or unkind to regard your countrymen as your priority. Those closest to you get preferential treatment. And as a Welsh politician representing Wales, that is right, that is just and that is normal. [ Interruption. ] No, thank you. [ Interruption. ] [ Laughter. ]

 I''m sure that was a very funny joke. Thank you. If you ask me to choose between my countrymen, the people of Wales, who are my first and only priority, and strangers, every time, I will proudly choose my countrymen. The people of Wales deserve to be a priority in their own country. They are a unique and special people with a shared history that goes back thousands of years. If, like some of my colleagues, you want to move here and join us, you are more than welcome, but you cannot require the taxpayer to fund your lifestyle.

 I cannot even fathom—. I cannot even fathom the entitlement required to look at a country with failing—[ Interruption. ] No. I cannot even fathom the entitlement required to look at a country with failing public services, patients dying in hospital corridors, and think yourself entitled to subsidised housing when you aren''t even from there. If I moved to China or Eritrea or Bangladesh and told them to house me, they would flatly tell me ''no''. They are right to do so, and we should do the same. I do not accept for a moment that it''s the moral responsibility of the poorest nation in the UK to give up its social housing for people that aren''t even from the UK.

 Once foreign nationals have been banned from social housing, and those who cannot or will not find private accommodation are deported, the allocation system should be overhauled. It should prioritise British workers under the age of 35, young married couples, veterans, victims of domestic violence and care leavers. Being in work should also be a requirement to access any newly built social housing. These measures would go some way to reflect the fact that we live in a world of finite resources and that our own come first. So, I''ll be voting in favour of the motion.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Joe Martin', '', '12146', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=15443&l=en', NULL, 82.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Private rented sector', '', '', 'HMOs; new homes; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.345090', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776929', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think from the children''s commissioner''s perspective as well, the learner seems to not have a voice in the process. It''s easier for parents to access the complaints process, but sometimes it''s more difficult for learners. And of course then, where complaints are delayed and go on in a protracted way, that can have a detrimental impact on education and the provision of education.

 I think generally the thing that came out loud and clear as well is currently there is no clear guidance and support and training for people who are handling these complaints in schools. One of the roles, as you know, that we undertake is the complaint standards work we do, where we try and provide general good guidance on complaint handling and support. So, those were the themes that came out.

 We also looked at the committee''s recommendation where the committee asked us to look at assessing what the impact would be on us as an office if we were to have a role on these complaints. We looked across at the work that is undertaken by the Northern Ireland ombudsman and also the Scottish ombudsman, who currently have a role to look at school complaints. We also looked at a pilot that occurred in England that was undertaken by the Local Government and Social Care Ombudsman. That''s quite a dated pilot, it happened in 2010, but actually the report and learning that came out of that report is helpful.

 It''s no surprise, in a sense, that both Scotland and Northern Ireland, in their casework, really try and resolve these complaints at an early stage, nip it in the bud and try and find resolutions. The type of issues that they''re looking at are complaints about bullying behaviour not being responded to and resolved, special educational needs, as it is in those jurisdictions. We have a certain element on that.

 We looked at the volume of casework that they are currently undertaking. Between Northern Ireland and Scotland, between 4 per cent and 7 per cent of their overall case load is made up of complaints about schools, so it is quite a substantial area of their work. Our jurisdiction mirrors, apart from in relation to schools, those two bodies. The last part of our report really tries to provide some very tentative assessment of what would the likely volume of complaints look like if we were to have an extension to the ombudsman''s remit in this area. Tentatively, we have outlined that we feel between about 200 and 300 complaints a year may result in coming to the office. But, looking at what others do, clearly, we would have to gear up and make sure our service is very responsive so that we could provide those early resolutions on cases.

 Also, alongside our casework and investigative resource, we would need to make sure there''s support there for schools, governing bodies and headteachers to equip them to deal with these complaints. One of the interesting things that came out of discussions with Northern Ireland in particular—they have the widest remit on this work—was actually sometimes having that external body there to assess complaints and give a final adjudication is quite helpful for the schools as well, because it takes the matter away from the schools when things are becoming protracted.

 We''ve made and suggested a couple of recommendations in the modelling report for you. If you would like us to continue to explore this work, we''ve suggested that we continue to work with the Welsh Government and to support its review of the governing body effectiveness work. We''ve suggested that we do a more detailed assessment of the financial implications if you were to consider that our remit should be extended. And also, we''re asking you to consider what legislative changes would be needed to bring schools within our remit. The previous committee led and introduced the 2019 Act and the same provisions are in there that allow the Senedd to extend the bodies in our jurisdiction, but also to take away some of the restrictions that are there. The key one is that, in relation to any complaints that we receive about local authorities or any other matter relating to education, currently the provision is that we cannot look at anything that is related to conduct, curriculum matters, management or discipline in schools. That''s the key part of the current Act that would need to be considered.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Katrin Shaw', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=8622&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.962245', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777710', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 They complement each other, of course, in terms of the work, both the supplementary and the annual budget, but they are two elements of work that I am progressing. The annual budget is the one that lands next year, but, in advance of that, I need to be finalising the work on this financial year, and therein lies the importance of supplementary budgets, both to begin that work, as the budget that wasn''t passed by the Senedd, but at some point also to consolidate the end of the financial year with a second supplementary budget. That has been the usual process, and will need to be the usual process, of finalising allocations retrospectively, almost, for the financial year.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=9967&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; programme for government; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:52.076488', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774614', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The Cabinet Minister''s response to this petition explains the existing legal framework, placing an animal welfare duty on owners and setting out the enforcement powers of local authorities. It also notes the Welsh Government''s code of practice for the welfare of equines, which sets out standards for care and is enforceable by local authorities. The response notes that

 ''Tethering is not illegal, but is not suitable for long-term management and our Code of Practice outlines strict conditions for tethering, including access to water, food, shelter and regular supervision. We encourage alternative management methods and have no current plans to review the welfare legislation in relation to tethering.''

 The letter also points to specific guidance for local authorities, ''Guide to the Control of Horses (Wales) Act 2014''. The Minister says that members of the public are encouraged to report

 ''any welfare concerns relating to tethered horses directly to their Local Authority. If there''s an immediate risk to public safety or concerns about disorder, the police should be contacted via 101.''

 The British Horse Society also has online guidance for the public about reporting fly-grazing to the relevant local authority to take action. The petitioner''s response sets out the welfare concerns about fly-grazing animals in Cardiff. They say that, as there is no intention to change existing arrangements relating to tethering, it''s important that existing legal powers and code of practice are acted on by the local authorities.

 Another campaigner on this issue has separately provided Members with a Friends of Swansea Horses report from 2015, outlining earlier research on the welfare of tethered horses there, and associated costs to the horses, the public and local authorities. And we''ve had that report shared separately with us.

 Our possible actions here would be that, if we agree that stronger enforcement is needed, then we can write to the Cabinet Minister again to highlight the petitioner''s concerns and ask what more can be done to encourage and enable local authorities to use their powers, including an update on progress with improving the training and skills of local authority enforcement officers and working with enforcement agencies. Either in addition to or instead of option 1, we could write directly to the Welsh Local Government Association to ask what is being done to co-ordinate the enforcement approach across local authorities. Then, if we are content with the Welsh Government response, we can thank the petitioner for raising the issue, set out the committee''s views and close the petition.

 I think it''s important to note that there have been over 13,000 signatures from within Wales, and we should decide whether a request should be made to the Business Committee for a Plenary debate. But there are also others that we may need to consider. As I was reading through this, my preferred option would be for us to write directly to the local government association and ask what is being done to co-ordinate enforcement. But I''m happy to take any other options from Members.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16259', '2026-09-24', 'Sara Crowley', '', '12150', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. New Petitions', '16259', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16259?startPos=93&l=en', NULL, 40.2, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'local_government_enforcement', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies', '', 'enforcement powers; local authority enforcement', 0, '2026-09-25T12:02:30.944221', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16259&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772178', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Therefore, the context of the budget is challenging, and therefore the combination of that with the new Plaid Cymru Government in Government for the first time leading this first budget, and introducing our programme for government, which needs to be reflected in the fiscal priorities—all of those things come together, leading to a process that, internally within the Government, is going on at the moment. I am leading that budget process in co-operation with the Cabinet and setting out the challenging financial context for the Ministers, and I am expecting them then to be making the prioritisation that’s needed to balance the need that needs to be met and introducing effective public services, and also putting forward, through this budget, a way of realising the programme for government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=6369&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; programme for government', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.507480', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774329', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But I would just like to raise governance and accountability for this Bill. Do you believe that this Bill provides sufficient clarity about which organisations are responsible and accountable for cross-border services?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16298', '2026-10-01', 'Matthew Jones', '', '12151', '', '', 'Health and Social Care Committee', '2. Legislative Consent: Health Bill - evidence session 1: NHS Wales Joint Commissioning Committee', '16298', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16298?startPos=2199&l=en', NULL, 51.7, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'LCM; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-05T15:05:44.197604', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16298&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774551', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
