 We can see that it has reached and exceeded that 10,000 threshold. We know that ALN funding is regularly raised in the Chamber. I think that shows how important a topic it is to people across Wales, with the huge increase that we''re seeing in young people with additional learning needs, and the need to support them appropriately for the duration of their time in education. Here in this petition they''re saying that the system is at breaking point. Without immediate earmarked protected funding specifically targeted at front-line school support, they say critical resources risk being even further diluted across wider central budgets while classrooms remain severely underresourced. They''re calling for cross-party co-operation to break that deadlock.

 There are several possible actions here for us. If there''s anything specific we wanted to raise, we could raise that with the Cabinet Minister for education. We could highlight the petitioner''s additional comments and questions with the Minister, and we could copy in the Early Years, Children, Young People and Education Committee, so that that committee can take that into account in its work planning as well. That''s something I think we could do as a course of action. 

 But also, I think the most important decision for us today is whether we wish to request a Plenary debate on the petition. We know that this is a topic that is very much in the news and does not look as though it''s going to go away anytime soon because of the number of young people and families and teachers and support workers affected. So, I would like to recommend that we do request a Plenary debate on this petition.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16259', '2026-09-24', 'Vikki Howells', '', '5107', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. New Petitions', '16259', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16259?startPos=747&l=en', NULL, 17.2, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-25T12:02:31.027041', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16259&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772180', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Money is sometimes allocated late in the year, sometimes very late in the year. Money has been claimed back. There is an expectation it could have been carried forward, but that''s only an expectation. The rules are really unfair. If not always able to carry it forward, money was claimed back. I did suggest at the time that we should not rely on the Treasury being fair—I don''t think anybody''s ever thought the Treasury was fair—and that we should actually take the money and spend it. We could give it to local authorities or give it to housing associations without any problem at all. But this is not a way to run a national budget. It does not cost the Treasury any money. Once it''s here, it''s here, and I don''t see why they want to get involved in micromanaging Wales. It has to end. And once the money is allocated, then why does the Treasury want to get involved? It is not their money, they should be leaving us alone.

 On funding, despite what has been said and believed, Wales does well out of the current system. We receive substantially more than we pay in. Estimates suggest that we collect about 70 per cent to 75 per cent of the per capita income in Wales. We spend about 110 per cent, including central Government expenditure, here in Wales. This is what being part of a larger country means—money is provided to those who need it most.

 What should a funding system look like? Characteristics of a funding system for subnational Governments that were originally proposed by the Calman commission include: stability and predictability; simplicity and transparency; autonomy; efficiency; equity, so that resources are allocated in a way that takes account of relative need; and accountability, so that the link between decisions made and the tax paid by voters is clear. Any additional tax take within a region should impact on the budget available.

 The Barnett formula evolved from the Goschen formula, which was designed specifically to preserve Scotland''s public expenditure advantage over England. The formula was thus never designed with Wales in mind. Applied rigorously, the formula should have led to convergence in levels of spending per head across the UK. The formula has, however, preserved the spending advantage for Wales over England. There have been periodic adjustments to the operation of the formula since 1980, mainly through relative population changes. These adjustments have increased relative spending levels in Wales. The main advantage of the formula is its simplicity and objectivity as a basis for making spending allocations. There has been notable success in securing formula bypass since devolution. For example, Objective 1 match funding and funding for city deals. Northern Ireland has done substantially better in receiving money in excess of the Barnett formula. Remember, the Barnett formula calculates the minimum to be provided. Anything over and above that is allowed to be provided by the Government if they so desire.

 We also have the Barnett floor. It was set following the Holtham commission investigation into the needs of Wales. At the 2015 spending review, the UK Conservative Government—see, Sam, I do mention the Conservatives—implemented a funding floor in Wales. This provided a guarantee that the Welsh Government block grant funding per head would not fall below 115 per cent of equivalent spending per head in England. Currently in Wales we get about 120 per cent of the equivalent funding per head in England. With the result of the Holtham review that Wales needs 115 per cent of the equivalent funding per head in England in devolved areas, if we get 120 per cent, we''re doing well.

 Currently, the total budget is £27.5 billion—£22.8 billion in revenue and £3.6 billion in capital. So, if Wales gets its 115 per cent, which is what Holtham said, then we get about £1 billion more than we should be getting, or than would be calculated. So, this idea that Wales is treated very badly by everybody might play well in certain circles, but it doesn''t actually play with reality. This needs recalculating. I suggest academics, jointly appointed by the Welsh Government and the Treasury, at five-yearly intervals, should recalculate Wales''s need. But remember, there''s a danger that Wales will get less.

 I support additional funding for rail. I have stations in my constituency that we need to be opening and reopening. Would money better be spent on transport in general? We need work doing on bus and rail interchanges. We need to ensure adequate parking near the new stations. What we do not need are rail lines to fulfil the wishes of the few passengers who want to use them.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16313', '2026-10-06', 'Mike Hedges', '', '332', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Debate: Fiscal Reform', '16313', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16313?startPos=15943&l=en', NULL, 44.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', '', 'housing associations', 0, '2026-10-09T13:35:11.007202', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16313&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776247', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The landscape has changed fundamentally, I would say, since that scheme was developed and launched back in 2021. As I said, there have been great changes in terms of the cost-of-living crisis, there have been changes in terms of the broader economic pressures and, of course, this great increase and the volatility we''ve seen in the energy markets. So, I do think that the challenges that face households now are somewhat different to the ones that existed back when the original plan was launched back in 2021. So my officials are currently assessing what that means in terms of the plan, in terms of the targets within it, and in terms of those interim targets. So, that''s our position at present with that scheme.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=1362&l=en', NULL, 115.0, 'High', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Property & energy', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee; fuel poverty', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.709907', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771225', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Cabinet Minister, does the Welsh Government accept that planning delays, bureaucracy and developer contribution requirements can act as a barrier to delivering the homes Wales needs? In England, the Conservatives want to replace section 106 agreements as they are a real barrier for developers. Will you review Wales''s approach and, in a similar way, bring forward changes where planning requirements are preventing or delaying the delivery of much-needed affordable and social housing?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=6559&l=en', NULL, 159.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'housing_supply; planning_system; homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Section 106; homelessness; housing delivery; new homes; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.603412', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776738', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The important thing is we''ve committed to improve these situations. So, wherever we are on a comparative basis, we know—because I have the same discussions with businesses—that this does arise in every conversation. So, we have to commit to doing much better. And that doesn''t mean that we agree with those who argue that we should just deregulate everything. There is a reason why we have a planning system, and our constituents wouldn''t thank us if we just got rid of that completely. There is a balance to be struck in any context, isn''t there, between different interests and different drivers. But I think that what we''re trying to focus on is can we meet those valid requirements in terms of ensuring that community interests or individual interests are protected through a planning system, for example, but do so in a way that is more rapid and is less bureaucratic and complex, still delivering the same level of assurance and transparency that is needed, but meaning that there is certainty.

 What companies often tell me—. That is, getting the answer ''no'' isn''t such a problem. Getting a hard ''no'' quickly is worth a lot. The problem is that things just take so long, don''t they, and then you don''t have any certainty. If you get a ''no'' quicker, that frees you then to move on and refocus, and I think there is a lot of merit in that. So, part of the work, as I said earlier, is working with the private sector, with businesses, to say, ''Well, can we simplify the processes in a way that doesn''t get rid of the outcomes that are important, but means that we can do things much more effectively than we are at present?''', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16245', '2026-09-30', 'Adam Price', '', '5030', '', '', 'Economy, Energy and Connectivity Committee', '2. General Ministerial Scrutiny: Enterprise, Connectivity and Energy', '16245', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16245?startPos=2410&l=en', NULL, 28.7, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', '', '', 'planning system', 0, '2026-10-02T06:30:29.849886', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16245&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774088', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Cabinet Minister, sadly, it''s not just unpaid carers who have been seemingly kicked to the kerb by your Government. The programme for government quite rightly makes specific commitments relating to cardiovascular disease, strokes, mental health and diabetes, yet it does not say anything specifically about lung disease, despite it being the third biggest killer here in Wales. Not only that, but Wales has the highest lung disease mortality rate in western Europe. Given the scale of lung disease in Wales, why has it been omitted from the Government''s programme? And what message does this send to the hundreds of thousands of people living with lung conditions who continue to experience some of the worst outcomes anywhere in western Europe? Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Natasha Asghar', '', '336', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=3568&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.353519', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776651', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Council tax is set, of course, by local authorities and it''s important that it is. I would remind the Member that, in a recent budget deal before we came into power, we made sure that there was a baseline of increase in funding for councils, and a big part of that was to protect council tax payers, which I agree is something that''s very, very important indeed.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=1500&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'council tax', 0, '2026-09-23T11:54:34.406443', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770717', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

Forward work programme – Legislation Committee Forward work programme – Legislation Committee Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings The forward work programme of the Legislation Committee sets out the work which the Committee is intending to carry out in the following weeks and months. The Legislation Committee''s forward work programme is often determined by its responsibilities set out in Standing Orders 21 and 21C. On a routine basis, the Committee considers subordinate legislation and legislative consent memoranda which are laid before the Senedd. For further details, please see the ''Current Work'' information on the Committee''s homepage. Any documents published indicate the current intentions of the Committee at the time of publishing, however the programme may change at any time to respond to external events. Business type: Other First published: 11/06/2026 Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47572', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 140.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Legislative commitment; Bill progress', 'Legislation Committee; laid before the Senedd; subordinate legislation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:50.494140', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47572', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I have to say, of course, that planning departments—. Never mind the process itself, the planning departments are under significant pressure in our local authorities. One of the outcomes of years of cuts in public services is cuts to planning services and services of those kinds in order to protect education and social services and so on. So, I have great sympathy with the decline in our planning departments on the ground, and the Royal Town Planning Institute has drawn attention to this in a recent report and is very concerned that we don''t have an adequate supply of planners coming through the system, even if we were to change the process, in order to deal with the level of work.

 Now, we as a Government are trying to do something to address that issue, and we''ve supported the work of training new qualified planners across Wales and have created a new bursary scheme jointly with Ceredigion County Council in order to create up to an additional 12 qualified planners in rural areas, where the recruitment challenges are even greater.

 So, I agree with some of what you said; I would point out, of course, that there are problems within the system as it''s constituted at the moment, but there''s now a wonderful opportunity for you to submit your ideas into the consultation—and not just the consultation, write to me, each and every one of you, with your practical ideas as to how we can get developments to move forward more quickly than they do at the moment.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=9904&l=en', NULL, 104.5, 'High', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; local development plans', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:59.193543', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773008', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 ''end access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals''.

 I just wanted to pick up on a point that I would have said earlier on. I believe you''re Canadian originally, so—. Well, sorry, I''m not sure. Or there are other members of your group who weren''t necessarily from here. [ Interruption .] But this motion—. Originally, originally. But this motion would say—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Matthew Jones', '', '12151', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=16151&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', '', '', 'HMOs; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.414283', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776968', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Much can be said about the barriers facing young people, from low pay and insecure work to a lack of progression opportunities, but I want to focus on the opportunities being created by our climate commitments, and the good-quality jobs that can come with them. As Greens, we see meeting our climate commitments not as a burden, but as a huge opportunity. It can create thousands of long-term skilled jobs. Crucially, we need careers that equip young people with skills for life. We should not be directing people into industries with an expiry date attached to them. Instead, we should be investing in sectors that we know will continue to grow and provide stable employment for decades to come.

 Some of those careers will be in the trades. We need to deliver renewable energy projects, retrofit our existing housing stock and build the sustainable homes and infrastructure of the future. We need more engineers, more skilled construction workers and more investment in research and development if Wales is to become that global leader.

 I''d like to highlight the potential of marine and tidal energy. Wales is blessed with extraordinary coastlines and the second largest tidal range in the world. By harnessing that natural advantage, we can create skilled careers by strengthening our energy security. If Wales isn''t ready for this transition, it''s our young people who will, once again, miss the boat. We need to be able to equip them to move into these good long-term careers, otherwise the opportunities will simply go elsewhere.

 The Warm Homes plan is another good example of where environmental and economic goals come together. Retrofitting homes and building fossil-fuel-free homes from the outset will cut emissions, lower energy bills and create skilled employment in construction and engineering. These are jobs rooted in communities, and jobs that we will need if we are serious about meeting our climate commitments. That''s why I''m so pleased to support this motion, and I urge the Welsh Government to consider and ensure that the pathways into green industries are ambitious, accessible and available to young people across Wales. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Paul Rock', '', '12176', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=9326&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency', 'Property & energy', 'LGHP Committee members', '', 'Warm Homes; retrofit; retrofitting', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.605230', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768277', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In the meantime, it''s crucial that the record levels of investment that Dŵr Cymru and Hafren Dyfrdwy have had in PR24 are used to replace water mains, for the reasons that you outlined, so they can raise their day-to-day performance for customers. In terms of resilience, that will help, as will fixing leaks across both England and Wales. Around a fifth of the water that goes into the distribution system is lost through leaks. The majority of that is what''s called customer-side leaks and not company-side leaks, but it''s lost one way or another. That''s billions of litres a day. Companies need to do more with the record levels of investment that they''ve been allowed to charge their customers to improve leakage, and we are holding them to stretching targets to do that.

 I think the answer is a combination of the short-term measures that are coming out of the price review, that long-term portfolio of projects, and the third limb is changing our relationship with water as people who use it and as people who drink it. That''s why, in the summer, you will have seen that we launched a water efficiency campaign called Let''s Save Water. The purpose of that is not only to remind people about simple ways of saving water—which we all know, but it''s helpful to remind people—but to educate people about the scale of the challenge that we face. For example, what we found through monitoring of the reach of that campaign—which has gone really well; it''s exceeded its key performance indicators, and I''m really pleased about that, but especially social media reach—is that the message that we use almost five times as much water individually as we think we do is really starting to hit home.

 The evidence suggests that when we ask members of the public, when we ask ourselves, ''How much water do you use?'' people think it''s about 30 litres a day, which is three buckets of water; the reality is closer to 140 litres. We''re using almost five times more water than we think we are. We have to reset our relationship with water, because there is a relationship between the water that comes out of our taps and the water in our natural environment. Environmental improvement will happen only if we change our relationship with water. I think those are the three ways that we''re addressing the immediate and the longer term resilience challenges.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=1501&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:21.922624', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773140', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Can I thank you, First Minister, for advance notice of your statement, although not quite as in advance as the Western Mail ''s notice of your statement today, which seemed to have more detail than any Member of this Senedd prior to the release of it at 9.34 a.m. this morning? I''m afraid to say, again, a bit of a sense of déjà vu, a lot of recutting and rehashing of your manifesto, recutting and rehashing of your 100-day plan, and there''s only so much rehashing that you can do with the same sorry document so many times to try and polish what many people would describe as something that I won''t refer to. Because that, I''m afraid, is what many people will see it as.

 I have to say that I was listening carefully to the radio this morning, as you found out in First Minister''s questions today, and Laura McAllister referred to your 100-day plan as a ''gimmick''—I agree with her, and it''s not always that I agree with Laura McAllister, but I agree with her on that. And now, of course, the rubber needs to hit the road in terms of getting some delivery for the people of Wales. And it''s not going very well so far, is it? Waiting times up, PISA scores down, your grandstanding on independence really not making much of a difference, no matter how loud you scream. Now, I will say that, look, there is a lot of ambition in this document that we on the Conservative benches share—we want to see a more prosperous Wales, we want to see a better educated Wales, we want to see a healthier Wales; we want to see all of those things—but I''m afraid that it''s lighter on detail than I would like in terms of the availability to deliver on those things.

 Let me just turn to the NHS for a second, because I''m very concerned about the NHS, as are you, and I know that it''s something that is close to your heart. You referenced the climate emergency and the nature emergency; you didn''t seem to reference a crisis in the NHS, which I''m afraid it is, and many people perceive it as such. One thing that we do know is that that crisis is largely caused, according to the clinicians themselves, and according to the royal colleges, including the Royal College of Nursing, which was here last week in the Senedd, as a result of the beds situation in our NHS. There simply aren''t enough beds. The Labour Party was disastrous in terms of its management of the NHS; I agree with you on that. They cut the number of beds too far, too fast, and people now are paying the consequences.

 Just over the last three years, I am told by the Royal College of Nursing, 21 per cent of acute surgical beds have gone in the Welsh NHS, a fifth of our capacity. Thirteen per cent of acute medical beds have gone. It''s no wonder that our emergency departments can''t find the beds in those hospitals when patients need them. You didn''t reference that situation. I want to know what are you going to do to surge those beds in the short term to fix that issue. Because let me tell you: you create surgical hubs, you need beds for people to go into after they''re recovering from surgery, particularly if something goes wrong. So, I want to know what are you doing on that front.

 You also suggested that there''s going to be a switch in the way that the NHS is funded, with more going to primary care. Now, I want more money to go into our primary care services, but I don''t want it to be to the detriment of hospital care. What assurances can you give me today, First Minister, and the people of Wales, that you won''t be cutting funding to our hospitals in Wales in order to compensate for that extra investment in primary care? Because we need to know.

 You referenced the cost of living. I agree with you that we need action on that. That''s why I raised the need to do something on council tax today in First Minister''s questions. We need action to extend free childcare. We had a free childcare plan as well, and we''ll support you to be able to try to deliver some more free childcare. Ours was slightly different; we wanted more parental choice so that grandparents could look after their children too, and that was better for the taxpayer in terms of cost. But I want to know what you''re doing to cut taxes. Can you cut some taxes for us? A penny off income tax. Is it not possible to shave some money off the budget where we''ve got waste, where we''ve got largesse, so that we can put money back into people''s pockets so that they can decide how to spend it in the economy themselves?

 You referenced in your document—. By the way, when the statement came through, I clicked on the well-being link. It didn''t work. I hope that''s not an omen for the future in terms of whether you''ll be able to deliver things. I eventually got around to finding the right link. You talked in there about the desire to allow people to buy their own community assets. I support that; we''ll support you with that. I want people to be able to buy their own home. I want stamp duty gone. I want the right to buy back. I want to bring empty homes back into use. There''s insufficient detail on those things in your plan.

 I''ve got all sorts of other things that I''d like to discuss, but the clock''s against me. Let me say this: if you''re able to deliver on your ambitions, many of them which we support, we will try to work collaboratively with you. It might be difficult. There may be some stumbles along the way, but we''re prepared to reach out on a cross-party basis, and it was good to hear the same message coming from Reform as well, to try to make Wales a better place. But be under no illusions: we want to see a Wales where there are lower taxes, where there''s an end to wasteful spending, where there''s less emphasis on the grandstanding and more emphasis on the day-to-day jobs and using those powers and resources that you already have in your armoury to deliver a better future for our country.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Darren Millar', '', '171', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=10105&l=en', NULL, 110.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system; Tax & finance', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'community assets; council tax; empty homes; programme for government; right to buy; stamp duty', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.928870', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770999', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think that one of the main things is a lessened risk of elector confusion about whether they''re registered to vote or not. So, getting that notification and then the length of the period—. With a shorter period, at least there''s some clarity as to, ''Okay, I''m registered from this point.'' That''s a particularly important factor around any by-elections that might take place. So, if a by-election were to end up taking place during a notification period in the local authority area, it wouldn''t be ideal just because the ERO would then need to contact anyone who is in that period and would essentially have to ask them to go ahead and complete a normal application to register instead and to do so before the deadline for that by-election. So, from our perspective, any notification period that is longer than it needs to be increases the risk of that by-election falling within the notification period. And just to say as well, the feedback we received from the local authorities as part of the pilot evaluation suggests that, while the 60-day period in and of itself didn''t present any significant issues, most of the responses that were received from people who chose to opt out tended to be within the first two to three weeks of the 60-day period. So, that''s some of the rationale behind our recommendation for it to be shortened.

 I think it''s also become fairly apparent since our evaluation last year in some of the discussions with Welsh Government and stakeholders at the working group that, just in terms of some of the implementation timescales that Tom Hawthorn was just talking about and getting this rolled out in time for 2027, a longer notification period than the 28 days that has been opted for by the Welsh Government makes an already challenging timescale even more challenging. So, whilst I think it could be something that could get looked at for the business-as-usual AVR process, there is a point there around specific year 1 implementation for 2027, and just with the timescales we''re working up against, going much longer than 28 days would be quite difficult. And, obviously, there are risks there as well, which I''m sure we''ll get into, but—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Davies', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=687&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; working group', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.790026', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769839', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 There''s a specific question on Flying Start, and I have heard Labour Members raise questions about the future of Flying Start. We''re somehow being accused of wanting to take money away from fighting child poverty when this is the party that has been criticised by his too often for wanting to spend too much on tackling child poverty through the childcare offer. This phase of the childcare offer is literally being delivered through Flying Start. This is building on what Flying Start has done. More families living in poverty, incidentally, are not in Flying Start areas than the ones that are in Flying Start areas. Both are really, really important. We want to help them all. I hope that''s clear enough.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=11192&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.102251', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771014', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 When it comes to the retention of the improvements, what can be reused, for example, I''m sure will be. But, Ed, could you comment a little bit about those tensions, I suppose, or how we manage that, because, obviously, with a decant strategy of moving out and work happening, we''ll need to be in here until at least next summer, as well? So, there is a period of time where, that work, we will benefit from. Ed.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=2823&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.358017', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777489', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 It comes at a time when schools are already dealing with increasingly complex pupil needs, concerns regarding student behaviour, teachers'' workload, and challenges in recruiting and retaining staff. Bearing in mind the plan''s importance and our aspirations for education, what further support will the Welsh Government be providing to teachers and to staff more broadly during this Senedd term in order to ensure that they receive the appropriate support to deliver this plan?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Sera Evans', '', '330', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=10752&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.029627', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771013', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
