 So, first, bring back section 35, legislate to give every Welsh council the power to set qualification rules for its register, and use it to close the register to foreign nationals. Second, amend the allocation of housing and homelessness regulations. Welsh Ministers decide who is eligible. Strip it back to the absolute minimum devolved legislation allows. Third, rewrite the code of guidance and the preference framework so that British citizens with a connection to their communities come first every time. Fourth, houses in multiple occupation. Councils license these properties—wait two ticks, Ken—and Welsh Ministers set the conditions attached to those licences. Every licence holder should be required to verify and record the nationality and immigration status of every occupant, and the use of HMOs across Wales as asylum accommodation must end. Yes, go on.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Francesca O''Brien', '', '12190', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=14725&l=en', NULL, 110.0, 'High', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', 'Opposition housing leads', '', 'HMOs; homelessness; houses in multiple occupation; multiple occupation; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.269897', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776935', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Cabinet Minister, more than 200 residents have signed a petition calling for the site in Rogerstone North to be ruled out. I''ve written to the council myself highlighting the residents'' concerns and calling for the site to be dropped, and the decision now rests with the local authority. So, will you join me in putting pressure on Newport council to protect this community green space by removing it from the authority''s allotment plans?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Natasha Asghar', '', '336', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=3050&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', '', '', 'community asset', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:48.075315', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773553', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 You mentioned tackling misinformation and I think that''s absolutely crucial. We''ve touched on social media, haven''t we? But there''s so much around trying to get in quite early around that misinformation that can easily get around and really cause some community cohesion issues.

 I know that, on a different scale, the UK Government announced today a new agency that''s going to be established. And I''m just wondering, this isn''t just—. Obviously, this will be a cross-Government responsibility about how we tackle misinformation. I''m just wondering what more we can do, what you''re doing in that space as well, and how you can bring people together to make sure we''re tackling misinformation at source as effectively as possible.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Jayne Bryant', '', '5038', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6134&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.199882', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771260', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Wales has a proud industrial history. It has brought jobs and prosperity to communities across the country and made an enormous contribution to the development of modern Britain. But that history has also left us with problems, which, many decades later, we are still having to deal with. Old mines, quarries, chemical works and industrial sites simply did not operate to the environmental standards that we would expect today. And for some of the people I represent, that isn''t just history. Close to the north-eastern border of my constituency, as mentioned by Elwyn, near Rhos, Cefn Mawr, Acrefair and Ruabon, is the former Monsanto chemical waste tip. This is not rumour or local folklore. Parliamentary records confirm that Monsanto chemicals were deposited there in 1964 and 1965—prior to my being born, just.

 More than 60 years later, people are still asking questions about that site and the neighbouring acid tar lagoon. Wrexham council has investigated the sites extensively. Its position is that contaminants are present, but the sites do not meet the legal definition of contaminated land. It does, however, continue to inspect and monitor them. But I can understand why people living nearby remain concerned. If you live close to somewhere like this, you want straightforward answers. What is in the ground? Is it getting out? Is there any risk to my family or my home, and if there is a problem, who is going to do something about it? These seem perfectly reasonable questions to me, and 60 years is an awfully long time to be asking them.

 There have been calls for people living near contaminated sites to be offered blood tests for PCBs. Public Health Wales has considered that, and its advice is that blood testing is not recommended. It cannot reliably tell somebody how or where they were exposed, or what the consequences for their health might be. We should listen to that advice. There''s little point in offering people a test that may leave them with more questions rather than fewer.

 But that makes investigating the land itself all the more important. We need to establish what is actually there, whether contaminants are escaping and whether they present a genuine risk. This is where councils need help. They have responsibilities for contaminated land, but investigating sites like these is complicated and expensive. I welcome the fact that capital funding has now been agreed, but Wrexham County Borough Council is already making the point that additional funding will be needed if councils are going to investigate potentially contaminated sites properly. I think we should listen to them. There is little use giving local authorities responsibility for dealing with these sites if we don''t also give them the means to do the job.

 Then, of course, comes, as Niamh has mentioned, the difficult question of who pays if something needs putting right. Where the original polluter can be identified and liability established, every reasonable effort should be made to recover the costs from them. But when something happened 50, 60 or even 100 years ago, that isn''t always possible. Companies disappear, businesses change hands and records are lost. We cannot simply shrug our shoulders. The fact that pollution is old doesn''t make it any less real. And it isn''t reasonable to leave one council and its council tax payers facing an enormous bill simply because the original polluter can no longer be pursued. There is a balance to be struck here.

 I don''t want to frighten people unnecessarily. Public Health Wales makes clear that PCBs can come from a number of sources and that finding elevated levels in a particular place does not necessarily establish where someone has been exposed. What I do want is for people to have confidence that somebody is actually finding out. If a site is safe, tell them, give them reassurance. If there''s a problem, tell them and then do something about it.

 The people living near the former Monsanto tip have lived with questions about that site for long enough. There will be other communities across Wales in very much the same position. They deserve answers, they deserve a system capable of establishing the facts, and where action is needed, they deserve to know that the money and the determination are there to see it through. As their representatives, the least we can do is to make sure their questions are heard and keep asking them until they get a proper answer. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Andrew Griffin', '', '12187', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Contaminated land', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=8907&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'council tax', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:48.813209', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773728', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
