Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28, start date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 00:00:00 GMT, end date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 23:59:00 GMT

Closing date: 25 September 2026.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgConsultationDisplay.aspx?ID=632', '2026-07-31', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd committee', '', '', '', '2026-09-25', 161.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'NRLA named', 'Consultation open', 'closing date; consultation; draft budget', 1, '2026-08-24T07:35:33.145471', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgRss.aspx?f=76', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 It is important, as the new LDP is put together, that lessons from that previous experience are learned, building on what did work and looking specifically at what didn''t work so that the new plan does gain more support and confidence in the communities and provides a firm foundation for the future of Wrexham.

 Given that I have powers within the LDP process, I can''t take a direct role in the process of developing the new plan, but I am content to provide practical support for that process, and I would be very happy to meet with you to hear about how you think the community engagement should work. The Design Commission for Wales can also be of assistance, and I will be meeting them next week, and so there will probably be ideas there that we can explore.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=7017&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'LDP', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.643652', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776725', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Work is ongoing across Government on carbon budget 3. Obviously, the deadline is the end of this year. The UK Climate Change Committee has made it clear that much of the heavy lifting depends on that action from the UK Government, predominantly because industry and energy in the main are non-devolved. So, action in those spaces is really key if we are to meet the carbon budget 3. But we''re doing everything we can within those areas of responsibility that we have, predominantly, of course, around transport and housing, which are the two main sectors that can contribute to this. And, as I say, that work is ongoing and we will be making an announcement later on this year to outline those actions specifically.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4325&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.852429', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771795', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 It touches on an issue that the previous committee had considered with a few different landfill sites, but this one in particular has been ongoing for a number of years. Our predecessor committee felt that there was still a lot to be done on this particular petition, even though they''d had a response from the former Deputy First Minister, whose portfolio this fell into. Our predecessor committee agreed to refer it on to us, with a view that we might perhaps like to highlight it with the relevant policy committee and pursue the appropriate Minister with responsibility for landfill operations on all these issues around forever chemicals, water quality and odours.

 So, I think there''s one course of action that I would like to suggest for us, which is to take it up, first of all, with the new Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee. We could ask them to pursue the matters raised by the petition with the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability, and I think it would be appropriate for us to do that because I know that committee is looking to bring that Minister in for general scrutiny as soon as possible. That committee is also looking at its forward work plans, so all of these issues around landfill, water pollution and the air quality issues could be something that the committee might wish to consider more broadly, as well as taking up directly with the Minister.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16175', '2026-07-09', 'Vikki Howells', '', '5107', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. Updates - Petitions referred to the Seventh Senedd', '16175', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16175?startPos=801&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:13.263366', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16175&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767266', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I made a written statement on Monday that confirmed the 20,000 social homes that are desperately needed in Wales, for everyone living in Wales. The way that social housing is allocated is a matter for local authorities. Local authorities have a housing register and homes are allocated according to need, and that''s how it should be too. Therefore, I look forward to the work now of supplying more social homes. That''s what''s at the heart of the problem; the core of the housing crisis in Wales is that we don''t have enough social homes. The supply isn''t adequate to meet demand, and that is where we should be focusing our efforts always.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4017&l=en', NULL, 93.5, 'High', 'digest', 'housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Sector bodies; Housing minister', '', 'social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.069546', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768148', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Now, in October 2025, Ynys Môn county council''s planning and orders committee approved 22 of 25 exception proposals to return sections of road from 20 mph to 30 mph. And as Helen Jenner has quite rightly mentioned, the cost of this makes this institution, actually, and those in Government at that time, a laughing stock. Only it would be, if it wasn''t so serious.

 Now, I support the motion calling on the Welsh Government to scrap Labour and Plaid Cymru''s default 20 mph speed limit. Let me be clear, this is not an argument against 20 mph limits outside schools, near playgrounds, around hospitals or on highly populated residential streets, where evidence does show a genuine safety need. It is an argument against a blunt, one-size-fits-all default that treats very different roads as though they were the same. The Welsh Conservatives are clear: restore the 30 mph default while retaining 20 mph limits where they protect pedestrians and vulnerable road users.

 Now, Labour and Plaid Cymru, you chose the opposite approach, and, to this day, your co-operation agreement has left the legacy of this Senedd and, indeed, Wales with some terrible decisions made during that co-operation agreement, this being one of them. They imposed the default first, leaving councils and communities to work backwards, road by road, and traffic regulation order by traffic regulation order. And do you know what? It actually cost the Minister at the time their job.

 Public opposition could not have been clearer. A record nearly 500,000 constituents out of a just over 3 million population said ''no'', but they were ignored. Public opposition should be listened to. These people were not opposed to road safety. They simply wanted common sense, local knowledge and the right speed limit on the right road. Lee Waters, the Minister responsible, told the Senedd that the Buckley pilot showed that an area-wide approach does not work. He also said that piloting involves failing and learning from the failure. So, if the pilot showed an area-wide approach had not worked, why was a nationwide default imposed before those lessons were reflected in the roll-out? The public were assured that the policy had been carefully designed, yet, in 2024, the Welsh Government—oh, Ken''s gone now—revised its guidance, allowing councils to identify roads. And, Helen, we''ve still got roads in Conwy, actually, that haven''t gone back to 30 mph. They just haven''t put the signs up yet. The consequences are visible in my constituency.

 Now, this is Government in reverse. Ministers imposed the default nationally, rejected an unprecedented public demand for change, and expected councils to sort out the consequences locally. The costs are substantial. I get in a taxi now when I get here from Bangor Conwy Môn, and I ask the taxi driver, ''How does the 20 mph affect you?'' And they hate it. They say it actually hits their pockets. And taxi driving isn''t the easiest job in the world.

 I''m going to finish now. Look, we didn''t put any amendments forward. We wholeheartedly support this debate. And if you had listened to your residents during election time, and even now since, you would all be supporting this 20 mph.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Janet Finch-Saunders', '', '212', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Reform UK Debate: Default 20 mph speed limit', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=13012&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'co-operation agreement', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:49.292706', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773777', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In terms of the grant itself, I''m pleased that you''ve welcomed that, because this has been a concern for many leaseholders, that the costs of putting the temporary alarm systems in place in these buildings while the work is ongoing—. In some cases, leaseholders have had to pay, and that isn''t fair. That isn''t fair. There are some developers who have paid for it, but there are other scenarios where that hasn''t been the case. The grant will be available, and the application process will open very soon for that, and the agent or the responsible body will have that responsibility to make that application, not the developer. It''ll be people acting on behalf of the leaseholders who will be able to make that particular application.

 In terms of the regulations, as I''ve said, I''ve moved swiftly in getting those regulations in place. I am just as frustrated as you about the slow progress here, but those regulations wouldn''t be there at all if it wasn''t for the work that we did in scrutinising the Bill, in collaboration with the former Cabinet Secretary. So, the right to go to tribunal to claim money back—. We do have to have those regulations in place, and then we have to—. As you say, it is frustrating that we can''t start that immediately, but it will come in due course.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: Building Safety Programme Update', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=17329&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'building_safety_and_leasehold', 'Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'building safety; leaseholders', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.869821', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771106', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 With regard to forcing councils to ring-fence elements of their council tax, that might have some potential, but I would imagine that it would encounter certain issues over precedence in the direction of spending in local government and, fundamentally, anything that can be done to support local authorities doing this work, because they''re the experts and it''s a regional and local problem for each property.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Dr Henry Dawson', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 1', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=2963&l=en', NULL, 178.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; local_government_enforcement; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; compulsory purchase; council tax; empty properties; empty property; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.223056', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774981', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, to speak specifically about child poverty, as somebody who used to be a member of this committee, I know that some Members will remember our scrutiny of the draft child poverty strategy of the previous Government, and then also our analysis and scrutiny of the current strategy. I''m very clear that that strategy isn''t fit for purpose, if we truly are to see change, the change that we need to see in terms of poverty rates amongst children, and more importantly, perhaps, the impact of that poverty on our youngest generations and on the future of our nation, ultimately.

 So, I am already working on the new strategy. I am drawing upon the evidence that was gathered to draw up the previous strategy, and a lot of the evidence that this committee''s predecessor committee heard in that area. So, evidence from families, evidence from organisations, from stakeholders and others, and from families over the past four years as the previous strategy was formulated, to ensure that the new strategy focuses very closely on what is important to them, and the kind of support that could assist them as they feel the impacts of poverty, but also to see how we can support them to be lifted out of poverty.

 So, with regard to the work that I''ve done, I''ve met with the external tackling poverty group, which includes organisations such as Children in Wales, Save the Children Wales, the Child Poverty Action Group, Citizens Advice, and so on. And the experts, the policy experts, are working to help us to ensure that the new strategy and the targets—I feel very strongly that we need targets and milestones along the journey—to ensure that they are fit for purpose. So, that work is being done.

 I hope to consult, then, on the new strategy and the targets early in the new year. We''ve been very clear, as a Government, in terms of what our priorities are in order to target our efforts at those policies that will have the greatest impact on people in Wales, and children living in poverty in particular. So, of course, that strategy will help us to focus our work in that regard.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=137&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.573325', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771148', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In terms of your broader question as to how we encourage more people to participate in the planning process more generally and to take an interest in what''s happening, I do think that there is an opportunity within the development of LDPs to ensure that the community voice can be heard at an earlier stage and more powerfully within that process. When it works, when the community is made aware and is involved in shaping the future of their communities—. I''ve seen it in action, happening in a way that allows housing developments that perhaps could be contentious in certain areas, but if people have been involved in the process from the outset then many of those concerns can be allayed. And therefore, I am eager to look at that aspect specifically. So, thank you for highlighting those issues.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=10126&l=en', NULL, 104.5, 'High', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'LDPs; consultation', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:59.225133', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773013', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, I look to all Members here to consider our roles in a different way perhaps to how we have considered our roles and participation in budgets of the past. In fact, the Welsh Conservatives have an advantage over all other political parties here. You''re almost the continuity party in this Senedd, because you are an opposition party and you''ve played—[Interruption.] You''re consistently an opposition party; I do not prejudge the future. But Reform is a new party in this Senedd, Labour is a party in opposition for the first time and Plaid Cymru is a party in Government, and there are new roles for all of us to play in that. But I hope that as we continue discussions over the next few weeks and months—. And those discussions are happening, and I want now to use pace and to think about how we can have those discussions across political parties and individuals in this Chamber to bring ideas into this big melting pot that is the annual budget. So, I hope that we can do that in a more meaningful way than we have done coming out of the election before the summer. And I think that''s a challenge that all of us have to rise to, and me, in particular, as the finance Minister.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16261', '2026-09-16', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16261', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16261?startPos=2982&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:16.921724', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16261&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769536', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
Committee, and today is its fourth consideration. Members of the previous Petitions Committee wanted it to be kept on as the planned flood alleviation scheme by Neath Port Talbot Council was still in its planning stages at the time, with some uncertainty over future funding from the Welsh Government. The Welsh Government’s flood alleviation funding programme for 2026-2, published in February 2026, shows £96,371 of continued commitment from 2025-26 for detailed design and full business case. Sorry, I''m unfamiliar with what that means.

 The petitioner has been in contact recently to ask about further progress on the petition. And she''s also shared a video of the flooding from last December, and notes that flooding was also experienced again in March of this year. Although there is some information available on Neath Port Talbot Council website, including historical information about the flooding issues in the area, the last update the committee received on progress was last December.

 So, our actions here are that we can write to the Welsh Government to seek further clarification about how the continued commitment to funding will translate into full implementation of the scheme at Caenant Terrace, and when it now expects that to be completed, or we could write directly to Neath Port Talbot Council again for a further update on progress in light of the Welsh Government funding commitment. And if we did go with option 2, we may wish to keep the petition open pending a response. A third option—we could just express sympathy with the petitioner but close consideration of the petition now. I would not be in favour of that option from the options available to us. And I just want to add, in light of the previous committee identifying flood management as a key priority for the seventh Senedd, the committee may want to highlight the petition with the new Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee and ask about plans to consider the issue. So, I definitely think we could write to the council to see if there is a further update on that, as a first step maybe. I don''t know if anybody else would want to do anything further at the moment, but I''d very much welcome comments.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16259', '2026-09-24', 'Sara Crowley', '', '12150', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '3. Updates to previous petitions', '16259', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16259?startPos=3288&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T12:02:31.351323', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16259&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772242', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, that work is starting this week, because tomorrow, as it happens, I will be chairing a round-table discussion related to—. The people in that meeting will be officials from other Governments, and also academics from across the United Kingdom and also the Channel Islands, where they''ve started thinking about legislation in this policy area. So, what I hear from that meeting will then feed into the first meeting of the panel that I will be co-chairing next week.

 So, I am going to be announcing the membership of that panel. Once we have made—. We are currently in the process of appointing the panel, so I''m sure you''ll appreciate that there are processes that have to be followed in terms of those appointments, but the process has long since started. But as I was saying, the membership of that group has been designed around what we want this work, this Bill, to deliver, thinking about human rights, thinking about devolution, thinking about the Welsh legislative landscape, thinking about the relative experience, thinking about the perspective of public services and how we can deliver change.

 So, this is a complex job of work. It is going to take some time to do, but the programme for government has committed to bringing forward a proposal for what this Bill, possibly, what this legislation, will look like before the end of the term, clearly. But it won''t be in the first tranche of legislation that we as a Government would want to pass, because we do want to ensure that this is done right, and we want to ensure that it is all something that is fit for purpose and within our competence as a Government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=2624&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee; programme for government', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.854747', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771214', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
