 But the point that Andrew Jeffreys has just made in terms of how we—and as I have done in a letter recently to the Chancellor—look to see how our settlement, and the settlements across the public sector investment by the UK Government, and how the Barnett consequential works for us in that—. That''s a hugely significant part of the budget that we will be putting together. We have no idea whether that settlement will increase in order to allow public services to be able to breathe sufficiently to accommodate what are the inflationary pressures that we''ve already alluded to, or whether it becomes a very real task of needing to live within the settlement we know as it is, which is around £450 million additional in the next financial year.

 We have to be very realistic with the people in Wales, with those people who work in the public sector or who receive services from the public sector, or grants into the business sector, that if it is the case that the UK Government decides to continue to press down on spending next year, and the inflationary pressures are as they are, and there''s no reason to suspect that they''ll change in the immediate future, then that''s a particularly tough set of circumstances for us as a new Government, for this Senedd, to be contemplating in the running of all the devolved services we run.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=6889&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.589071', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774349', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

Published 28/07/2026 | Reading Time minutes

On 14 July 2026, the First Minister, Rhun ap Iorwerth MS, set out the “first phase of legislation” that the Welsh Government will introduce to the Senedd. This covers areas including the right to buy community assets, renting protections and water regulation.

This article looks at these legislative proposals in more detail and how opposition parties have responded.

The First Minister committed to develop a community right-to-buy scheme, and provide the “legislative basis to enable eligible community groups to nominate and register valuable community assets to be given the right of first refusal when the owners decide to sell”.

The previous Welsh Government established a Community Asset Commission, which recommended that the Welsh Government should “introduce Community Right to Buy legislation in Wales that will be applicable across public, private and third sectors”.

Proposals relating to community right to buy were introduced in England recently.

The First Minister also committed to strengthen the rights of tenants in the private rented sector. The Welsh Government will introduce legislation that aims to strengthen the enforcement framework and regulatory role of Rent Smart Wales .

These changes are intended to “lay the groundwork” for further changes to improve housing affordability, make rents fairer, and limit no-fault evictions, which will be brought forward later in the Senedd term.

The First Minister also said the Welsh Government is developing proposals to legislate for the “right to adequate housing”.

Senedd Research has previously considered the differences between tenancy legislation in Wales and England

The First Minister committed to proposals that “support the developing climate and nature action plan”, by making existing environmental legislation “clearer, more workable and better able to support delivery” in a range of areas. The new Climate and Nature Plan will aim to deliver net zero by 2040 and substantive nature recovery by 2050.

Senedd Research recently considered what’s next for nature and climate action.

The First Minister committed to developing a “statutory duty on rural-proofing”, to “ensure rural needs are considered consistently when policies are developed and delivered”.

A consultation on this and the above environmental proposals was launched on 22 July 2026. It is yet to be decided whether the proposals on rural proofing and environmental sustainability will be taken forward through the same or separate Bills.', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/a-first-phase-of-legislation-the-welsh-government-s-legislative-priorities/', '2026-07-14', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd Research', '', '', '', NULL, 335.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'private_rented_sector; rent_controls_and_affordability; evictions_and_possession; rent_smart_wales; homelessness; social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system; Private rented sector', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Legislative commitment; Consultation open', 'Rent Smart Wales; adequate housing; community asset; community assets; community right to buy; consultation; evictions; housing affordability; introduce legislation; legislative priorities; no-fault evictions; private rented; private rented sector; right to adequate housing; right to buy; tenancy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:45.480643', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Okay, I''ve got a number of people who''ve indicated they''d like to speak, so I''ll go to Anna first.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Vikki Howells', '', '5107', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=2385&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:22.038407', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773182', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 You will know that we have committed to bring forward new legislation in the area of housing—a number of specific Bills—in order to give better protections to renters, and to look at exactly what kinds of measures are needed here in Wales as we move forward. And I do hope that we can work together, every one of us, in order to follow the same approach that we followed in the last Senedd. I look forward to having initial meetings with party spokespeople in order for us to explain how we intend to move forward with the legislation—not in this area, clearly, but in the area of housing more generally.

 You are quite right, this legislation in Wales is very broad in its scope, and it aligns, of course, with the legislation that was passed in England, but it builds on that and brings different elements in—safety and who is accountable, but also, yes, the residents'' voice. I think it is crucial that we acknowledge the residents'' voice in all of this, and the importance of including them as we move forward. So, I do intend to announce shortly that we will establish a forum for leaseholders, and the issue of building safety will be something very important that that forum will be discussing. That builds on the work that was happening during the last Government.

 I very much appreciate that I had the opportunity to hear directly from the Cladiators as a campaign group, who have been tirelessly campaigning and talking about the work that needs to happen at pace. This morning, I also had an opportunity to meet with representatives from the leaseholders of the Sand Wharf in Cardiff and Swansea Point in Swansea, and to hear directly from them about what the current experience means for leaseholders and the concern and the worry that it causes. And certainly, speaking with them and speaking with the committee as well—. As we move forward, I hope that the local government committee will help us to scrutinise the regulations to do exactly what you suggest, which is to make regulations that will lie under the legislation now to make sure that that is clear and that those regulations are suitable. So, there is that work. There is a lot of work to do under this Act; it''s not just passing the Act—there is so much work that needs to happen underneath that, and we do have a timetable for that. But the very first thing that I did was to bring forward that consultation on regulations about the costs of remediation, because I was aware that that was something that was weighing very heavily on a great number of people.

 In terms of the expert, specialist workforce that''s needed, certainly that is something that we are giving due attention to. As you know, building control departments and planning departments have lost workers, have lost staff, over a long period of time now, and, certainly, we are paying attention to that and how we can bring that expertise back in. Of course, the idea is that there would be three authorities leading on this work, and I think by doing it in that way—and I was pleased to support that amendment to the Bill as well—in doing it in that way—. We aren''t expecting all 22 local authorities to take on this burden individually. We''re going to do it in a regional way, and I think that that will create the specialist workforce that we are going to need. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: Building Safety Programme Update', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=17784&l=en', NULL, 159.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'private_rented_sector; building_safety_and_leasehold', 'Private rented sector; Property & energy', 'Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'building safety; consultation; leaseholders; renters', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.929358', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771111', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I just want to go through a couple of specifics before we look at some broader points, if that''s okay. I noted that in annex 1 of the report you published, the staff salaries and related costs in 2026-27 is £34 million, and then the estimate for 2027-28 is £37.5 million. It doesn''t line up with the figures in table 4, which show 2026-27 to be the same at £34 million, but show for next year an estimate of £38 million. Could you just explain why there''s a difference there?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Sam Rowlands', '', '8734', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=86&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:36.902285', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777352', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But there are other examples, of course, where you have cross-border England and Wales legislation, where it is convenient for you to accept that that is in operation on both sides and that it works for the Welsh Government''s aims too. In other cases, it''s where there are technical changes that don''t cause any problems. But in the end, the main principle is what is the most convenient way, in line with our principles, to make the changes.

 The principle, of course, is that we like to legislate in Wales for Wales on everything. Considering what has been said already, that isn''t always possible; you know that yourself. But there are some exceptions on occasions where we can say, ''Yes, we''ll accept that because it is 100 per cent in line with our principles'', and for the various reasons that I''ve mentioned as exceptions. Is that clear?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=3299&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.659677', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770426', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

