 So, taken together, I''m confident that this package of work will ensure that, over the next four years, we will be developing that capacity, and we will be in a position to deliver on our key priorities, as the legislative counsel has suggested. I mean, is it ideal? Is it a perfect situation? No, it is not, but very few things are in life, I tend to find.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=2400&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.491606', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770448', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Since 2023, holiday-let operators have had to achieve 182 days of commercial occupancy to qualify for business rates rather than council tax. If they fail to meet the threshold, they can face significant additional costs, often including second home premiums. In theory, Ministers may argue that this sounds reasonable, but governing is not done in theory. It must be rooted in the realities faced by communities in Tenby, Saundersfoot, Newport, St Davids, Aberaeron, Newquay and countless other coastal and rural towns whose economies depend heavily upon seasonal tourism. Owners cannot control the weather. They cannot control rising living costs that reduce holiday bookings. They cannot control wider economy uncertainty, and they also cannot control the fact that west Wales remains a highly seasonable tourism market. Yet Welsh operators face one of the highest occupancy thresholds anywhere in these islands.

 Reports suggest that almost 40 per cent of operators have failed to meet the target while many are now questioning whether their business should remain viable. These are not large investors. They are family-run businesses that support jobs and local economies. They employ local cleaners, gardeners, tradespeople and maintenance workers. They support cafes, pubs, restaurants, shops and visitor attractions across our communities.

 What is particularly difficult to justify is the comparison with England. There is the equivalent threshold of 70 nights. In Wales, it is 182—more than double. So, where is the evidence that this higher threshold is delivering better outcomes? Has housing become more affordable in Pembrokeshire and Ceredigion? Has the housing supply significantly increased? Or has this policy simply created financial pressures and uncertainty for local tourism and businesses? The evidence increasingly points to the latter. 

 The Government''s consultation generated over 1,200 responses, yet the result was largely administrative changes and technical exemptions. The central issue remained untouched: the threshold itself. This is not about protecting second homes, or those who exploit loopholes, it is about recognising that legitimate tourism businesses should not be penalised because they cannot guarantee year-round demand. The realities of Ceredigion and Penfro are not the realities of Cardiff. A one-size-fits-all approach simply does not work.

 At Reform UK, we believe that Government should support enterprise, not burden it. If housing supply is the problem, build more homes. If affordability is the issue, reform planning and increase housing delivery. But please do not place the blame on small businesses that create jobs and support one of Wales''s most important industries. So, I urge this Government to think again. Please review the 180-day rule, work with the tourism sector and local communities and introduce a fairer, more flexible approach that reflects the realities of rural Wales. Because good policy should be based on evidence, common sense and economic reality, not arbitrary targets that risk damaging the very communities that they claim to help. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Paul Marr', '', '12180', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=13656&l=en', NULL, 148.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets; housing_supply', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open', '182 days; business rates; consultation; council tax; housing delivery; housing supply; second home; second homes; self-catering', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.934431', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767084', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Just to touch on the enforcement fund as well, if I may, a lot of our enforcement work that we''ve done would not have been possible without accessing that enforcement fund. We''ve made four applications to the sum of £450,000, so it''s made a massive impact in enabling us to deal with our worst properties across the borough.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Georgina Wayman', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=4912&l=en', NULL, 207.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; housing_supply; welfare_and_support; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Leasing Scheme Wales; Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty homes; empty homes grant; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.458712', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775055', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In my own constituency of Caerdydd Ffynnon Taf, a 47 MW gas-fired power station is proposed on the Cardiff east park-and-ride site, in an entirely unsuitable urban location close to schools, care homes and the new Maple Fields housing development. Therefore, the holding directive to give time for Planning and Environment Decisions Wales to consider whether to call in this application is welcome, but communities across Wales are seeking reassurance that we will avoid an unsustainable proliferation of data centres. Please can you provide an update on a specific data centre strategy for Wales as part of the national development framework? Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Nick Carter', '', '12173', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=9619&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'Planning and Environment Decisions Wales', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:59.154049', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773005', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Whilst we welcome the decision to review the current framework, the real question is whether this process will lead to meaningful change or simply result in another strategy document, unless the Government is prepared to follow through with meaningful and measurable reforms, because Wales needs action, not bureaucracy. The Government''s own first 100 days plan states that it wants

 ''a new National Development Framework that...empowers local government and reduces costs, bureaucracy and duplication.'' 

 Those are objectives that I think many Members across the Chamber could indeed support. The challenge, however, will be whether this Government is prepared to deliver the reforms necessary to achieve them. The Minister has highlighted this review has been launched within the Government''s first 100 days. Can she therefore explain what concrete reforms she expects this process to deliver beyond consultation and engagement exercises?

 For too long, communities, businesses and local authorities have expressed frustration at a planning system that is often complex, slow and difficult to navigate. We are in the midst of a housing crisis. Too many young people are finding home ownership out of reach. Social housing waiting lists remain too high. Local authorities are under pressure to meet housing demand, whilst balancing infrastructure and environmental considerations. At the same time, businesses often face delays and uncertainty when seeking planning consent for developments that could bring jobs and investments into our local communities.

 If this revised framework is to be judged a success, it must help address these challenges. It must support the delivery of more homes in the places people actually want and need them. It must help regenerate our town centres, it must support better transport connectivity and infrastructure, and it must create the conditions for economic growth in every part of Wales.

 Planning should be about enabling opportunity, not creating obstacles. Housing must remain a central priority, but this review must also address the wider infrastructure that allows communities to thrive. New homes must be accompanied by transport links, utilities, healthcare provision and employment opportunities. Planning policies should help deliver balanced and sustainable communities, rather than simply allocating land.

 The Cabinet Minister has also referred to a national conversation. Of course, it is right that the public should have a voice in shaping the future development of Wales. But that conversation must be rooted in the practical concerns raised by people every day. When I speak to residents, they are concerned about whether their children will ever be able to afford a home in their community. They''re concerned about congestion and poor transport links. They''re concerned about attracting jobs and investments into their towns and cities. They''re also concerned about the future sustainability of their local services. Those are the conversations that really and truly matter. It is essential that local communities feel their voices are genuinely influencing decisions, rather than simply being consulted after key decisions are made already here in the Chamber.

 The Government has also spoken about empowering local government. Again, it''s a welcome aspiration, but this has to be more than warm words. It means trusting councils and local communities to shape their own future, reducing unnecessary duplication between national, regional and local structures, and it means ensuring that planning decisions are taken as close as possible to the people affected by them. I would therefore welcome further detail from the Minister on what specific planning processes she believes are creating unnecessary duplication, and what additional freedoms local authorities can expect under the new framework.

 I hope the Cabinet Minister will ensure this review recognises that different parts of Wales have different challenges. The needs of rural communities are often very different to those of cities. Coastal communities face different pressures from those in the Valleys. A successful framework must recognise those differences, rather than attempting to impose a one-size-fits-all solution from Cardiff Bay.

 The Minister also said that the revised framework will help align a range of Government plans and strategies. Whilst better co-ordination is indeed welcome, we must avoid creating additional layers of complexity that slow down decision making, investment and development. The ultimate test of this review will be whether it makes the planning system more effective, more responsive and easier to navigate for communities, businesses and local authorities alike. So, Cabinet Minister, we must approach this process constructively. We will support reforms that reduce bureaucracy. We will support measures that help deliver homes, infrastructure and investment. And we will support genuine efforts to strengthen local democracy and improve planning outcomes.

 Ultimately, this review must be judged not by the number of consultations undertaken, reports commissioned or strategies produced, but whether it delivers tangible improvements for communities across Wales. The people of Wales need a planning system that helps build homes, create jobs and strengthen community support as well as economic growth. With that in mind—and my final area of questioning will be on this—can the Minister clarify how local authorities will be given greater influence under the revised framework, what specific measures will be introduced to reduce bureaucracy and duplication, and what timetable the Government is working to for moving from consultation to implementation? That is the standard against which we will assess this Government''s proposals. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Natasha Asghar', '', '336', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=9111&l=en', NULL, 165.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'housing_supply; planning_system; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Sector bodies; Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; consultations; housing waiting lists; new homes; planning consent; planning system; social housing', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:59.073172', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773007', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I do accept the point that there are financial challenges for a company that is not for dividend and is not able to raise equity, because record levels of investment can only be funded through retained earnings and debt. If PR29 results in another step change in investment for Dŵr Cymru, like we saw in PR24, I think that will be a challenge for the company.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=1872&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:21.965203', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773174', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 How to fund that and budget for it reflects that it''s a cross-governmental responsibility. We''ve talked about how we''ve been able to weigh up and balance that preventative spend, but the truth is that elements of everybody''s budgets will be part of this. And yes, the envelope is very tight in financial terms, but in making decisions where there are two choices, we choose that preventative approach. We can then make progress in a positive manner, I believe, in terms of ensuring that housing is made healthier, that food choices are healthier through the food strategy, steps within the health service itself, and so on.

 One final point that I would make is that there is a view very often that preventative measures, as the Member said, take years sometimes to feed through the system. That isn''t always the case. It is possible to make changes within a relatively short period of time in terms of diet, which, for some people, can reverse type 2 diabetes, for example. So, the Government is looking at what can have that swifter impact, because it is possible, whilst also having to look at the longer term picture as well. But I think that there’s a fundamental change of approach here reflected throughout the programme for government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=11901&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.221080', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771036', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 One of the key changes was the introduction of the individual development plan, so the IDP. The IDP replaced both the non-statutory and the statutory statements that were in place in the previous system. So, previously, there would have been an escalation of school action, school action, and a special educational needs statement, commonly known as ''statements''. And the IDP replaced that to have one system in place, which was a statutory provision for learners assessed as having ALN. So, the Act extended the provision to children and young people from zero to 25. Before that system, there was no statutory protection for the early years age category, and under the previous system, there was a separation of a system up until the age of 16 and then a separate system post 16. So, this system aimed to bring all of that together, and that new and old system operated in parallel, through a phased transition, between September 2021 and August 2025.

 So, our overall audit question wanted to ask: do public bodies understand ALN demand? Do they understand the workforce and the skills? Do they understand the costs involved in supporting the system? And do they have a level of understanding about the outcomes and the impact from that? The audit focused mainly on Welsh Government and councils and their remit in relation to schools, although we also touched in the report on further education and a little bit on health as well. So, our evidence base included document reviews, data analysis and interviews with over 30 stakeholders, and we drew on reviews by notable colleagues in Estyn, the Children''s Commissioner for Wales and the work concluded by the Children, Young People and Education Committee from the sixth Senedd. 

 So, in terms of what we found, the report sets out that we felt that the system is under strain and is facing challenges that threaten its sustainability. Our findings show that public bodies do not have that complete picture, or enough information for effective planning and budgeting and ultimately to assess value for money. And we have concerns about the current performance of the system in supporting learners to meet their full potential. This picture is not unique in Wales; our audit colleagues in other parts of the UK have highlighted similar issues in recent years. So, our conclusions are based on evidence showing us that the system does not have good enough data to understand the extent of growing demand and complexity. There''s patchy workforce data that limits the system''s understanding about a range of capacity and skills challenges. Public bodies don''t have a good enough understanding of ALN costs, and we have concerns about the system''s financial sustainability. The goals of the ALN system are clear, but there is only a limited picture of whether it is delivering good outcomes for learners. And the system remains adversarial, despite evidence of some improvement.

 So, just to go into a bit more depth on the key evidence highlighted in our report, for the academic year 2024-25, we''re looking at around 48,000 learners in Wales aged zero to 25 who are recorded as having ALN or, previously as it was called, special educational needs. However, our report explains that this is an incomplete picture. The available data shows that there''s been a 58 per cent reduction in the number of school learners recorded as having ALN or SEN between the years 2018-19 and the years 2024-25, and the reasons for this are disputed. Over the same period, the number of school learners with ALN having that statutory status to that support through a statement or IDP—that''s increased by 164 per cent.

 So, the spending involved in supporting learners with ALN has been increasing, and this puts pressure on the wider budgets. While we cannot put a firm figure on it, the sums we can more easily identify suggest current annual spending approaching £1 billion, but this is not the full picture, and the true costs are likely to be higher. Councils'' budgeted expenditure on ALN or SEN increased by 34 per cent in real terms between the years 2018-19 and 2025-26, and while the Welsh Government described the previous system as complex, bewildering and adversarial, our field work suggests the system can still be adversarial, although this may be difficult to avoid entirely, and stakeholders described the difference that can exist between expectations and delivery, and that that can create tensions.

 The available data does show a fall in the number of appeals going to the Education Tribunal for Wales. However, this alone does not show the system is less adversarial, and other factors may contribute to that decline. Wider disputes are also difficult to assess because no routine data is collected on challenged ALN decisions that do not reach tribunal stage.

 Alongside our report, we also published a data tool that helps to set out how much councils are budgeting and spending on additional learning needs services. Following on from those findings, our report''s recommendations focus ultimately on improving information and improving the data around the system. The recommendations were written to focus specifically on the Welsh Government as the leader over national ALN policy. However, other public bodies, including councils, will need to be involved if these recommendations are to be effectively implemented.

 The recommendations do emphasise the need for more consistent data about learners with ALN across age ranges and settings, publishing clearer information about ALN costs and monitoring data on outcomes for learners with ALN. We''re currently awaiting the full response from Welsh Government. Ideally, we and the committee would have had that response by this point, in which the Government would have set out how they would have intended to respond to those recommendations. The timings of the Senedd elections in May, the summer recess and the time it''s taken for the new Government to embed have impacted on the timing of the response, and the Welsh Government agreed this revised timetable with Audit Wales.

 A letter was issued by the director general for learning, communities and culture in June 2026, which confirmed the Welsh Government acknowledged our report, and that a full response to the recommendations would be finalised during the autumn. We''ve continued to engage with Welsh Government officials, and we understand that a full response is being prepared currently. We will be having further conversations with Welsh Government officials before that is finalised to consider whether we think that it shows a full response to our recommendations.

 Just to briefly touch on other developments since the publication of our report, we''re aware of several developments around this topic since the publication in April. Members will be aware of the discussions around ALN spending during the Senedd''s consideration of the supplementary budget earlier this year. We''ve also noted that several of the data sets that we refer to in our report have been updated since we published. From our assessment, none of these fundamentally change anything we said in our report, and the direction of travel has continued to be the same, with costs continuing to rise, the overall number of learners with ALN continuing to decline, but those with the statutory status of the report continuing to increase.

 So, that concludes the briefing element. Thank you for your time. I''m happy to take any questions or observations you may have.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16267', '2026-09-21', 'Sara Leahy', '', '', '', '', 'Public Accounts and Public Administration Committee', '5. Audit Wales Brief: Additional Learning Needs (ALN)', '16267', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16267?startPos=2611&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Delivery bodies', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-23T11:54:37.531973', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16267&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770592', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I suspect that, as we progress with this work and as we publish a budget, there may well be budget lines that historically have looked a particular way that may look different, even though what we''re trying to achieve may be the same. But it needs to be seen in the context that the budget itself is better spent in a particular way in order to achieve the outcome. It''ll be for Ministers to be able to—. It''ll be for the systems that Emma has just spoken of, and the background material, to explain that, but it''ll be for Ministers to be accountable and to explain to their various committees as well how this has been done.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=7786&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; programme for government', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.738012', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774203', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But what I''ve asked my officials to do, and the stakeholders who are advising us on these plans to do, is to look at what the focus should be now as we progress, to prioritise what actions would have the greatest impact on people''s lives. The same is true of the ''Anti-racist Wales Action Plan''. That is currently being evaluated. So, that work is going to—. And I''m very clear that I want to look at what will make a difference to people''s lives in relation to that. Why do some inequalities still remain in place? What would make the biggest difference there? So, there''s a report on that, an interim report on that evaluation work, which we expect to see next spring.

 So, I also think, of course, you will know—. We have created a dashboard that was published over the summer, and there will be more data coming through on that as more data becomes available. So, I am very clear that I do not want to start something new just for the sake of starting something new. I think, certainly in terms of some of these plans, there are certainly things we can build on. What I am clear about is that I want to see a difference being made in people''s lives. Plans should not just be a list of the actions that we take, which are praiseworthy and good, but things that lead to a change in people''s lives.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=4758&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.046946', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771291', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think that the engagement that''s undertaken by the Senedd with schools, for example, and beyond, and the Youth Parliament and so forth, is positive. And I know that we as a Senedd have been nominated for an award in certain categories in the past as well. So, I do take your point about being able to see that. 

 But, Manon, do you want to add anything on what more we could give to the committee to understand this better?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=1939&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.216686', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777393', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In this case, each case did meet the criteria. They were approved by the executive team. But, in addition, we reported what we had done to our remuneration committee. That''s only required for payments that exceed £95,000, but I think for full transparency, we kept the committee fully apprised of what had been done.

 Both posts have been deleted, but we have created one single new post. The reason for that is that changes in business processes, technology, service demand and working patterns meant that we needed something different in that space. The volume and nature of work had changed, the skills required to deliver that work had changed, and that is what has been wrapped up in this single new role that we have, designed to meet current need, but also our expectations of future need in the business.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Kevin Thomas', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=2260&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.179825', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774380', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 For many communities, tourism is not simply an important industry, it is the industry. It supports family businesses, keeps our high streets alive and helps sustain the very communities that people want to visit. When tourism prospers, our community prospers. But that prosperity depends on businesses having the confidence to invest, employ local people and plan for the future. Tourism businesses understand the need to support local communities and recognise the pressures on the housing market; what they need are policies that reflect the commercial realities of a seasonal industry.

 We all share the same ambition: a successful tourism industry alongside communities where local people can afford to live. Indeed, your own manifesto recognised the importance of tourism, and I quote:

 ''Plaid Cymru recognises the importance of the tourism industry to Wales.''

 ''We will promote tourism which provides maximum benefit to our people, environment, language and culture''.

 I agree with that ambition. The question is not the destination, the question is whether the route chosen is actually taking us there.

 The issue I want to focus on today is the 182-day occupancy requirement for self-catering holiday accommodation. Let me be clear: this debate is not about whether there should be a qualifying threshold—the tourism industry accepts that there should be one; the question is whether 182 days is realistic, proportionate and evidence based.

 Representatives from across the tourism industry have consistently argued that 105 days is a more realistic threshold for demonstrating genuine commercial activity. His Majesty''s Revenue and Customs recognise 105 days of actual letting as a qualifying test for the furnished holiday lettings tax regime. Ministers may argue that the purposes are different, but the principle remains the same: identifying genuine commercial activity. And, I have to say, persuading HMRC to agree to anything outside their own way of thinking is no small achievement. It''s certainly not an organisation renowned for changing its mind simply because somebody asked politely. So, what evidence led the previous Welsh Government, with Plaid Cymru support, to conclude that 182 days was a necessary and proportionate figure? Why 182? What modelling or economic assessment supported that decision? If that evidence exists, publish it.

 The Government''s stated objective was to increase the supply of homes for local people, but intentions do not automatically create good outcomes. The assumption behind the policy was that raising the threshold would return more properties to the residential market. But has that happened to any meaningful extent? Many tourism businesses report that income has fallen off significantly, with some parts of the self-catering sector reporting reductions of around 30 per cent. The Welsh Government''s own tourism business barometer highlights continuing pressures within the sector. But reducing holiday lets was never the objective. The real test is whether more homes have become available for local people. So, the question remains: how many properties have actually returned to the residential housing market because of this policy? How many homes have become available to local families? What measurable improvement has there been in housing affordability or availability?

 If the Government believes this policy has succeeded, publish the evidence. I understand Ministers are considering further flexibility and additional exemptions, and I welcome the fact that they are listening. But there is a certain irony here. A policy introduced to solve one problem is now requiring exemptions to solve the problems created by the policy itself. The tourism industry is not asking for special interest. The message is loud and clear: it is asking for a system that is fair, proportionate and based on evidence. So, Cabinet Minister, if you remain convinced that 182 days is the correct figure, publish the evidence that justifies choosing it. Publish the evidence that shows it has delivered more homes for local people. Because if this policy requires an ever-growing list of exemptions simply to make it workable, perhaps the problem is not the exemptions, perhaps it is the policy itself. Exemptions are not the answer. Good government is measured by whether policies deliver the outcomes they promise. If the evidence shows that this one is not, then we need real and substantial change. I call on all Members to support our motion. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Steven Rodaway', '', '12191', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=12421&l=en', NULL, 93.5, 'High', 'digest', 'rent_controls_and_affordability; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Private rented sector', '', '', '182 days; holiday lets; housing affordability; self-catering', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.790908', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767079', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 If complaints come in that are simply incorrect—. People assume AI''s always right; it of course isn''t, and if people don''t check it before they''ve sent it in, it can be wrong. So, one of the things we see is—. There''s different legislation in England to Wales, and they will bring in complaints based on different bits of legislation. Again, it''s okay, we say to staff, to push that back and say, ''Sorry, this is incorrect; you need to apply a lens to this.'' So, in that way, we''re trying to put it back to the complainants and asking them to do that again in a way that is a bit more manageable for us.

 And on the other side of it, obviously, we''re starting to think—and we''ll probably come on to this later; Sarah can talk about the work we''re doing on AI within the organisation—about how can we use that to help us with our own productivity and efficiency. So, that''s the other side of it, really. But, with the complaints, it''s just a pragmatic approach in saying to staff, ''It''s okay to go back and ask for another revision of that complaint.''', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=7512&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.774739', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777599', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
