This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=986&MId=16410', '2026-11-23', '', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '', '16410', '', '2026-11-23', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Legislation Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-10-02T13:01:05.773118', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 One of the things about case loads is that, in recent years, we''ve moved to manage those so that staff are not overwhelmed and they don''t carry huge case loads where it starts to become a bit much and they can''t see the wood for the trees. So, we''ve limited case loads to try and help them with that and help them move through that work more quickly. We have proper supervision arrangements in place, and that''s particularly important when we''re working in a hybrid way and some staff are working from a home environment, and proper performance management as well, to make sure—. And that''s not just about making sure that we get through the work; it''s about supporting people who perhaps are struggling or who need a bit of extra help. So, all of that is in place.

 We''ve got some very good, I think, supportive human resources policies in place. We do stress risk assessments, or we certainly offer them to staff—if they''re starting to feel a bit overwhelmed, how do we help them? And we do have external counselling services if they wish to talk to someone independent to assist them with issues. So, we do a lot of different things. We also have a well-being group, who are quite active in doing different initiatives to support staff. And we recognise teams coming together regularly to support each other, and we do two days a year where we bring all staff together into all-staff events as well, and there''s a well-being element to that, as well as an important business element.

 We''re committed to—. Our place doesn''t work without the staff. They''re the ones that do all the work, and we spend 75 per cent to 80 per cent of our money on staff, so this is hugely important. We want to support them to deliver to their full potential, we''ve got ambitious targets we want to achieve, but we recognise we''ve got a responsibility as an organisation to help them, sometimes, when things are not so good.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=11156&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.331722', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777733', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Welsh Labour has been clear that we at least intend to be a constructive opposition. That''s why we do welcome the principles of today''s announcement, recognising the permanent reduction of 30 per cent, providing valuable support and assurances to businesses in accommodation, hospitality and leisure that qualify for this reduction. This announcement, of course, builds on the policies of the previous Welsh Labour Government that provided over £1 billion of rate support for hospitality and leisure businesses since 2020-21.

 But we do have to pay for this reduction, don''t we? The Minister says that the proposals are cost neutral and that the burden will fall on the largest businesses. But we also have to recognise that non-domestic rates are based on the value of property, not the turnover of a company, its profit or its ability to pay. A business that has a high rateable value as a site isn''t necessarily a large business or necessarily a very profitable business.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=10033&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.088934', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769218', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The attack on universality that we had earlier today, our belief in universality and making sure that we bring all parents in to the support that can be given through childcare, whilst, at the same time, emphasising the need to tackle child poverty through the Cynnal payment, I think all of that reflects something that I''m very proud to stand by. We will be measured by the way that we adhere throughout to those values of ours and, yes, building a united, peaceful Wales that rejects division is something that means an awful lot to, I know, the benches that I sit on, but hopefully all of us here too.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=11356&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.128439', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771017', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I''m a member of the Co-operative Party, who have long campaigned for a community right-to-buy scheme, and I support legislation to enable eligible community groups to nominate and register valuable community assets and be given the right to first refusal when the owner decides to sell. Far too many community buildings are left to deteriorate, and community action could stop some of these becoming derelict. Is any funding going to be made available to help these community purchases?

 I also want us to leave a greener legacy for generations to come, and I welcome the environmental proposal to support developing a climate and nature action plan. What I will ask, though, on animals, is: will the proposed legislation provide additional support for animals in Wales? And again I ask for legislation to stop the private ownership of primates.

 I note in the First Minister''s statement that control over water runs far deeper in Wales''s collective memory than it being seen merely as a matter of policy or regulation. Your economy Minister joined me in the last Senedd in supporting the public ownership of water. Will the Welsh Government consider nationalising Welsh Water?

 Finally, on the economy, will the Welsh Government consider funding university research in computing and life sciences and the spin-offs that come from it?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Mike Hedges', '', '332', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=10709&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'community assets', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.217563', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767788', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

