 However, there are elements of this Bill that do have an impact on issues or matters that have been devolved and we have expressed concern about elements relating to transfer plans, plans that are to do with taxation issues, and looking at how they will collect data and who will manage that data and share it through their SPR, namely the single patient record. So, those were our concerns, but our officials have been in constant touch with the officials in the UK Government and have been discussing these issues, and we have literally just had a response from the Department of Health and Social Care of the UK Government that tackles some of those points. I understand, although I haven''t had time to fully assess the response, that they will be introducing amendments to the Bill during its progress through the House of Lords, based on our concerns. So, those concerns will be allayed.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16299', '2026-10-08', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', 'Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '269', '', '', 'Health and Social Care Committee', '2. Legislative Consent: Health Bill - evidence session 4', '16299', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16299?startPos=118&l=en', NULL, 51.7, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'LCM; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:09.077790', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16299&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777763', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Unpicking a bit more some of the points that were raised there—on the NHS, it was and is priority No. 1. This is a Government that has already made choices, including on a budget deal with the last Labour Government, to ensure that we have the resources to make sure that we build a more sustainable future for the NHS. We begin now from the point of the legacy that we inherited from the last Labour Government. Data is just coming through now from the time of the election and the weeks following that, and, yes, it confirms how dire the situation is that we are trying to resolve. But through the measures outlined in this programme for government, building towards the introduction of up to 10 hubs, a change in emphasis in terms of the work patterns of doctors—this is clinically led, remember, to free up time, to get more people through the system—the injection of £150 million, some of it clinical, some of it on the actual buildings themselves, which will be the home of a sustainable NHS for the future, we stand firm by the pledges that we have made and in making this a priority.

 On this element of somehow north Wales not being mentioned in this programme for government, I come from even further north than he does, and this is a Government—and I have made it clear—that isn''t about the tokenism of having a north Wales Minister; every single Minister in this Government is a north Wales Minister and a south Wales Minister and a west Wales Minister, because we believe in bringing Wales together and building a brighter future for people, wherever they are. And to attack the first north Wales First Minister for somehow not caring about the north isn''t something that people are going to even believe makes sense, frankly.

 I come back to additional learning needs and remind the Member again that he had £9 million that Labour were going to put into something that we consider to be very important; we''re glad that he does too. We had £100 million on the table; we have £40 million that we can spend this year. That is what this party put on the table, seriously, in a way that isn''t just money but is money that can be spent—money that can be spent to make a real difference to councils, to schools, to parents, to children. The offer is still very much available. This has to be about more than politics.

 And I will end, if I may, on this attack, which I''ve heard mentioned and read about it in the paper—and I was thinking, ''Surely, he''s not going to make this accusation here in the Senedd Chamber''—that the childcare offer is something that he objects to in its universality. So, suddenly, the Labour Party objects to the universality that provides bus passes for pensioners, so we can assume that Labour wants to take away bus passes for pensioners, we can assume that they''re against the universality of free prescriptions. Can we start thinking that also they want the universality of the NHS, even, to be taken away from people? Is he happy to create a two-tier system where rich people are to be supported to go off into private healthcare, or does he, like me, believe that the more we can build together—yes, including that principle of universality—binds us together as a nation and gives us solutions for the long term that address the needs of people in all parts of Wales from all backgrounds?

 We have here a programme for government that is about transforming Wales, and I guess the point to make on governing being difficult is that government is difficult, especially when you inherit a broad range of issues that don''t put us off, that don''t make us diminish our ambitions, but make us work harder to deliver them for the people of Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=9827&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.888554', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770995', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think this year is one where we are starting the conversations about a new strategy, asking ourselves some quite profound questions—what are we here for, what difference can we make, how can we deliver for Wales, generally? So, I think, tilting our new strategy to something that is much more explicitly outward-facing is something that we''re talking about, and, in fact, we had a really good board session just yesterday, led by Ian, where we had a good conversation, with some of my early thinking, and the thinking of the team, around impacts, how we measure that and so on. 

 So, yes, I think—. I wouldn''t want to say to the committee that we''re going to hit our KPIs—. As I said earlier, I think we want to stretch ourselves. We are an ambitious organisation. But getting that core work right is absolutely fundamental, those quality measures Ann-Marie spoke about before, and really valuing the professional expertise that we have is really important, and then how that weaves into trying to arrest this—reading the social attitudes survey the other day—concerning decline in public trust in public institutions, which continues to slide. And any part we can play in pulling that back, I think, we want to. So, yes, it''s a moment of profound reflection about our role and where we can really make a difference, particularly in the value-add space, if I can put it like that, building on this bedrock of the work that we do every day on performance and finance audit. 

 We touched on it last time as well, but if we are able to take the advantage of just making those few tweaks to our legislative framework, so that we can really focus where risk is, and elevate the impact of some of our thematic work, we would really like to do that, and that''s something, perhaps, we''ll return to when we look at the estimate discussion.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Catherine Mealing-Jones', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=3611&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.461717', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774384', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The Minister has told us that this revision will cut costs, bureaucracy and duplication, and I certainly welcome that, Minister, but I''ve read your party''s manifesto, and it points in a completely opposite direction. Plaid promised Welsh language impact assessments in the planning system. They''ve promised community ownership stakes in larger energy companies, a new Welsh water regulator and a review of second homes and holiday lets, not to mention a new climate and nature action plan. Some of these, though, Minister, are not mentioned in this statement. But every one of those is another piece of red tape, another stakeholder, more paperwork for local sites to wade through, and another reason for planners to say ''no''.

 So, firstly, can the Minister tell this Chamber which of these new layers is she prepared to drop in the name of efficiency, which she wants? Because the numbers demand it. When ''Future Wales'' was adopted, the Government said that Wales needed 7,400 homes a year. In 2024-25, we built under 5,000, and last year we built just over 5,000, still well below the levels pre-pandemic. Her own experts now say we need 8,700 a year. We in Reform said plainly during the election that Wales needs at least 10,000 additional homes just to catch up. So, I ask the Minister: will this framework finally contain a delivery figure for all tenures that this Senedd can hold her and the Government to account on? And we need to include all tenures because, as I mentioned before, Minister, 83 per cent of Welsh homes are owner-occupied or are private rented accommodation, yet this Government acts like the private sector is irrelevant. Almost every housing headline this Government produces is about social housing, about speeding up development for social housing or anything that ticks the box for net zero.

 This country already has one of the largest housing stocks in Europe for social housing. So, in the framework, will the Minister rule out any two-tier systems for private and social, making sure that any cuts to red tape also apply to private developments, to help the majority who are private renters and mortgage holders, because, Minister, private developers deliver almost half of so-called affordable housing? Of course, the only way to have generally affordable housing is by making sure that market supply actually meets demand. The young couple saving up for a home, or a family renting privately, deserve a Government that is on their side too. Where is the ambition for people to want to own their own home?

 Then there is drainage. Since 2019, any development in Wales of more than one home has needed a separate sustainable drainage system approval before a spade goes into the ground. England has never switched that regime on; drainage is handled through planning and expected on schemes of 10 houses or more. So, a small, local builder building two houses faces a second approval, a second fee and a second delay that his competitor over the border doesn''t have.

 So, as you launch a national conversation—perhaps Plaid are out of ideas—let me contribute to it with these ideas that Reform have already published. Put clear statutory time limits on planning decisions and put financial penalties on planning departments that fail to meet them. Limit repeat consultations, relax the rules so that farming families can build homes on their own land and, finally, commit that the new framework carries a burden test, which is what I''ve asked for previously.

 None of this needs a conversation. Our planning system doesn''t need another long review. We, on these benches, will back measures that get homes built faster. But if this revision simply adds Plaid''s new layers on top of Labour''s old ones, this whole process will cause more harm than good.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Francesca O''Brien', '', '12190', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=8262&l=en', NULL, 280.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'private_rented_sector; second_homes_and_short_term_lets; housing_supply; planning_system; social_housing; local_government_enforcement', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Private rented sector', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', 'Consultation open', 'Future Wales; affordable housing; consultations; financial penalties; holiday lets; planning system; private rented; renters; second homes; social housing', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:58.962115', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772992', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
