 ''proportion of complaints handled by public bodies and then escalated to us''.

 I think your target is under 5 per cent, and it went up from 6 per cent to 11 per cent. Likewise there''s been an increase in some of the similar KPIs, and that''s having invested in and improved capacity.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=10016&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.153937', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777605', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Following Natural Resources Wales''s marine assessment and subsequent planning advice to councils last year, social and affordable housing schemes across Carmarthenshire and Pembrokeshire face significant delays. I have been contacted by one contractor who builds almost exclusively for affordable social housing, and he has an order book of £30 million-worth of secured work that has now stalled. Its turnover has halved, it supports approximately 80 staff and 320 subcontractors, from groundworkers and bricklayers through to merchants, plant hire firms and delivery drivers. Everyone accepts in this Chamber the need to protect our rivers and estuaries, but nutrient pollution comes from a wide range of sources across whole catchment areas. These delays are affecting both families waiting for homes and the firms ready to build them.

 So, can we have a statement on what has replaced the marine nitrates development taskforce, what further mitigation measures are being considered, and when these housing schemes can realistically move forward? Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=7540&l=en', NULL, 132.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'housing_supply; social_housing; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'First Minister / Cabinet; LGHP Committee members', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; affordable housing; housing delivery; social housing', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.535430', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770956', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But to your question about back into Government, what we always do is that the reports, whether own-initiative or public interest, the ones where we''re identifying systemic issues, those reports will come to all Members of the Senedd. But, absolutely, we specifically write, or I specifically write, at that point to the Minister. So, everything on health will go to the Minister, and likewise to other Ministers. But, as you can imagine, a lot of the systemic issues would be in health.

 We also meet on a twice-yearly basis with senior officials in the health and social care department. We talk about our work, what we''re seeing, and, certainly, the themes and the concerns we might have about what we''re seeing coming through. I am about to write to the Cabinet Minister again, really, just highlighting some of the themes that we''re seeing coming through in our work, aside from the formal report. So, we do try to do that. We''re very conscious that the public money that''s invested in the work that we do—. It''s important that, when we find things, we put them in front of the people that have got the authority, the power and the responsibility, actually, for holding these bodies to account and driving service improvement.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=7830&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.838226', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777578', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Likewise, in relation to the part of the recommendation related to other languages, we have been trying to promote that, and that''s embedded in our new strategic plan, to ensure that people know that if they want to use a different language with us, they can use that. One of the key pieces of work we''ve got in relation to the oral complaints service is to try and really target that at people who don''t normally reach us, so we''re working with third sector voluntary organisations to try and promote that service now as well.

 But we want to improve the position on the Welsh language provision. We can''t force people to use our service in Welsh, clearly, and we know from the research that we undertake that, actually, people who are fluent Welsh speakers know about our service pretty well, that the levels of recognition are good. So, we just need to make sure that our services are accessible and our website is being reviewed to make sure it''s really clear that people can access those provisions in Welsh and other languages.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Katrin Shaw', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=9464&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.070457', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777574', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We all want to see more housing as soon as possible, as has been said today, but there has to be some common sense attached to it. I know this has been brought up quite a number of times today in this Senedd, and I want to just reiterate points made by the Member for Carmarthenshire and the Member for Fflint Wrecsam, because my constituency shares those same pressures, and that''s why I welcome the consultation that is open right now. But how do you envisage better infrastructure planning around new housing developments? And, as you develop the new national development framework following the consultation, will you work with local authorities to look to reform planning policy to ensure that any large housing developments are only approved where there is the necessary infrastructure already in place, or there is clear funding commitment to deliver it alongside the housing? As you''ve said today, that should be happening, but as you know and I know and this Chamber knows—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=7810&l=en', NULL, 104.5, 'High', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; planning policy', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.723501', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776794', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Picking up on the points that the panel have made so far, council tax is only as good as what goes into it. Across what we believe are the seven definitions of types of empty homes to record, to look at and investigate, one of those is properties that are recorded on council tax as being occupied and owner-occupied, but the empty homes officers themselves know they''re empty. We will never know how many of those there are out there without complaints or referrals from the public. We could explore more where councils look into commercial property and business property. We could look at domestic rates valuations and rates of compliance there. But, again, that is much of an unknown for an empty homes officer that''s only really ever dealt with residential property. So, the data is as good as it can be for what we need it. Obviously, it could always be better with some input and maybe some reform later down the line.

 In terms of what Scotland are doing, I echo what Andrew has already said, that Wales is—. And I always refer to Wales as being three or four steps ahead when it comes to empty homes work. The programmes, the schemes, the initiatives always are way ahead of the game when it comes to comparing Wales with England and Scotland. Scotland are only just a little bit behind, but they are at least having these kinds of conversations and looking at exploring other types of enforcement power. Compulsory sale orders is one that''s been talked about over the past five, six years, but has never been brought to fruition. But, from our point of view, the fact that it is being talked about, that it is being considered and that these things are an option is very good.

 Going back to what the panel have said so far in terms of enforcement, all of the enforcement powers that we have, the EDMO, the compulsory purchase order, the enforced sale order, none of those are designed to do empty homes work en masse. They''re only designed to do and deal with empty homes individually, kind of on a case-by-case basis. That''s why you''ll only ever see a handful of them done, if at all, throughout a year. They''re not designed to bring hundreds of empty homes back into use in one fell swoop. So, they are case by case; they have to be a last resort. That''s the way that they''re designed, that''s the way that legislation deems for us to use them. With that in mind, and with the fact that all of these powers will only ever bring maybe 1 per cent or 2 per cent of properties back into use a year, we need more powers or we need more options. We need more solutions.

 One of my famous quotes that people always quote me on is that if there are a million empty homes in England or if there are 25,000 empty homes in Wales, we actually need 25,000 different solutions because every home is different. Every property is different. Most importantly, every person''s circumstance is different. So, we have to look at all of our options. We have to explore everything that we possibly can do, both from a local authority point of view, but also at a higher level, a Government level, to spread those options out, or at least increase the availability and suitability of options for empty homes.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Adam Cliff', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 1', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=937&l=en', NULL, 178.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; local_government_enforcement; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; compulsory purchase; council tax; empty homes; empty properties; enforcement powers; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:24.909573', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775336', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, the reason that I say this in terms of this question is because all of that legislation was in English, of course, and it''s important to consider as well that the legislation that existed for Wales was also English law or was legislation that applied to the rest of the United Kingdom. So, every time we legislate, we have decisions to make on how we legislate, and going right back to when we started this work, I would say that it''s easier, almost without exception, to amend the legislation that already exists—namely to do it in English. That is just easier, and that''s the reality.

 But we decided 15 years and more ago that we wouldn''t do it like that, that we would try and restate as much as we could and create legislation for Wales in particular that was bilingual. Therefore, for example, going back to the law on consent for organ donation, that would have been quite a bit easier to do if we just changed the 2004 UK legislation, but we took out that subject in its entirety, 30 pages—. It was just one small change we were making, of course, just changing the consent, but we took all of that legislation on the subject, all 30 pages of it, and we restated it. I think it was only one page that changed the law, but we created it in the Welsh context and it was bilingual.

 And that''s what we do then by consolidating and codifying the law. Every time we do that, we increase the bilingual legislation that exists. So, it''s just important to understand that context. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dylan Hughes', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=4694&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.936196', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770611', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think that part of it is then getting into the practical stuff that''s come on the back of that. Yesterday I announced, which was almost—. It feels like an aside, but for me it was a really important thing. When we talk about the Burns stations, Transport for Wales are going to be building the Burns stations. This is about us putting—. This is maintaining that economic benefit in Wales, which I think everyone would be pleased with, but it''s also about moving quicker, because Transport for Wales have only got Wales. I know they''ve got a bit at the borders, but it''s Wales they care about. It''s Wales that they''re responsible for, so we can actually get those supply chains moving really quickly.

 That links me into the £14 billion. Again, if I take it just on the words, in the meetings I''ve had with the Department for Transport, and the meetings that were being attended by the two Secretaries of State, the £14 billion has been consistently supported and reiterated. So, we''re in a position where I can''t expect anything more. It is absolute. Part of me trying to help that is the Transport for Wales vision document, which is something that I''ve endorsed in full. That was done in the previous administration by Transport for Wales. There are two reasons for that. One reason that we talked about, I think, is that transport is strategic, so you can''t keep flitting and changing between things. The second reason is it''s a really good document. It''s good. It actually does the job that I think Wales needs from a rail perspective.

 But we''ve been underinvested in rail from a capital perspective to a degree that is a disgrace, I think, and the people of Wales need to see that improvement happen. I''d encourage all parties in the Senedd to support that position, because we''ve proven, when we''ve got responsibility for rail on the core Valleys lines, we can make a difference. We need to do that across the whole of Wales. It''s not just Cardiff that needs to benefit; we need to do it across the whole country. We''ve put a specific ask in this year. Elin has written to John Healey, the Chancellor, requesting that £1 billion is put into this spending review so we can move on the Burns stations in particular. If you could, as a committee, be another supporter to that as well, that would be a help.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16288', '2026-10-07', 'Mark Hooper', '', '4822', '', '', 'Economy, Energy and Connectivity Committee', '2. General Ministerial Scrutiny: Transport', '16288', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16288?startPos=4166&l=en', NULL, 17.2, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-10-08T13:49:57.804809', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16288&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777037', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

Published 22/07/2026 | Reading Time minutes

Air quality is an important public health issue in Wales. This research briefing examines the main sources of air pollution, how air quality is monitored and regulated, the health effects of pollution, and how pollution levels have changed over time.

Air pollution can be caused by both human activity and natural processes. Major pollutants such as particulate matter, nitrogen oxides, and ozone, are continuously monitored across Wales. The main human sources of air pollution include transport, domestic burning, industry, electricity production, and agriculture.

The burden of poor air quality is equivalent to around 1,000 to 1,400 deaths each year in Wales. A large body of evidence shows that air pollution can cause negative health outcomes, including respiratory and cardiovascular diseases, cancer, adverse pregnancy outcomes, and effects on cognitive health. Exposure and risk are not evenly distributed, with some groups and communities more affected than others. Wider social and economic consequences include pressures on the NHS and the interaction between pollution and existing inequalities.

Action at both the UK and Wales level has sought to limit the production of air pollution and people’s exposure to that pollution. Notable legislative milestones include the Environment Act 1995 and the Environment (Air Quality and Soundscapes) (Wales) Act 2024 (the 2024 Act). Legislation empowers and obliges the Welsh Government and local authorities to act to reduce air pollution, including by declaring Air Quality Management Areas and smoke control areas , and introducing policies to bring pollution below legal thresholds. In 2026, the last Welsh Government used powers in the 2024 Act to set targets for reducing fine particulate matter pollution . The Welsh Government has also acted to reduce pollution from domestic burning, agriculture, and transport .

Concentrations of many major pollutants, including sulphur dioxide, nitrogen dioxide, and particulate matter, have fallen substantially in recent decades . Continuing challenges include urban ozone pollution and local variation in exposure. It can be challenging to isolate the specific impact of individual policy measures. Case studies focussing on Port Talbot steelworks closure and 50 mph speed limits on trunk roads illustrate how local air quality can change in response to industrial and transport developments. Action to reduce greenhouse gas emissions can also have a co-benefit of reducing air pollution, and therefore improving human and environmental health.

Research briefing by Charlotte Morgan and Dr Matthew Sutton , Senedd Research, Welsh Parliament

Senedd Research acknowledges the parliamentary fellowship provided to Charlotte Morgan by the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) which enabled this briefing to be completed.', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/air-quality-and-public-health/', NULL, '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd Research', '', '', '', NULL, 20.0, 'Noise', 'archive', 'local_government_enforcement', 'Housing system', '', '', 'environmental health', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:46.483932', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think we are helped this year by the fact that the committee last year did agree that increase in our funding that I referred to, which helped us to put more staffing in place. And we set our structure earlier this year—seven new staff working within front-line casework positions. So, that is helping us. And the reason we asked for that money last year was to accommodate the increases we were seeing, but to try and futureproof us, so that we weren''t continually coming back to committee and asking for more staff. So, that, I think, is reflected in the estimate this year—the work that we did last year, and the support we had from the committee.

 What we''re focusing on this year in our estimate, really, is how we can be more efficient, more productive with the resources that we have. And that''s about investment in technology, which we detailed in the estimate, and it''s about using that investment to look at new ways of working and continually sort of push ourselves in how we''re doing things, to try and get more resource out of the organisation through those ways. So, that really is the focus of our estimate this year, based on the work that we did last year with the committee.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=10601&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.253633', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777553', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Tourism accounts for around 13 per cent of all employment in Ceredigion and 23 per cent in Pembrokeshire, so I''m delighted that this Government recognises the importance of the tourism industry to Wales. But evidence gathered from the previous Government showed that high levels of tourism, if not managed, can have a negative impact on an area. High levels of second homes and short-term lets can impact on local housing markets and the sustainability of local communities, particularly in areas where properties are not occupied for much of the year.

 It''s important that we are promoting tourism that provides maximum benefit to our people, environment, language and culture, and I welcome the lots of ideas that have come up in this debate already. So, I fully support the Government taking steps to enable sustainable tourism that secures jobs and income into local economies and which benefits our communities, environment, language and culture. And I support measures that prevent harm. That''s why I support the visitor levy as a choice for local authorities to ensure that local communities reap the rewards of tourism. Funds raised through the visitor levy enable investment in the local environment, the Welsh language and culture, and local infrastructure, which also supports tourism.

 However, it''s also important for the Government to act quickly when good policy intentions are not delivering the intended change and, in the case of the 182-day threshold, may be in danger of taking us in the wrong direction. Since becoming a Member of the Senedd, I''ve met with many constituents who have told me about the challenges that the 182-day rule for holiday lets is creating. I''ve heard about the pressure that the policy is putting on many self-catering businesses and the toll it is taking on people running local businesses. I''ve heard how it is threatening the viability of some local businesses, as well as jobs and spend in the wider economy, and I''ve also seen examples where it''s not achieving the intended outcome for the local housing market.

 So, whilst I fully support the intention of the 182-day rule, I also welcome this Plaid Cymru Government''s commitment to keep the 182-day occupancy threshold under review, and I urge the Government to begin this work quickly, so that we can find pragmatic solutions that better support the original purpose of the policy. This should include whether 182 days is the right threshold, but also have clear and reasonable exemptions, particularly where a property would not qualify as a private home. We need clear guidance to avoid holiday let owners being left stunned by unaffordable backdated council bills, sometimes by three or four years, or living with the mental stress that they soon could be. So, that''s why I''m pleased to support the Government amendment today, and I urge other Members to do so. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Anna Nicholl', '', '12178', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=12193&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', '', '182 days; holiday let; holiday lets; second homes; self-catering; short-term lets; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.759645', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767061', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Having said that, we would want more people who don''t get the outcome they want to be satisfied. I am reassured that 87 per cent of the people who get the outcome they want are satisfied with what we do. What drags that figure down to the 36 per cent is the people who are not satisfied because we don''t take the complaint forward, or we don''t actually uphold it.

 So, it is of concern. We are very aware of it. We will continue to work, particularly through our caseworkers who have the contact with complainants, to make sure that we''re quite clear about why we can''t do something, why we''ve made a particular decision, and try to explain the rationale for it. Sometimes, people will accept that and perhaps say, ''Okay, I can see that; I''m satisfied.'' Other times people will not, and I referenced already at the start of the meeting we are seeing more and more persistence amongst complainants—they have a right to that, of course—but less likely to take that ''no'' for an answer, and those are the people who will remain dissatisfied.

 We''ll keep working on this. We want to drive this figure up, and we''ve set ourselves a stretching target to try and achieve that. It is a concern, but we''ll carry on working on it.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=7990&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.862096', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777596', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

