 The Cabinet Minister mentioned the legislation pool as well. Just to give you a little bit more information around that, that''s a pool of individuals who essentially belong in my division, but that I can deploy out to the Bill projects that need them the most. That not only gives us quite an agile way into some of these projects, perhaps more agile than previously, but it also brings expertise within a cohort—people who have managed a Bill before, people who have experience in that very difficult job of taking a policy idea and working with lawyers, stakeholders and economists to build it into instructions to legislative counsel that can work and can be delivered successfully and to time. I just think it''s a good illustration of trying to build not just more capacity but more agility and more expertise into the system.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Sophie Brighouse', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=2523&l=en', NULL, 115.0, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Bill progress', 'Legislation Committee; legislative programme; subordinate legislation', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.514056', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770452', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, it''s going to be down to each individual registration officer to make that assessment. They''ll need to make sure they have a really clear view about how the data was compiled, what sorts of verification processes were in place when that data was being compiled, what coverage it''s likely to have, whether it''s universal or it''s targeting particular groups, how frequently that data is being updated, so is it older data or has it been refreshed relatively recently? Those are all considerations that they''re going to need to take into account.

 We''ve flagged guidance about this. We''ve been able to publish guidance for registration officers this week, which is based on the regulations as they''ve been tabled. So, it''s guidance about what they might do once the regulations come into force. And that sets out some of the questions that registration officers will need to be asking themselves to help satisfy themselves that they''re getting hold of the right data.

 I think just one other concern that we know registration officers have been flagging is whether they can actually secure access to data, or even where it''s held within their local authority. Some of them have reported maybe some challenges in getting access to that data, and it does take time sometimes to achieve that. So, it might be that, again, for this first year, there are data sources that haven''t been made available in time for the matching process. But in future years, with more time available, that might be more possible.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Hawthorn', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=2350&l=en', NULL, 109.2, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; council tax; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.054592', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769882', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But to be serious and to respond to the points that you''ve made, this will be a contribution that alleviates some of the pressure on our hospitality and leisure sector. It is more than just pubs, it is cafes and restaurants, and it is gyms and museums and libraries. So, it''s the widest possible array, and when you add that to shops as well, it''s the widest possible array of many of the businesses that feature in our town centres and some of our villages as well. So, it goes further than Government has done before in Wales. So, that''s the important contribution of today. But I''m sure, as some have said already, that there are a myriad of different other issues that face our hospitality and leisure sectors. There are many pressures in our economy. The inflationary pressure that we are feeling today and will possibly be increasing again is something that puts particular pressure on so many of our high-street businesses. There are actions that the UK Government as well as the Welsh Government can take. But, hopefully, today will have been a strong signal that this Government and this Senedd believe that our small businesses that are trying to work so hard to keep going in our communities right throughout Wales in difficult times—this Government has their back and will try and take all initiatives that it can to support them in these testing times.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=11250&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.265363', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769234', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 You''ve talked about what happens in Scotland and in Ireland. I guess my question is: have you talked to the UK Government to see whether what we''re going to be producing here is going to be that much different to what they already have? What I''m really concerned about is that if the framework comes out in the autumn period, we then have a debate and discussion, I''m assuming, about what that framework then looks like, we have a back and forward—. In the meantime, there are contracts on the table, jobs coming to Wales. Could those defence companies, other manufacturers et cetera—AI you''ve mentioned—then say, ''Actually, you know what, this is taking way too long. We''re going to go and have a chat with Stephen Kinnock, the Secretary of State for Wales''? And then, while you''re trying to work through this new relationship with him as well, could we then end up in a position where we''re going around the houses, everyone''s looking at each other and somebody''s looking for an answer?

 For me, this is about the jobs that are already here, protecting those. I completely agree in terms of having an ethical framework, as a trade unionist. I get all of that, I totally understand that. But, for me, this is about the companies that we already have here. Wales has more than 200 companies serving aerospace, defence, more than 50 organisations in maintenance, let alone all the colleges and all of the training et cetera, and the really good apprenticeship jobs that are here as well.

 You''ve talked about the global situation. You''ve talked about what is happening as far as NATO is concerned. Lots of discussions. Everyone talks about, ''How much money are we going to chuck in the pot? Why is this more important than actually building homes for people?'' I don''t think that it''s one or the other. I totally appreciate and understand where you''re trying to go with this. My biggest worry in and amongst all of this is that we slow things down. Because like you''re saying—you''re not saying that this is about telling people, ''Don''t come and invest in Wales.'' You''re not saying that defence manufacturing jobs are something that your Government would be against. Although defence, I don''t think, was mentioned in your programme for government. I don''t think that word was there. So, I was a bit confused, really, because I was looking through it and I was like, ''The word isn''t there.'' Based on what you''ve said today, there seem to be a lot of contradictions.

 So, for me, can you guarantee that, whatever you do, you''ll do in social partnership, the trade unions will be fully involved and engaged, and that this is about job security in the wider sense as well? We''re talking about good jobs across the whole of Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Enterprise, Connectivity and Energy: Welsh Defence Ethical Framework', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=13005&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.347254', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771035', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We will now move to the vote on the Reform UK debate, access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals. I call for a vote on the motion tabled in the name of Llŷr Powell. If the proposal is not agreed, we will vote on the amendments tabled to the motion. Open the vote. Close the vote. In favour 31, no abstentions and 57 against. The motion is, therefore, not agreed.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Kerry Ferguson', 'Deputy Presiding Officer', '12177', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Voting Time', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=18376&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', '', '', 'HMOs; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.598313', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777030', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 At the end of the summer, 1,954 homeless people, including 102 children, were living in temporary hostel accommodation. A further 2,539 people, including 268 children, were in hotels and bed and breakfasts. That is nearly 4,500 people in Wales without a proper home of their own, nearly 400 children without the security that a proper home provides, and in too many cases without a bedroom of their own.

 Those are not abstract figures, they are the families whose lives are put on hold because our social housing system is failing to meet their need. Housing is a finite resource—there are only so many homes available at any one time. In the midst of a housing crisis, it is the duty of this Senedd, of the Welsh Government, to decide fairly who should get priority. The position of Reform is plain: Welsh homes should go to Welsh people first, and social housing should serve people with the longest established link to Wales and the United Kingdom. But let me also be clear about what this means: EU nationals who have been granted settled status here have a lawful right to reside here. Reform respects that, and they would not be the target of this policy. Our concern is with a system that can put recent overseas arrivals ahead of the British and Welsh families who have lived, worked, paid taxes and raised their children here, only to find themselves in temporary accommodation or unable to secure a home in their own town. We must not allow subsidised social housing to become a further incentive for people to come here illegally from abroad. At a time when Welsh families are living in hostels, hotels and B&Bs, the first duty of Government must be to the people it is elected to serve.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Tom Montgomery', '', '12183', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=17680&l=en', NULL, 99.0, 'High', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', 'Sector bodies', '', 'HMOs; homeless; social housing; temporary accommodation', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.551778', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777016', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The Welsh Government has been clear that we need parity of powers with Scotland as a basic, in order to do the day job—something that others in this Chamber seem to struggle to understand. We need more borrowing powers in order to invest in services and infrastructure here; the devolution of policing and justice in order to align policy in order to deliver better outcomes; and basic fairness with powers over rail and the Crown Estate. We will no longer tolerate a third-rate devolution settlement. So, can the First Minister outline what discussions he''s having with the UK Government to make that clear?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Carrie Harper', '', '278', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the First Minister', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=3030&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'devolution settlement', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:58.319765', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772869', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, first off, welcome, Cabinet Minister and your officials. Would you be so kind as to introduce your colleagues?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '4. Scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning on the Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=7803&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister; LGHP Committee members', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.800051', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774382', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Within the first 100 days of this new Government, a public consultation was launched marking the start of the process for revising the framework and beginning a national conversation about the long-term future of Wales. Rather than consulting solely on planning matters, engagement work throughout the process will invite people to consider the long-term future of Wales and the role that places, communities, infrastructure, the environment, the Welsh language and culture should play in shaping our nation''s future to 2050.

 We are inviting the people of Wales to help shape the direction, priorities and scope of the new national development framework. This reflects our commitment to work with local authorities, regional partners and stakeholders across Wales to develop a framework that meets Wales''s needs, empowers local government, and reduces costs, bureaucracy and duplication. It also supports our ambition to move towards a community-centred approach to planning, giving communities a real say on how we secure the homes, the workplaces and infrastructure they need. The framework will play a key, overarching role in how those decisions are made locally, regionally and nationally.

 Importantly, the national conversation will also help co-ordination across a range of plans and strategies that are being taken forward during this Government term, as outlined in our programme for government that was published last week. This collaborative and co-ordinated approach to align national priorities will ensure more effective delivery of Government objectives.

 The decisions made through the planning system touch every aspect of daily life. They influence where homes are built, how people travel, where businesses invest, how energy is generated and distributed, and how we protect and enhance our natural environment. That is why it''s so important that the new national development framework is shaped by a broad range of voices, experiences and evidence. I want this to be an open, inclusive and meaningful national conversation. The national development framework belongs to everybody, considering its importance in providing a framework for new development across Wales. We welcome and encourage representations from everyone, and every group, institution, business and individual has a contribution to make in developing the national spatial plan.

 Alongside the opportunity to submit written representations and submit evidence to support the revision work, the current consultation includes a series of public and stakeholder events across Wales. They''ll be taking place in Cardiff tomorrow, on 30 September, Aberystwyth on 6 October and Llandudno on 23 October. They provide the opportunity for all, including local authorities, businesses, developers, infrastructure providers, environmental organisations, housing bodies, community groups, young people and members of the public to have their say and give their views.

 Evidence will also play a critical role throughout this process. Through this consultation, I’m inviting stakeholders to contribute information, research, analysis and insight on the opportunities and challenges that Wales is likely to face over the coming decades. This evidence will help inform the preparation of the revised framework, its overall vision, the options that will be considered and the policies contained within it.

 At its core, planning is about creating better places for people and the environment. Therefore, the revised national development framework must support sustainable economic growth, resilient communities, environmental stewardship and the long-term well-being of current and future generations. I therefore encourage everyone with an interest in the future of Wales to engage with this consultation and contribute to this important national conversation. Responses can be submitted online, by e-mail or by post until 20 November this year, but there will be more opportunities to engage shortly as well. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Sian Gwenllian', 'Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=7888&l=en', NULL, 121.0, 'High', 'digest', 'planning_system; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; local development plans; planning system; programme for government', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:58.919417', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772991', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We have not yet seen a pipeline of demands coming through for data centres. There are various rules, depending on what water is used for, as to who should pay for it. So, customers are required to pay for investment for water that''s going to be used domestically. The situation for paying for water that''s used non-domestically is much more complicated. It will ultimately depend on where policy ends up on who pays for water that''s needed for data centres. But we have not yet seen that pipeline of demand coming through. I imagine we will do, but we just haven''t yet.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=2454&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:22.049007', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773214', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The strategy sets out how we will use digital services, data and technology to help manage the rising demand, improve access to our services, make better use of our information and data, but also maintain secure and reliable systems. The investment requested in this year''s estimate supports a number of practical improvements. These include an image exchange portal that will provide a secure and more efficient way to share information with professional advisers. It will reduce the administration, improve the information security and governance around that sharing of information and help cases progress more quickly. I do understand again that that is in place in a number of other public services ombudsman offices around the UK, so that would be bringing us in line with that as well.

 SharePoint and knowledge information improvements—again, this is making our own organisational knowledge, guidance and information easier for our own staff to access, supporting, again, consistent decision making and reducing time spent on searching for information and data. Again, our staff survey results have shown that there''s been a reduction in confidence that staff see that as a reliable source of information. So, that went down from 81 per cent to 64 per cent in the last year.

 And then, a central repository. So, again, bringing together—. You know, a data repository, bringing together information not just from our own organisation, but other relevant open sources of information, to provide a more complete picture, which would allow us to pick out themes, demand and emerging issues across Welsh public services, again, which, in turn, would help us to support our decision making, strengthen the evidence-based improvement work that we''ve already talked about, and, again, try and identify those trends and pressures a bit earlier.

 And also in the estimate is some investment in enhancing, really, our cyber security and resilience, helping protect the sensitive data, obviously, that we hold within the organisation, maintaining those services that we currently have, reducing the risk of disruption and adding some additional capacity following internal and external reviews of that area.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Sarah King', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=10917&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.303412', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777680', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In Reform, our position has been clear from the outset. We have three simple red lines to ensure that we support a budget that actually puts Wales first. I''d like to first say that none of those red lines is directly copying what England are doing with the ALN funding, so I look forward to her apology for the comments she initially made regarding Reform''s position.

 The first red line is we called for international spending to be brought to an end, so that those millions of pounds could instead be invested here in Wales, as they always should have been. The Welsh Government budget was always intended to be spent in Wales.

 Secondly, we called for greater investment in our home-grown NHS staff. It is unacceptable that newly qualified Welsh nurses are completing their training only to find there are too few opportunities for them to work in the NHS here in Wales. We invested in training these professionals, yet this year, nearly 400 nurses and midwives could not find jobs in the NHS after initial job allocations. Coincidentally, the Welsh Government has hired at least 500 nurses and other medical professionals from India through the Wales in India initiative.

 Thirdly, we highlighted, as other parties have, the urgent need for greater support for the additional learning needs system. There is no excuse for not being aware of this strain, even before we all highlighted it to you. Concerns have been raised about these financial pressures from Audit Wales to local councils to school leaders.

 Unfortunately, on the first two priorities, the Government has failed to act. On additional learning needs, while the Government has acknowledged the issue, what has been offered to local councils is only a fraction of what is required for our schools. It falls well short of the level of support that school leaders have been calling for. It is clear that rather than attempting to fix the system, this is an offer designed to secure political support, treating disabled children like a political football, and I know my colleague will touch on this later.

 Llywydd, at the election, Reform became the largest opposition group in the history of the Senedd. That was not an accident. It reflected the desire of many people across Wales for a different approach and for different voices to be heard in Welsh politics. We were not elected to wave through Government budgets that, in our view, fail to deliver for the people of Wales. We were elected to hold the Government to account and to argue for policies that reflect the mandate we have received. The people who sent us here expect us to stand by our principles, and that is exactly what we''re doing today. The majority of people in Wales did not vote for Plaid''s manifesto, and the majority of the Members of the Senedd were not elected on its programme. The proportional representation system that Plaid helped to design was always going to result in a minority Government. You knew you needed to seek collaboration.

 From the very first sitting in this Senedd, Reform has made it clear that we are prepared to work constructively with Members from across the political spectrum where proposals genuinely benefit the people of Wales. That offer still remains on the table, but that offer requires collaboration, not the expectation that an opposition party will sheepishly support a budget that fails to deliver for their voters. And for these reasons, Llywydd, Reform will be voting against the supplementary budget.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Cai Parry-Jones', '', '12174', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=23691&l=en', NULL, 33.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Delivery bodies', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.554714', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767966', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 For individuals to have access to Llais and other organisations to assist them in shaping their complaint, bringing it forward at the right time, is really helpful to us, because we should then have fewer complaints that we can''t deal with, because a lot of that work has been done by Llais in advance. You make a good point: if they''re working with an organisation and people that they trust and have been talking to for a long time, they perhaps would be more accepting of the answer at the end of that process. But we work closely with Llais. Officers meet with them on a regular basis. 

 I hope that Llais, the advocates and the people that work there, know they can pick up the phone and talk to us and make sure they give the best advice possible to complainants. Some might never come to us, because Llais managed to deal with things in a different way, perhaps with the public body. But if things get to that point where they feel it does need to come to us, then Llais will support complainants to bring it, and it works well.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=9226&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.044461', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777556', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The possible actions are as follows. As this petition relates to an issue within the remit of the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee, our committee may wish to draw that committee''s attention to the petition, invite it to take the petitioners'' views into account when scrutinising the Cabinet Minister on climate change policies, thank the petitioner for raising this issue and close its consideration of the petition.

 Option 2: alternatively, the committee could consider undertaking an inquiry into the effects of climate-based policies on nature, farms and daily life in Wales, gathering the evidence to achieve a better understanding. It should be noted, however, that the climate change committee may take the view that, as its own remit explicitly covers climate change and environmental matters, the Petitions Committee would not be the appropriate committee to undertake such work.

 I would be opting for option 1 as a result of this. I think we all saw in summer, with the wildfires, the heat, that climate change is upon us, and I would go for option 1, personally. Up for discussion?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16259', '2026-09-24', 'Donna Cushing', '', '12168', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. New Petitions', '16259', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16259?startPos=2070&l=en', NULL, 80.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Consultation open', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; inquiry', 0, '2026-09-25T12:02:31.191546', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16259&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772266', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, I do think that there is more that could be done with regard to the welfare system. I have thought, for example, in the short term that there is more that could be done in that regard, in terms of ensuring that we can share data better, given that the DWP isn''t always able to share the data, or isn''t willing to share the data, that we need in order to use those data to ensure that what we provide under our powers does reach the pockets of people as quickly and as easily as possible—those people who are eligible for that support.

 So, I have emphasised that Wales needs to have a voice in these decisions, and that Wales''s priorities need to be reflected in full when the decisions are made. I''ve made that clear, for example, with Sir Stephen Timms, who, of course, as the committee is probably aware, is undertaking a review at present of the personal independence payments. We know that any change in that regard is going to have a disproportionate impact on Wales because of the number of people in Wales who receive those PIP payments, and disabled people. So, I have said clearly to that Minister that we need to ensure that the voice of Wales and the voice of disabled people in Wales who will be impacted by these reforms are heard more clearly. There has been a great deal of discontent expressed in that regard, and I support that discontent, and have expressed that.

 So, I do look forward to having that positive and productive dialogue with the UK Government on this, because it is a crucially important agenda. It''s something that has so much influence on the lives and the quality of life of people in Wales. So, I do think that we need to ensure that our stance is clear, that our voice is heard and that we work together, whilst urging the devolution of these powers, and that we ensure that we can do everything we can, such as being able to share data with the DWP, to ensure that what we provide can reach those families as easily and as quickly as possible.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=2105&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.799392', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771192', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 What we''ve been very keen to do is to ensure that we avoid redundancies and that we ensure that we have sufficient staff to deliver the work to finally clear that backlog. So we have held a number of posts vacant during the year, and we''ve managed the position whereby we have a number of temporary contracts in place so that we can smooth the journey from completion of backlog to a new steady state. We anticipate, as a result of the audit transition to beyond backlog, that there are around 20 posts in total that we will need to remove from the establishment. Obviously, that will be reflected in future budgets and estimates that this committee sees, but, of course, alongside that, there is a corresponding reduction in audit fee income. All of that needs to be worked through, and you''ll see that in the estimate and the fee scheme that comes to this committee in about a month or so''s time.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Kevin Thomas', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=1656&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies; Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.045905', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774128', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think it just leaves me to say ''thank you very much'', Cabinet Minister, and your team for coming here, assisting and giving evidence. We will break for lunch right now.

 Thank you again. We will, obviously, send you a transcript of today so that you can check its factual accuracy, which is quite telling given that we''re discussing AVR. But, again, thank you for your time. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '4. Scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning on the Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=11042&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister; LGHP Committee members', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:26.501724', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775034', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, the strategy might not necessarily be these certain policy directives or pieces of legislation for the country, but more around empowering those local authorities, resourcing them to be able to take that flexible approach to make it easy for people to adopt or to access the support that they need around tackling empty properties.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Dr Helen Taylor', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 1', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=1421&l=en', NULL, 155.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:24.981727', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774704', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

