 That''s taxpayer-funded accommodation.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'John Clark', '', '12169', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=16056&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', '', '', 'HMOs; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.402890', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776962', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I do understand the frustration that can be caused by long waits for housing and by poorly managed homes. The solution is to increase the supply of social housing, prevent homelessness, uphold standards and support local authorities, not to divide communities or to pit groups of people against each other. The queue for social housing is too long, but don''t create unnecessary division. Don''t pit people in need against each other. Rather, we should work together to create more social homes in Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Sian Gwenllian', 'Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=17233&l=en', NULL, 143.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'hmos_and_standards; housing_supply; homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Private rented sector', 'Housing minister', '', 'HMOs; homelessness; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.517366', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776983', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Our work of rebalancing the rates system will be delivered through changes to the existing differential multipliers, improving support for selected hospitality and leisure businesses. An expanded scope for the lower multiplier will be funded by a marginal increase to the higher multiplier, applicable to the highest value properties in the tax base across all sectors.

 From 2027-28, selected small to medium-sized hospitality and leisure properties will be subject to the lower multiplier currently applied to retail shops. This will bring eligible hospitality and leisure businesses in line with the permanent support introduced for retailers from this year. Eligible properties with rateable values below £51,000 will attract a 30 per cent reduction in their non-domestic rates bills.

 This permanent support will replace and exceed the temporary discretionary relief of 15 per cent for food and drink hospitality properties that is currently being provided. It will mean that a wider range of hospitality and leisure businesses will get the long-term certainty that they need to invest and to grow.

 The eligible food and drink hospitality properties are pubs, restaurants, cafes, bars, food courts, licensed clubs and live music venues. The eligible visitor accommodation properties are hotels, guest houses and hostels. And in the leisure sector, the eligible properties are cinemas, theatres, libraries, museums and gyms.

 These businesses play important and complementary roles in vibrant high streets and town centres. Along with retailers, ratepayers for around 20,000 properties will therefore benefit from the expanded lower multiplier. Over 12,500 more properties of the relevant types already attract full relief, and that means that they therefore do not pay any non-domestic rates. Ratepayers for a majority of properties across Wales, particularly small businesses, will also continue to benefit from a range of permanent reliefs currently worth more than £200 million per year.

 There is a balance to strike between the broader support that is provided through the lower multiplier and the resulting impact on the value of the higher multiplier. The higher multiplier will be set at just over 4 per cent above the standard multiplier from 2027-28 onwards. This is a small increase to the existing difference, and it''s a fair way to redistribute liability so that we can better support the businesses that are at the heart of our high streets and town centres.

 These changes to the differential multipliers are linked with the broader work of the town centres taskforce. This taskforce will take account of national policy frameworks, as well as considering the more tailored support that may be needed in individual town centres. Our approach recognises that needs will vary between places that may be facing different challenges that might go beyond the rate system.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Elin Jones', 'Cabinet Minister for Finance', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=9363&l=en', NULL, 99.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'non-domestic rates; visitor accommodation', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:25.991364', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769211', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 You will know—and I am straying into the territory of other Ministers here—that work is ongoing to look at planning policy, particularly in relation to energy infrastructure. There is also work happening on a new national energy strategy for us here in Wales. I will entrust that work to my Cabinet colleagues, and I am confident that they will get it right and also confident, of course, that all Members here in the Senedd will have an opportunity to scrutinise that when the time is right.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=3183&l=en', NULL, 27.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', '', '', 'planning authorities; planning policy; planning system', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:48.099821', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773574', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Drug and alcohol family courts offer a model of intensive support for families once court proceedings have been initiated. But in Wales, our Social Services and Well-being (Wales) Act 2014 already includes a similar model, namely the integrated family support service. This intervenes earlier by providing intensive support to families with multiple needs. This model has evolved and different forms of it can be found across Wales. I therefore intend to review this service and have an open discussion about which elements work well and what needs to work better. Clearly, there are some of those.

 We will also work with the UK Government to develop a method of solving problems in family courts. We have shown how we can drive change by introducing child-centred courts across Wales, and the data have shown that this is successful. This established a way of working that puts the child at the heart of the process and uses a problem-solving approach. That puts us in a good position to capitalise on that experience and learning in the future.

 I want to assure Members, Dirprwy Lywydd, that our family justice system will continue to focus on keeping children safe with their families wherever possible. Services will work together to prevent needs from escalating and to help families make sustainable changes. When families come into contact with the courts, we want a more empathetic experience that focuses on the best outcomes for the child.

 Of course, these responsibilities extend across different parts of Government and a contribution will be required from the judiciary and the UK Government. There will also be a need to capitalise on what has been learned from the drug and alcohol family courts model and from families with direct experience, and I''m finishing here. Although this is a complex change, I believe that our partners share the same enthusiasm that I''ve heard across the Chamber this afternoon to work together and move this work forward urgently. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Delyth Jewell', '', '5207', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Family Drug and Alcohol Courts', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=10749&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.960929', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776846', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

Legislative Consent: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill Legislative Consent: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings The Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill (the Bill) was introduced in the House of Commons on 14 May 2026. The long title to the Bill notes that it is “A Bill to make provision enabling the Secretary of State in certain circumstances to make regulations relating to the transfer of securities issued by, or property, rights and liabilities of, a steel undertaking; and for connected purposes.” The Bill is subject to the legislative consent process under Standing Order 29 . This is when the consent of the Welsh Parliament is sought by the UK Government to legislate on an issue in relation to Wales that has regard to devolved matters. The Legislative Consent Motion for the Bill was agreed in Plenary on 14 July 2026. Legislative Consent Memorandum – June 2026 On 23 June 2026, the Business Committee pre-emptively agreed (33KB) that the Legislation Committee and the Economy, Energy and Connectivity Committee should report to the Senedd by the morning of 14 July 2026. The Welsh Government laid a Legislative Consent Memorandum (PDF 248KB) on the Bill before the Senedd on 25 June 2026. The Legislation Committee laid its report (PDF183KB) on 14 July 2026. Business type: Legislative Consent First published: 25/06/2026 Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47487', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 63.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', '', 'Legislation Committee; Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:51.027445', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47487', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 There is strong consensus among many in this Chamber about what we could do and what we can work to deliver for the people of Wales. It is time we genuinely worked together collaboratively and co-operatively to deliver these. Additional healthcare funding and the shift towards primary care is a step in the right direction, as is the £20 million capital funding for social housing. The housing crisis is a health crisis, and we know, as does the Government, that collective solutions will be more cost-effective, helping those who need it most, where they need it most, in their homes. This is something we will expect to see prominently feature in the next budget.

 Likewise, noting the £1 bus fare provision, we want to see investment in public transport go further, with cleaner, integrated, sustainable travel putting money back into people''s pockets as well as addressing the urgent need to transition away from fossil fuels.

 On climate, I''d like now to move to the most glaring omission in this supplementary budget. There is a small, but welcome, uplift in both resource and capital allowances to develop and implement climate change policy, green growth and environmental protection. But this funding is apparently ring-fenced for the Welsh Government energy service, not to address the climate change mitigation and adaptation. The Met Office yesterday released data showing that more than 2,700 people are thought to have died from heat-related causes during the May and June heatwaves in England and Wales. Of those, almost half died as a result of the extra heat caused by human-induced warming. The authors of this report said:

 ''It''s time we woke up to the fact that we now live in a country with dangerously hot summers. To protect people during future extremes, we must urgently adapt''.

 Our summers are 3-4 degrees Celsius hotter than they would have been without human-induced climate change. Last month, 35.9 degrees Celsius was recorded at Bute Park in Cardiff, breaking the previous June record of 33.7 degrees Celsius, set in 2000. That week, children were sent home from school. As a country, we are incredibly underprepared. The UK Climate Change Committee''s recent climate risk assessment found that critical action was needed in Wales to address health risks from heat. It estimates that UK-wide heat deaths could increase from around 1,400 to between 3,000 and 10,000 in the 2050s without further adaptation. The health toll is rising and it''s costing lives.

 This week, as was mentioned earlier today, North Wales Fire and Rescue Service have been tackling a major incident with the outbreak of wildfires near Conwy mountain. Families are having to flee their homes to safety. This is a frightening situation for all involved, and our thoughts are with anyone impacted by this devastating situation. Having declared a climate emergency all those years ago, where is the action to treat the situation as the genuine crisis that it is?

 For the people of Wales, these issues cannot wait until the next budget, or the next one. The climate change action fund has not changed but the reality of the situation has. Where will the money come from to address this unfolding crisis? The Government must provide assurances that climate mitigation and adaptation will be given the necessary prominence it needs, going forward, and within the next budget.

 Finally, I''d like to say, as a Member of a party that never whips its Members in any Parliament, we will be voting to support this budget. I believe that to do otherwise would be an irresponsible dereliction of duty. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Anthony Slaughter', '', '5025', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=25725&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system', 'Sector bodies; Opposition housing leads', '', 'social housing; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.780409', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767972', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
Welsh Government urges Chancellor to speed up delivery of £14 billion rail pledge, as current funding covers just 1% of Network Rail''s spend across England and Wales. Cabinet Minister for Finance Elin Jones also presses UK Government to act on the cost of living, public spending and investment in Wales ahead of the Autumn Budget. Finance Minister: "Wales has waited long enough. This budget is the UK Government''s chance to show it''s serious about delivering for our communities."

Welsh Government urges Chancellor to speed up delivery of £14 billion rail pledge, as current funding covers just 1% of Network Rail''s spend across England and Wales. Cabinet Minister for Finance Elin Jones also presses UK Government to act on the cost of living, public spending and investment in Wales ahead of the Autumn Budget. Finance Minister: "Wales has waited long enough. This budget is the UK Government''s chance to show it''s serious about delivering for our communities."

The Chancellor is finalising the Autumn Budget, due to be delivered on 28 October. Ahead of the Budget, Elin Jones has written to the Chancellor as part of the Welsh Government''s commitment to stand up for Wales and demand fairness from Westminster, including a fair deal on funding, investment and opportunity.
The proposals include:
Rail: bringing forward £1 billion of the existing £14 billion rail investment commitment to accelerate improvement projects across Wales.
Cost of living: targeted support for struggling households, including unfreezing Local Housing Allowance rates to prevent people falling into homelessness and protecting Cynnal, Wales''s child payment, from tax and benefit deductions to ensure families benefit from some extra breathing space and to support our efforts to reduce child poverty.
Public spending: reversing real-terms cuts to Wales''s day-to-day and capital budgets.
UK Government investment: backing major projects including the decarbonisation of Pembrokeshire Power Station.
Cabinet Minister for Finance Elin Jones said:
"Wales has waited long enough. This budget is the UK Government''s chance to show it''s serious about delivering for our communities.
"Right now, just 1% of Network Rail''s spending across England and Wales is going towards our railways. That''s not good enough. The UK Government has committed to invest £14 billion to address the historical underfunding of Wales’ railways and I want to see that promise turned into action, starting with this budget.
"Families across Wales are still feeling the squeeze, and I want the Chancellor to go further to help them, whether that''s through housing and energy support or protecting the payments that matter most to families on the lowest incomes.
"Wales already makes a strong contribution to the UK economy, and investing in our future will help deliver the Prime Minister''s own ambition to spread prosperity and opportunity to every part of the UK. This budget is the moment to match that ambition with action."', 'https://media.service.gov.wales/news/uk-budget-must-deliver-on-its-rail-promises-to-wales', '2026-09-27', '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', 'Finance; Cabinet Minister for Finance - Elin Jones', '', '', NULL, 58.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'homelessness; welfare_and_support', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'homelessness; local housing allowance', 0, '2026-09-28T06:34:39.823351', 'newsroom:uk-budget-must-deliver-on-its-rail-promises-to-wales', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 You''re asking about progress. The money that we''ve already spent—you''ll know that there was £55 million in total allocated in the supplementary budget, which we have put to work, both in terms of expanding childcare, and also, with some capital grants, to enable that to happen. So, 78.9 per cent of all two-year-olds are now offered childcare. Over 13,000 new two-year-olds have been offered a childcare place. Over 9,900 new two-year-olds attended their funded childcare, and over 2,000 of those children attended funded childcare in a Welsh-medium setting. 

 And, as I said, we are now expecting, and I''ve had assurances from local authorities—. And many Members in the Chamber will probably see this happening now—their local authorities putting out that offer to parents of that age group. So, that''s happening progressively in the way that we said it would. And then, by the end of the financial year, all two-year-olds in Wales, in every single local authority, will be offered 12.5 hours of funded childcare. So, I think there''s quite a lot of statistics there for you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=1100&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'draft budget; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:20.193078', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773520', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

