 We can see that this petition has gathered a similar level of support. But I would point to the fact that less than 5,000 of the signatures on this have come from within Wales, although it has exceeded the 10,000 that is needed for that threshold for us to discuss whether to request another Plenary debate.

 There are lots of issues within here. For example, the petitioner, who is an expert on red squirrels and squirrelpox, is asking questions around the vaccine feasibility study that had been announced in 2025 and who was invited to bid for that work. He clearly has some unanswered questions around that as an expert himself in red squirrel conservation, and believes, very firmly, that the squirrelpox vaccine research is the one strand of work most likely to prove to be the saviour of red squirrels and prevent them from becoming extinct.

 So, the petitioner is specifically asking us to seek an explanation in answer to some questions here. For example, why it took two years for a vaccine feasibility procurement to be placed on Sell2Wales and why nothing has happened for more than a year since. He''s asking if the Minister can explain why officials appear to have not taken forward re-advertising the vaccine feasibility procurement, and he''s asking questions around when Natural Resources Wales requested that Welsh Government be invited to join meetings with UK Government agencies around the results of a new vaccine development study as well.

 So, although we could at this stage request a Plenary debate on the petition, there are a number of outstanding queries, really, for the Minister here; that seems very clear. So, I would suggest, at this stage, that the most appropriate course of action looks to be to write to the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability to highlight the petitioner''s latest comments and questions, seek a response to those, and then we could, at a later stage, decide whether we wish to refer it on to the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee, or if we feel that a debate in Plenary time is needed.

 So, my recommendation, just to underscore that, would be for us to write to the Minister now and pose those technical questions.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16259', '2026-09-24', 'Vikki Howells', '', '5107', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. New Petitions', '16259', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16259?startPos=1062&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T12:02:31.063154', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16259&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772197', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Going to my first question, I, like so many others in this Chamber, had the real misfortune, I''m afraid, of having to read through your Government''s programme for government. Now, whilst it''s jam-packed with ambition—no, it really was not a ''yay''—it was very light on detail, which I suppose is the Plaid Cymru way. Now, it created a lot of fanfare with catchy announcements, but it never actually explained how things were going to be delivered.

 Cabinet Minister, there are nearly half a million unpaid carers in Wales who look after their loved ones day in, day out, saving the Government billions—and I mean billions—of pounds. To put it into perspective, across the UK, it would cost £185 billion every single year if the state was to replace the care provided by unpaid carers with professional paid care support. Now, despite the mammoth contribution unpaid carers make, their work often goes unnoticed, with a lack of support on offer. It is clear this Government needs to be doing a lot more when it comes to helping unpaid carers, but it doesn''t look like that''s happening, especially after reading your programme for government. Cabinet Minister, can you please explain to me, and this Chamber, why there were more commitments in your programme for government to Euro 2026, independence and the Tour de France than there are to improving the lives of unpaid carers in Wales? And why have unpaid carers been deprioritised by this Government? Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Natasha Asghar', '', '336', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=3423&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.336291', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776635', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
