 I think it''s also worth—. It''s helpful to note that the regulations set the minimum floor, if you''d call it that, for activity, that all EROs will deliver each year. So, there could be more flexibility for EROs to carry out more than one data matching exercise in future years if they wanted to and if they felt that that was going to be important to deal with the particular circumstances. And there''s a range of factors that we think they will be taking into account when they consider whether one exercise or more than one exercise is necessary and useful. So, that might include whether they''ve got scheduled elections in that year and what types of elections those are, whether they have got significant issues with some of those other electoral administration contact points and processes, such as the annual canvass, and, as the committee has already pointed out, whether there are particular local demographic circumstances where we''ve identified significant under-registered groups, including student populations, and then particularly thinking about the timing of when that activity would happen in relation to those under-registered groups. So, there is still flexibility within that year to, (a), change the time of when the exercise would take place and, (b), for electoral registration officers to decide it would be helpful in a particular year to carry out more than one exercise. 

 I think there''s just a couple of other things to point out. One is that, in terms of the impact on accuracy and completeness of having one exercise rather than four, the automatic voter registration process is a complement not a replacement for the other registration processes that will continue. So, both the annual canvass that''s happening at the moment will continue to happen, and also the ability for electors to register immediately before a particular set of polls, using the online registration portal. So, automatic voter registration is there to supplement that, rather than to replace those.

 And, I think, even looking at whether we''re looking at one exercise or more than one exercise, even based on the findings from our evaluation, I think we''d flag that, even if there was only one exercise per year, it would still potentially be leading to an improvement in the completeness of the registers of somewhere between 2 per cent and 8 per cent, based on the experience from the pilot. So, that''s still an important and helpful improvement on the accuracy, compared with where we are at the moment.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Hawthorn', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=1637&l=en', NULL, 109.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Legislative commitment; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; explanatory memorandum', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.940193', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769837', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think the other thing with health is, the reason we—. Where we see cases that are particularly serious or where there has been personal harm or injustice or, sadly, sometimes avoidable—certainly avoidable harm or injustice—sometimes avoidable death, those are the reports that tend to be public interest reports. They are put into the public domain in full, along with the recommendations that we make to those health bodies for improvement. Those improvements are about avoiding that happening again and those public interest reports are about making sure that everybody, right across the sector, can learn from that, but that those who are charged with governance and holding those bodies to account are also aware of those things and what could be put right to try and avoid those things happening in the future.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=7151&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.724953', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777576', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 On child poverty targets, this has been a major issue in that we had 27 years of Labour-led Governments that did have targets initially, then they withdrew them. We will bring targets. We are developing a strategy. One of the reasons that we have called for a strategy is that the sector—people working in this area—wants targets. I''d rather work out what targets are meaningful rather than pluck one from the air. The work is being done on that now. In early summer 2027, we will have that strategy, which must include targets. We need to know what we''re aiming for.

 On independence, I compare the situation in Wales to Ireland, where the Good Friday agreement specifically says that a referendum will be called—. I''m not interfering with what happens in Ireland; they can do what they want to do. But it specifically says when public opinion is clear that there is support for it, then you ask the question. In fact, the UK Prime Minister suggested in the House of Commons last week that the same should apply to Scotland too. It''s opinion polling. It''s sensing the view of the population. I want to influence people in a positive way, and you too, to engage positively with the possibilities that come from pursuing a particular kind of constitutional change.

 But remember, we work with what we have now. We have real ambition for our nation. I want us to embrace all the possibilities of being a normal nation, but we''re a Government that works right now with the powers that we have now, looking at the powers that we could have next, and next and next, because it''s all about building a nation, which is something that all of us should be interested in.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', '', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=11722&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.191679', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771023', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, what does this mean in practice? It means prioritising the areas where we can have the greatest impact. A health and care service that is fit for the future is something that every Welsh citizen needs and deserves. This not only means taking immediate action to bring down waiting lists, which remain unacceptably high, but also ensuring that our most precious public service is sustainable and resilient for the future.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=8560&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.673277', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770971', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 For Dŵr Cymru in particular, we have investigated it on more than one occasion in the last few years for the misreporting of some data, for example. We fined it £40 million in March of 2024 for doing that. And also, as one of 11 water and waste water companies in England and Wales, as part of our whole sector-wide investigation into whether companies were maintaining their sewage treatment works and their waste water networks as effectively as they should, we fined Welsh Water £44 million for that. And crucially, Dŵr Cymru offered undertakings in lieu of a penalty, so we were able to direct where that money was going to be spent to fix those problems. So, those are two examples of how we continue to hold the company to account, as well as to support it, using the extra presence that we have in Wales.

 We are also part of a Welsh forum for regulation, which is unique here. There was a forum for PR24, and we are part of the same forum that is designing what Wales-specific regulation for PR29, which will occur before the establishment, the commencement, of the independent regulator for Wales in 2032, will look like. And I think those are the main ways in which we continue to support and keep a grip on economic regulation in Wales as we ensure a smooth transition to the new regulator.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=496&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:21.806161', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773201', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, we undertook a very swift statutory review within the first 100 days. That review included survivors, people who provide expert services, partners, of course, who deliver elements of the strategy on our behalf, and, of course, the national adviser, Jo Robinson. So, the findings of that review will now feed into the new national strategy.

 So, on Monday, as it happens, I discussed this in the Cabinet. I presented a paper to the Cabinet about the strategy and what the timescale will be in terms of the new strategy, and I emphasised there the importance of this being seen and understood across every portfolio in order to bring VAWDASV to an end. So, they share the ambition, the entire Government shares the ambition, that this has to be a whole-system and cross-governmental approach. And I have had two very positive and beneficial meetings since entering this role with the national adviser, Jo Robinson, and we have been discussing what needs to be done.

 So, in terms of what we''ve already seen being done—and as I said, we''re still consulting on this and thinking about how this will take shape in the next strategy—I think that one of the gaps that we need to consider in terms of the new strategy is online harm. This is something that is becoming increasingly prominent and is something that, perhaps—. It''s not new, but it is something that continues to develop with artificial intelligence and so on. [ Inaudible .] Yes.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=5117&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.086438', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771321', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In relation to things like your planning powers, mostly urgent works notices—section 215 notices—under a lot of our legislation, like the housing Act, the building Act, the prevention of damage Act, the local authority charge takes priority over the mortgage. So, when we enforce the sale of a property, we get our money first—legal costs and auctioneers. But, in relation to the planning legislation, our charge doesn''t take priority. So, if a property is in negative equity or it is highly leveraged, it is really quite high risk to ever do works in default in those scenarios. So, prioritising the charges under the different legislation could be helpful.

 Uprating some of the offences: if you look at section 215, in Wales it''s a £1,000 fine and then one tenth for the second prosecution again. In England, they have introduced unlimited fines and then £250 to £500 for a second or subsequent prosecution. So, that could be dealt with similarly under section 79.

 So, there is a whole host of fairly straightforward measures that could be introduced that would reduce the risk to the authority but actually give greater security on ensuring that the authority will recover the money and give them comfort. In addition, the Welsh Government has given an enforcement fund, which enables the authorities to pull down money and underwrite the risks to them. So, they have got all the tools, but there is always a reluctance to take enforcement action on the potential of the ability to not recover your money or, possibly, prosecutions as well.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Andrew Lavender', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 1', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=2728&l=en', NULL, 270.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; planning_system; homelessness; local_government_enforcement; committee_scrutiny; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Housing system; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Housing (Wales) Act; Housing (Wales) Act 2014; Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty homes; empty properties; enforcement action; evidence session; inquiry; planning legislation', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.191396', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774945', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In Gwynedd alone, we have over 2,000 local people in need of social housing, often waiting for over two years. This motion will simply ensure people who already belong to this country have priority. Reform has been clear. Foreign nationals should not be in social housing. The order of priority should be British families with children, British veterans, British workers. This motion simply asks the Government to stop pretending that eligibility is somebody else''s problem, even as constituents are pushed aside.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'John Clark', '', '12169', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=16106&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', '', '', 'HMOs; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.409511', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776964', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Just in terms of the issues that our evaluation highlighted and the recommendations that we made to Welsh Government and to electoral registration officers, many of those have already been addressed in these regulations, which, again, is very welcome, for example the change to the minimum frequency of the AVR process, the shorter notification period and, particularly, the requirement for the notification to be sent in hard copy by post and also to be accompanied by the invitation to register for the UK parliamentary register. So, some important and welcome changes to the process as a result of the evaluation. But we do think, flowing on from the experience of the pilots last year, there are some outstanding challenges around implementation, and we''ve highlighted those in our written evidence and are aware that other organisations have also highlighted these points.

 So, there are three particular points that we think are worth bearing in mind. I think, first, it''s clear that we have some increasingly challenging timescales in terms of when the regulations will come into force, and then the subsequent activity that registration officers will need to make sure takes place so that electors can get registered and added to the register automatically in time for the local government elections next May. And those timescales are going to be placing a lot of pressure on electoral services teams at a time when they are already delivering the annual canvass this year and when they should be beginning their preparations for the elections next year. The second area that I think we want to highlight is around the electoral management software systems that registration officers use and the functionality that''s going to be needed—the new functionality that''s going to be needed to deliver automatic registration. We still have some uncertainty about when those updates to those systems will be available and ready to support the delivery of automatic registration. And then the final point is possibly a longer term point as well, which is how best to ensure greater consistency across Wales in terms of the data sources that the ROs are able to access for the purpose of identifying new electors.

 So, I think, for this year, these kinds of challenges will undoubtedly make it harder to deliver automatic voter registration. But as I said, this is an important first step in the right direction and it also provides a process that can continue to be refined and improved over the next few years, as we build towards the next set of Welsh elections in 2030 and 2032.

 And we also just would like to, I suppose, highlight that it would certainly be something of a missed opportunity if we couldn''t get to a point where AVR could be rolled out ahead of next year''s local elections, because it would mean that voters would not see the benefit in real terms in being able to participate in elections until the next Senedd elections in 2030 at the very earliest.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Hawthorn', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=189&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; written evidence', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.707822', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769809', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 What has happened over the years though? Well, social housing has been remediated using public money, up to 100 per cent of eligible costs, through the Welsh building safety fund. At Celestia in Cardiff Bay, the Welsh Government funded cladding replacement on the social housing block to the tune of nearly £2 million. The other seven blocks at Celestia, including private leaseholders, remain untouched. Same development, same defects, but years waiting. Let me be clear, I do not believe that taxpayers should be picking up more of the bill—exactly what you''ve said in your statement—for the private developers. Those who have built these unsafe homes and profited from them must pay to put them right. But that cannot become an excuse for private leaseholders to wait years longer than their neighbours in social housing. The Government found the urgency to fix the social side. It must now find the same urgency on the private side.

 Plaid Cymru claim they stand for fairness, so I ask the Minister: does she accept that this two-tier approach, faster action for social housing and barely there action for the private leaseholders, has been a failure of fair treatment by the Welsh Government? Just take Meridian Quay in my constituency, for example. Phil Lake, a resident, took an insurance giant to the High Court on behalf of residents. Their claim was worth more than £25 million. Only now, seven years after Mr Lake stepped in, is remediation expected to start, and will be paid through the financial services compensation scheme, not by this Government.

 Today, in your statement, you announced a Wales interim measures alarm grant. Any help for leaseholders is really welcome. But, in England, the funding for these alarms began in December 2020. Wales is almost six years behind. And we''ve been given no budget, no eligibility criteria and no opening date in this statement. So, will leaseholders who have already paid the price for alarms get anything back, because in my constituency, and I''m sure of many of us sitting here today, people are paying around £500 a month for these alarms, which is more than some people''s mortgages?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Francesca O''Brien', '', '12190', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: Building Safety Programme Update', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=16878&l=en', NULL, 115.5, 'High', 'digest', 'building_safety_and_leasehold; social_housing', 'Housing system; Property & energy', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', '', 'building safety; cladding; leaseholders; social housing', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.818125', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771105', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I understand your point in terms of providing grants for the interim alarms, and that that perhaps is a sign that things are going to slow down. However, what I know can happen is that these alarms can be installed as a temporary measure, but there is a way of adapting them so that they can remain there permanently. So, that is a sign that things can move forward, because those alarms can remain there after the work is completed.

 Just to remind you, in closing, Dirprwy Lywydd, the requirement of the previous Government in the agreement with developers was that work was to be started in every building by the end of this year. That was the expectation. Perhaps that expectation hasn''t been made clear enough to developers—I think it probably was—by the previous Government, but this Government is determined that the developers will take their work completely and entirely seriously.

 I look forward to returning here to the Senedd, not to talk to you about the problems, but to tell a positive story about what''s happened in Wales with the building safety programme. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: Building Safety Programme Update', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=19008&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'building_safety_and_leasehold', 'Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'building safety', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:21.095617', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771102', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 And I think the other point, in relation to people in that kind of situation, is around the—. We''ve spoken already about anonymous registration, that that can happen once—. If somebody raises that, it just stops the clock for the 28 days, so it doesn''t mean that they have to produce the evidence and so on within that 28-day period. And also, they can do it after they''ve registered. You can then go on and apply for anonymous registration.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Michael Kay', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '4. Scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning on the Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=9392&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:26.118938', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774410', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In terms of your important question on the accountability of developers in this whole area of building safety, I did meet them all, all of the developers who have signed the Welsh Government contract. I met them a fortnight ago. I was entirely clear with them in that meeting that we needed to accelerate the remediation work. I warned them at that meeting that I would provide a progress report to you here in the Senedd in the new year. I look forward to receiving that progress report. I will be more than willing to name any developers who aren''t acting under their duties to remediate in accordance with the expectation of the contract, and the expectation that the work should start before the end of this year. If they haven''t reached that position, then I will have to name people in this Chamber.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=5259&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'building_safety_and_leasehold', 'Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'building safety; leaseholders', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.495032', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776707', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We are clear this is not enough, but this is not our view alone. As has been said before, Welsh councils are united on how the pressures from ALN in schools are real and urgent. Many of us will have received correspondence from school leaders, outlining the immediate difference extra real funding could make, and we are all aware of the concerns felt by teaching unions. Extraordinarily, overnight, the two teaching unions that represent school leaders, the NAHT and ASCL, have written jointly to Welsh Government to state that they are now in formal dispute with the Government over the issue of pay and ALN funding. These aren''t militant unions. They''re not affiliated with the Labour Party. In fact, one of them used to be led by the education Minister.

 So, how has it come to this, just 64 days into a new Plaid Cymru Government, where two large, well-respected unions are having to ask for the intervention of an independent conciliation body to mediate its dealing with Welsh Government? It''s unprecedented. It''s a failure of social partnership, and it says very clearly that the supplementary budget, as proposed, isn''t good enough. Not our assessment; the blunt assessment of teachers, school leaders and their representatives.

 I heard the Trefnydd''s response to Lynne Neagle earlier in the business statement. It sounded to me very much like the response of a Government that''s stopped listening. Let me spell it out. Forty million pounds in one year is a bad deal. Forty million pounds in the second year, when it''s been eroded by inflation and demand growth, is a bad deal. Forty million pounds in the third year, when £200 million of further recurring consequential become available from ALN spend in England, is a ridiculous deal. No financially literate party could sign up to it.

 Llywydd, this Senedd Labour group is returning to our party''s foundational mission, being the political representation of organised labour in the nation and being the voice of the most vulnerable in our society. You will understand, therefore, given the fundamental and formal concerns that unions have regarding these proposals, how we simply cannot support the supplementary budget as it currently stands. There are some on the Plaid benches that are avowed socialists. Here is an early test for you: will you blindly follow your party''s whip, or will you vote with our pupils and our teachers?

 And let''s also be clear, Llywydd—[ Interruption .] Let''s also be clear—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=24002&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.584289', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767877', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 ''we must avoid creating additional spending obligations where the funding position has yet to be agreed.''

 Before seeking more policing powers, your Government should demonstrate that it can deliver with the powers it already holds. Education is devolved, yet Wales has the worst Programme for International Student Assessment scores in the United Kingdom.

 Your Government is also struggling with other existing responsibilities, such as cutting NHS waiting lists. Your own statistics show that the number of pathways waiting for treatment increased between June and July, with two-year waits also rising. Those are delivery problems under responsibilities you already hold. And yet, with all these current issues the Government is struggling with, surprisingly, you seek additional devolved powers for policing. Money doesn''t grow on trees, although our previous Welsh Government seemed to test that theory in Uganda. Given your record of failings, what evidence do you have that this Government can deliver if policing is devolved? Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16313', '2026-10-06', 'Steven Rodaway', '', '12191', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16313', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16313?startPos=6003&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-10-09T13:35:10.015980', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16313&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775984', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
Meeting status: Confirmed
This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=986&MId=16302', '2026-10-05', '', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '', '16302', '', '2026-10-05', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Legislation Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-11T07:47:52.289917', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, I think it reflects the significant investment that we''ve made over the last few years, in large part, actually, thanks to your predecessor committee, who provided us with additional funding to support this. And we have invested a lot in our audit development and guidance team. So, we have employed an additional member of staff in that team, to help us in terms of making sure that our staff receive the guidance, the training that they need. That''s really important, because people are huge in that quality space, as you''ll be aware. We also invest significantly, as you''ll be aware, in our trainee scheme. And we bring in, every year, 12 to 15 graduates, and people straight from school, to train them through the associate chartered accountant qualification. And we really do invest in those individuals. 

 We''ve invested in technology and tools. In our performance audit work, we''ve adopted INTOSAI—International Organization of Supreme Audit Institutions—standards as well, which are internationally recognised standards for performance auditing. We have worked very closely with the Chartered Institute of Public Finance and Accountancy, the accountancy body, to support the new performance audit qualification. We work closely with the institutes themselves, we have very good relations with the accounting bodies, and with the other PAF bodies, the public audit forum bodies. We worked very closely, for example, with the Northern Ireland Audit Office to bring in a new methodology and write a new audit manual, following the introduction of the new auditing standard, international standard on auditing 315, a couple of years ago.

 We''ve set up technical networks, so, hopefully, you''re getting the picture that we have invested significantly in this space. For me, quality—and I know for Catherine too—is absolutely everything. Our reputation, everything, rests on the quality of our work. And so it''s an area that we absolutely have to have at the forefront of our priorities.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Ann-Marie Harkin', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=828&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:50.871197', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774525', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, yes, there is definitely learning and improvements that could be made. Part of that is about ensuring that the specification for the electoral management software systems is really clear and it meets the needs of registration officers. That specification is being finalised now; it hasn''t yet been confirmed and released, so there is still a little bit of uncertainty about what that process will involve. I think, again, it''s fair to say that, if it had been clear earlier in the development process for this year, then that would have relieved some of the concerns that electoral services teams probably are feeling about the process at the moment.

 The other thing that Welsh Government are very helpfully doing at the moment is providing EROs with data impact assessments and template data sharing agreements that they can use to help get in data from the local data holding departments. That should also be really helpful in getting some consistency.

 Registration officers already do have some experience of running data matching exercises every year. They''re running a national data match exercise using UK Government data ahead of the canvas, and they''re also able to supplement that with local data matching as well. So, they do have experience of these processes in general terms; what they don''t have necessarily is the experience of exactly what this system will provide and will require. So, yes, I think this year, the first year, is going to be more challenging and, in future years, there will be opportunities to refine and improve that process, absolutely.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Hawthorn', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=2174&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.023876', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769849', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 My last question, and this picks up on the rural-proofing Bill, which, as I read it, at early days, seems to be a catch-all for quite a lot of different things that Welsh Government officials have been wanting to do for some time, and then the statutory duty around ensuring that rural needs are considered. Candidly, I''m also concerned about urban needs, and when I look at certain protected characteristics, I would probably argue that there are a higher proportion of people who have those characteristics living in urban areas as opposed to rural areas. So, I guess my question is: how can we avoid the danger that this legislation creates a two-tier system, where certain parts of Wales are given stronger rights than others?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=2248&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.457725', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770440', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The recommendations in the Independent Water Commission report that the UK Government and the Welsh Government have committed to implementing has a taxonomy for how that sort of supervisory regulation works in practice, which is based on the taxonomy that''s used in financial services. It will be the responsibility of the new regulator to implement that supervisory regulation in a way that results in it, firstly, staying independent and not being captured and, secondly, not running the company itself.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=3056&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:22.134675', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773474', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The board as well, as you heard when we came here a fortnight ago, is starting to put more of an emphasis on the impact of our work. In order to drive that work forward with energy, we felt it was important to have somebody at the senior team level to lead on that work and to give it the necessary attention and energy, and to transmit the message that that is important to us as an organisation.

 There has been some change in the responsibilities of the senior team members in order to bring some of the elements of that work together. Derwyn Owen, who you met the last time that we were here, now leads on that work, but he''s also responsible for a significant proportion of the work that Anne-Louise delivered previously. We think that this allows us as an organisation to evolve and adapt on an ongoing basis so that we can ensure the best value possible for the public in Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Dr Ian Rees', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=1465&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.014174', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774120', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I do emphasise that I think it''s important to be entirely transparent and, therefore, I have said that only homes that will remain as social homes for communities and families will be counted as part of that 20,000 target. Now, what exactly the percentage will be between new builds and acquisitions in terms of vacant properties and so on, I can''t give you an exact figure on that. But, also in that statement made on Monday, I have spoken of community-led housing and I would like to include housing that''s created through community groups—through the community right to buy, for example—and that we also include those within the target because they do provide a solution that is in keeping with those communities in rural areas and where the Welsh language prospers.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4546&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'community right to buy; right to buy; social homes', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.129226', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768122', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 And that close monitoring is happening. My officials speak to the developers on an ongoing basis and are trying to help them remove some of the barriers that are in place. But, at the end of the day, they do have to take their responsibilities seriously, and I do hope to see progress by the end of the year. That is what was in the previous Government''s contract. They expected to see the work started by all developers by the end of this year. We are a long way from that point, but that is the expectation I am placing on them, and, if that does not happen, as I have explained, I will then have to take further steps, and I will start to talk specifically about problems in this Chamber, because I am accountable to you in that regard. 

 In terms of the grant, I am pleased that you have welcomed that. This can be put in place very quickly, and we can get it out of the door as soon as possible. This morning I was speaking to a representative from Swansea Point in Swansea and he mentioned that there are around 240 buildings that he represents, and that those developments could benefit specifically from the grant. In Sand Wharf, there are 100 units that could benefit from this. 

 The criteria will be made available very soon so that people can scrutinise those, but certainly the grant won''t pay for waking watch costs, because those have been significant. We are now moving to grants for the alarms, and I do very much hope that those alarms can be permanent. You can put in temporary alarms that can be adapted for when the work has been completed and retained. So, hopefully that explains a little more on that.

 Your point on social housing and the clear desire of this Government to increase the supply of social housing is entirely appropriate. Of course, safety standards have to be maintained. We are talking about community housing and social housing that is high quality, low carbon and safe. That is crucially important. It''s part of the ambition of this Government that people in Wales should be able to access a safe and affordable home for the long term.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: Building Safety Programme Update', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=18363&l=en', NULL, 88.0, 'High', 'digest', 'building_safety_and_leasehold; social_housing', 'Housing system; Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'building safety; leaseholders; social housing', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:21.003174', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771095', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Beyond the funding for ALN services, the supplementary budget also ensures additional investment for expanding free school meals and improving the condition of our schools. I am sure that everyone across the Chamber shares the same desire to see pupils with full stomachs learning in schools that are fit for purpose. But, by voting against the budget today, we are preventing that investment from reaching our schools as soon as possible, and it is the pupils and teachers who will miss out.

 Llywydd, it is not just the additional direct funding that is essential for the sustainability of our local authorities. The additional investment of £145 million in the health service takes pressure off care services. The £55 million for childcare creates local jobs and helps parents back into work in order to strengthen our local economy. And the £8 million for the bus travel scheme for young people improves connectivity and enables our young people to socialise and make use of local resources.

 In conclusion, Llywydd, the vote before us tonight asks a relatively simple question, really: do we want to see a sustainable health service? Do we want to give every child across Wales the best start? And do we want to support all pupils to realise their potential and all teachers to enjoy their work? We can only do that by passing the budget before us, and I encourage all Members to support it. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elfed Williams', '', '12165', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=24654&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.658942', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767904', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 As we''ve heard, the supplementary budget that the committee scrutinised included £246 million of unallocated resource funding, which the Cabinet Minister said reflects substantial in-year pressures, particularly within the NHS. We recognise that there is always some uncertainty at this stage of the financial year and that Governments need a degree of flexibility to respond to emerging pressures, but the level of unallocated funding is considerably higher than in recent supplementary budgets. The committee noted that the average over the last four years has been £62 million and it''s currently around four times higher than the average of the last four years. We want to better understand what pressures remain across Government and how those pressures differ from the normal challenges of managing a budget through the financial year.

 Turning to the management of NHS finances, this supplementary budget includes a £100 million allocation to tackle NHS waiting lists. The committee supports the Government''s ambition to move beyond short-term interventions and deliver sustained improvement in NHS performance. And we heard the Cabinet Minister wants to focus on longer term outcomes rather than short-term monthly performance measures. We heard that the NHS is currently facing financial pressures of more than £300 million this year and that discussions with health boards are ongoing before further funding decisions are taken. However, what we did not hear in sufficient detail is how the approach of this Government differs from previous approaches that have also relied on significant in-year NHS allocation. That is why we asked the Cabinet Minister to detail the accountability mechanisms and measures in place to ensure the £100 million for reductions in waiting lists is used effectively, and to explain the practical differences between the previous approaches to NHS funding and the new Government''s approach. We want to understand how these investments will drive sustainable improvement rather than short-term recovery measures.

 Moving on to the allocation of funding arising from the one-off special educational needs and disabilities consequential. Firstly, we recognise that the principle that Barnett consequentials do not have to be spent in the same way as funding allocated in England is an important principle. We also recognise the very real pressures facing additional learning needs services across Wales in the here and now. The Cabinet Minister told us that this one-off consequential has been absorbed into wider budget management. That inevitably raises questions about sustainability in other areas. We therefore ask her to explain how allocations enabled by this one-off consequential will be managed in future years, when that funding is no longer available. The Cabinet Minister told us the Government wants to work with local government and develop a more sustainable model for additional learning needs provision, recognising the significant pressure in the system. That is why we''re seeking greater clarity on both the scale of the challenge and the Government''s plan to manage these pressures.

 The committee noted the Government''s plans to expand funded childcare. The supplementary budget includes £55 million to accelerate the roll-out of funded childcare for two-year-olds and to support the wider expansion of the childcare offer. We asked the Cabinet Minister to confirm how many places the £55 million allocation will support, what outcomes are expected from the funding and how those outcomes will be monitored and reported.

 We will now turn to capital budgets. The Government has chosen to allocate virtually all available capital funding at this stage of the year. We understand the rationale and recognise the benefits of allocating capital funding early to facilitate delivery and support economic activity, however, that approach also leaves limited flexibility should circumstances change. We''re particularly concerned by reports that UK Government decisions associated with defence investment could have implications for Welsh capital budgets. We asked the Cabinet Minister to provide an assessment of the implications of the UK Government''s defence investment plan and any consequences for Welsh Government capital budgets.

 Too often, debate focuses solely on the amounts of money being spent; the Finance Committee understands that what matters most is what that money delivers. We look forward to the Cabinet Minister''s response and to continue our scrutiny of these issues as the financial year progresses. Diolch, Lywydd.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Sam Rowlands', '', '8734', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=23424&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Finance Committee; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.526566', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767956', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 And then, going back to some of our recommendations, a lot of the recommendations in our evaluation were focused around this issue. So, for example, we recommended that the notification letter should have to be sent in hard copy rather than by e-mail, for various reasons, but one of which was that a hard copy notification is more likely to be noticed, less likely to be missed. Also, the regulations themselves have a few prescribed things that the notification must say. So, it must have information on the ability to opt out. It must be have information on the ability to apply for anonymous registration, if applicable. And it also has to specify the final date of the day of the notice. So, that''s all a kind of good level of mitigation from our perspective. 

 And then, in terms of engagement with groups—I think we mentioned this in our written evidence—we understand the Welsh Government is considering or planning some form of engagement with relevant stakeholders on anonymous registration and communicating the changes. From an Electoral Commission perspective, we''ll be updating information on our websites around registration, if the regulations are passed, to reflect automatic registration as a kind of new system in Wales. And, as part of that, we''ll include information highlighting that there options to opt out of the process, options around anonymous registration. 

 I think the only other thing I''d just add, if that''s okay, is, from the feedback of the authorities running pilots—and, obviously, we''ll want to look at this again as part of a national roll-out, and satisfy ourselves that it''s a similar kind of picture—across the pilots, where potential electors did contact the ERO wanting to opt out, it was a very, very small number. So, it was between 0.2 per cent, I think, in Newport—. It was 0.2 per cent in Gwynedd, sorry, and 4 per cent in Newport, and then Powys somewhere in between. So, really relatively small numbers, and I think a very common reason—possibly the most common reason—was parents checking if it was correct that 14 and 15-year-olds should be added to the register as an attainer. So, in that case, following an explanation from the electoral services team, that that is in fact the case and that it would be the same process for an attainer if they registered as an attainer in the normal manner, that query was resolved. Others chose to opt out. But, yes, a small number of queries from people as well who didn''t want to appear on the register for safety reasons, and they were concerned by the notification that they were about to be registered. So, in that situation, electoral services teams can, obviously, highlight the option of anonymous registration, but, from the experience of the pilots, a lot of people weren''t satisfied by the process or the option of anonymous registration either and would prefer to avoid registering entirely.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Davies', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=937&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; written evidence', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.828907', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769805', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 As well as the timing of that, the settlement typically is only a single year, which again makes it more difficult for councils to take those decisions over the medium to longer term. That is in part, as is included in the report, down to how the Welsh Government receives its budget allocation from the UK Government. But, again, there''s a section in the report that talks about the importance of scenario planning and making assumptions around funding to help plan over the medium to longer term. That applies just as much to the Welsh Government in the report as it does to local councils as well.

 Turning then to grants, again, with grants, the timing of those being announced quite often can be late in the year. There''s a chart in the report that shows that there''s a significant uptake in March of each financial year, where funding leaves Welsh Government at over double the comparative rate for the rest of the year, which shows that funding is leaving the Welsh Government and going to local government to ensure it''s being used within the financial year. That makes it very difficult for councils to plan that spending. Ultimately, to achieve the best impact, they either need to have an off-the-shelf project ready to go or they need to have lots of people to turn that around. And what we found is that that is not always the case, particularly in some councils that feel that, due to their size, they may not have the capacity to have an off-the-shelf or the people ready to work through that funding.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16268', '2026-09-28', 'Charles Rigby', '', '', '', '', 'Public Accounts and Public Administration Committee', '4. Audit Wales brief: Welsh Government arrangements for funding local government', '16268', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16268?startPos=2869&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'Delivery bodies', '', 'council tax', 0, '2026-09-30T12:51:38.069607', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16268&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772726', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
Welsh Government to host finance ministers from across the UK in Cardiff Cabinet Minister for Finance Elin Jones to press UK Government on fiscal reform, including a formal review of the 2016 Wales Fiscal Framework. Finance Minister: "The way our funding is calculated hasn''t kept up with the real needs of people here. It''s outdated, and it''s not transparent enough. That must change."

Welsh Government to host finance ministers from across the UK in Cardiff Cabinet Minister for Finance Elin Jones to press UK Government on fiscal reform, including a formal review of the 2016 Wales Fiscal Framework. Finance Minister: "The way our funding is calculated hasn''t kept up with the real needs of people here. It''s outdated, and it''s not transparent enough. That must change."

Wales will host finance ministers from across the UK in Cardiff today (Thursday 17 September) when the Cabinet Minister for Finance will make the case for a funding deal from Westminster that reflects what people in Wales really need.
The meeting, known as the Finance Inter-ministerial Standing Committee (FISC), brings together finance ministers from the UK Government, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland to discuss financial and economic issues faced in all corners of the United Kingdom.
A one-to-one meeting between the Finance Minister and the Chief Secretary to the Treasury will also be held as an opportunity to discuss issues specific to Wales.
The Cabinet Minister for Finance will raise several priorities for Wales to deliver fiscal reform, including:
A formal review of the 2016 Wales Fiscal Framework – the agreement that sets out the Welsh Government''s funding arrangements including its tax powers and fiscal management tools such as borrowing - to make sure Wales'' funding arrangements are fit for the future.
Stronger borrowing powers and more flexibility over reserves, so Wales can plan and invest for the long term.
Improvements to, and reform of, the Barnett formula - the decades-old system used to determine how much money Wales gets from the UK Government.
Delivering the £14bn commitment by UK Government for investment in rail in Wales, to address years of underfunding compared with other parts of the UK.
Further UK-wide action on the cost of living, to support households across Wales.
Increased capital spending for Wales in the UK Government''s upcoming Autumn Budget to drive economic growth and invest in our infrastructure including hospitals and affordable housing.
Funding to address the legacy of our industrial past, including through the restoration of coal tips and the remediation of contaminated land.
Cabinet Minister for Finance Elin Jones said:
"I''m pleased to be able to meet with my counterparts from across the UK in what will be an important chance to work constructively together on the challenges facing all our nations.
"I''ll also be clear about what Wales needs. The way our funding is calculated hasn''t kept up with the real needs of people here. It''s outdated, and it''s not transparent enough. That must change.
"We''re up against a genuinely challenging financial backdrop – so what we''re asking for isn''t about special treatment for Wales but for a system that''s fair, that''s clear, and one that reflects the needs of our communities.
"That''s why today I can confirm the Welsh Government has formally proposed a joint review of the Wales Fiscal Framework, which I''ll be discussing in a one-to-one meeting with the Chief Secretary to the Treasury.
"This Welsh Government set out our expectations on day one that Wales both needs and deserves a fairer settlement on funding and powers.
"Throughout the day, I''ll be pressing the case for greater investment in Welsh rail, and for capital spending and cost of living support to be prioritised in the Autumn Budget.
“Wales and all its communities deserve fairness and equality."', 'https://media.service.gov.wales/news/welsh-finance-minister-to-press-uk-government-for-fairer-funding-at-cardiff-summit', '2026-09-16', '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', 'Finance; Cabinet Minister for Finance - Elin Jones', '', '', NULL, 63.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'housing_supply', 'Planning & place', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Decision or commitment', 'I can confirm; affordable housing', 0, '2026-09-18T11:37:50.617984', 'newsroom:welsh-finance-minister-to-press-uk-government-for-fairer-funding-at-cardiff-summit', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 What do I attribute that to? Our focus this year has been bringing housing associations on board. We''ve now got all social housing landlords and housing associations in Wales also operating under the standards. So, that''s an important milestone for us, because when the legislation was set, it was anticipated that these standards would be rolled out right across the Welsh public sector. So, we''re on a bit of a journey there.

 We probably have not put as much effort in on health and local government. They''re operating under the standards, they comply. We do training at the outset, when bodies come under the standards. I know we''ve had lots of requests from public bodies, probably local authorities in particular, for top-up training, refresher training for staff, because obviously they have turnovers of staff as well. We haven''t been able to give as much time and resource to that as we would normally, because we''ve been focused on housing associations.

 So, there''s a limited resource that we have to go back and keep doing that support. That will be part of it. But I think it also probably reflects the pressure those sectors are under, in terms of seeing an increase in their own complaints and the pressure on resources to deal with them as well. So, we don''t want to see it going that way. We want to see it going the other way and we''ll continue to look at that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=10037&l=en', NULL, 132.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'private_rented_sector; social_housing; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session; housing associations; landlords; social housing', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.158093', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777614', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 There were quite a number of recommendations on this area of our work. But just to summarise at the outset, when we reviewed our processes, we looked at how we could be flexible. There are particular consultation requirements set out in the legislation. There are some consultations that we always have to do, and obviously, we will make sure we meet those statutory requirements. On some of the other consultation responsibilities, we''ve been flexible there to try and really make it more streamlined, the process. For example, we started one of our wider own-initiative investigations in April, and we''re drawing that to a close now next month. That really demonstrates that we''ve reviewed that process and made improvements in how quickly we''re able to look at issues. That one I mentioned related to financial redress under the then ''Putting Things Right'' scheme. Our aim with the own-initiative investigations is to really target recommendations that will help a number of people through one efficient investigation.

 We''ve also looked across, in response to the recommendations, at what Northern Ireland does, because one of the committee''s observations was that we should look at how they are doing some of their work. Our processes are very similar. But one thing we have picked up that Northern Ireland do is that, when they consult on an investigation, and perhaps the responses they receive, either from the public body itself or other parties, lead them to take a decision, ''Actually, we won''t conduct a full and formal investigation here'', they publish all of that intelligence and work as an overview report.

 In terms of the current position we''re in, as a result of the changes, we already have five own-initiative investigations under way this year and we have a number in the pipeline that we will propose. If, following those proposals and consultations, we feel that it''s not in the public interest for us to devote our resource to look at a particular issue, and we''re satisfied that public bodies are responding to the issue, and there''s no need for us to go there, if you like, we will publish those reports as well.

 All of the measures we''ve put in place are aimed at being slicker, quicker, focusing on the key issues. We''ve spoken briefly about data, but it''s key for us in terms of that intelligence and knowing where an issue might be where we can effectively and efficiently use one investigation to deliver outcomes for many people through that important power we have.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Katrin Shaw', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=9686&l=en', NULL, 115.0, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; consultation; consultations; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.108795', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777721', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But if we look at Dŵr Cymru, for example, and its performance over the last five years, it has areas where it has performed well—internal sewer flooding, for example, where it has been top across the sector. It''s been above average on a number of measures—customer satisfaction, its response to the customers on the priority services register, unplanned outages, sewer collapses. But, on balance, in more areas than not, improvement is needed, especially on leakage, per capita consumption, which remains stubbornly high, water supply interruptions, water quality, the rates of replacing water mains, its poor performance on pollution, and the compliance of its sewage treatment works with permits. They are significant challenges. 

 For the water company performance report this year, although I can''t tell you what it says, what I can say is that it will report performance in a slightly different way. Instead of simply reporting performance against those dozen commitments that I just read out, there will be more performance commitments in there; there will be 23. It will do more than simply say whether the performance commitment has been met or not met. It will also talk about Dŵr Cymru''s performance relative to the sector, and I think you will have heard from Dŵr Cymru themselves that there have been some challenges there. It will report on the trend in Dŵr Cymru''s performance over time, and it will attach more weight to some performance commitments that the evidence tells us matter most to customers. I think that is quite a challenging picture for Dŵr Cymru. 

 For Hafren Dyfrdwy, the situation is not challenging. In the period up to 2025, it is good on eight of the 12 performance commitments that we reported on, and improvements needed only in four—leakage, per capita consumption, water supply interruptions and internal sewer flooding. For a company that is a water-only company, Hafren''s performance on the environment is particularly strong, and I hope that that trend will continue.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=818&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:21.846224', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773168', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We''ve strengthened our oversight of Welsh companies'' performance, and we work very closely and collaboratively with Welsh partners to hold these companies to account where performance isn''t good enough. We''ve built a much stronger Wales-specific evidence base to inform our work, and we''re now entering a really important period of time, as Chris said, in transition for water. And this is a real opportunity to shape those future arrangements around Welsh priorities, around Welsh circumstances. But the really important thing for us is making sure that, in that transition period, performance isn''t lost—it can''t slip, and we really need to keep our eye on the ball there. Because, ultimately, customers will judge all of us not by regulatory structures that are created, but for the results that they see: so, cleaner rivers, more resilient services and greater trust in the sector.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Lia Murphy', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=242&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:21.778092', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773166', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I notice, obviously, that there''s been a budget increase from the previous year to this year, and you''ve employed more staff to deal with case management, but also improvement. But then looking at the KPIs under strategic aim 3, which is the proactive improvement work, there''s been a general deterioration in the KPIs in terms of that improvement work. What do you ascribe that to?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=9912&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.135737', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777595', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 During the reminder stage, or the prompt stage, as I call it, we did have opt-outs. We did have a handful, a very small number; we only had 28-odd for the whole of the mailout. But we had a few, and I took some of those phone calls myself—I happened to be in the office—and when I questioned, ''Why did you leave it until this point when you had the time?'', it was, ''I just put it on the mantelpiece'' or ''I put it in the recycling''. So, they weren''t concerned about their safety, they just wanted to opt out. They''d read it fully by then, considered it and obviously wanted to opt out at that stage. When we sent the final notification, again, we had, I think, just one person querying something, but nothing with regard to safeguarding. All those phone calls with concerns about their name appearing on a public register, or because of their safety or circumstance, all happened within the first tranche, and our team were able to advise them accordingly.

 So, based on that experience, I think 28 days would give sufficient time. I do think—and Clare made the point, and it was something that we experienced—that we need to have clear messaging on the envelope. It''s amazing how many people receive an envelope and don''t do anything with it. Certainly, that would also aid with the accuracy, where, if somebody has been matched and identified against the wrong property, what tends to happen is the householder just thinks, ''Oh, they might be the past occupier'' or that it''s a bureaucracy or admin error, and they just chuck it in the recycling. We need them to phone us. ''If this person does not live at your property, we need you to contact us immediately.'' And, again, with regard to safeguarding, there needs to be a clear message that if you do not wish to be automatically registered, you need to take action by a certain date, or immediately. So, I think there are mitigations around that, with clear communications and flagging that. 

 And I think—. We had a by-election. We had several by-elections, actually, during that 60-day period—never a quiet time—and, obviously, that 60-day period is quite difficult. You''re telling people they''re going to be on a register at a certain point, but, actually, there''s an election in the meantime. It''s about the messaging to the public. ''Oh, does that mean I can vote for this one?'' ''No, it doesn''t, you need to do something else.'' And 60 days was quite a long period for that, so I think 28 days probably sits better with an election timetable, because we don''t just have elections every four years, or even once a year; they happen throughout the year, so it''s something that, for the electorate, needs to be a bit more immediate.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Sandra Matthews', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=4696&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.307709', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769957', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The reality is that children who enter care often face significant disadvantages later in life. We know care-experienced young people are less likely to attend university, to attain the qualification level that they could have, and are more likely to experience homelessness, and even more likely to experience mental health difficulties as they grow up. Those outcomes are not inevitable, but they are deeply concerning and should compel all of us to ask a simple question: is there a better way of helping families overcome the issues that bring them into the court system in the first place, and if there is, why would we not pursue it?

 What strikes me about family drug and alcohol courts is that they do not lower the standards or compromise children''s safety; they do the exact opposite. They recognise the best way to protect children, and it''s often to address the root causes that place families in crisis and not simply manage the consequences. The evidence presented today shows that, when parents receive intensive support to overcome addiction and address traumas, and child benefit too, more parents successfully stop misusing drugs and alcohol, more families achieve stability, and more children are able to remain safely or return safely to their parents and family. That matters, because every child who can safely remain within a loving family environment is given something that no institution or service can ever truly replace—a sense of belonging, a sense of identity, a sense of permanence.

 The Welsh pilot demonstrated that the proportion of children in the care of one or both of their parents more than doubled during FDAC proceedings. Evidence from England shows substantially more children being united with their families compared to conventional proceedings. Those outcomes are not just policy successes, they are life-changing opportunities for children. Children who may stay in the same school, maintain friendships, remain connected to brothers and sisters, preserve relationships with grandparents and wider family networks, and avoid the additional trauma that can accompany family separation.

 Of course, there will always be cases where children cannot safely remain at home. In those circumstances, protecting the child must remain our overriding priority. But where recovery is achievable, where parents are willing to engage, and where evidence demonstrates better outcomes, we should ensure those opportunities exist.

 What concerns me is that Wales once had a pilot programme, yet today we have nothing—no family drug and alcohol court operating in our nation at all. Meanwhile, England has continued on the journey and expanded the model. That leaves Welsh children with fewer opportunities to benefit from an approach that has repeatedly shown promise. We often speak in this Chamber about early intervention, about prevention, about breaking cycles that pass from one generation to the next. Family drug and alcohol courts embody those principles more perfectly than anything I''ve ever seen. They are not simply about responding to addiction, they''re about giving children the best possible chance of a stable future, they''re about preventing trauma rather than merely dealing with the consequence itself, and they are about recognising that helping a parent recover can transform a child''s entire life trajectory.

 Members, every child deserves safety, every child deserves stability and every child deserves the opportunity to grow up in a nurturing family environment where that can be achieved safely. That is why I support this motion, and that is why I urge my colleagues across the Chamber to support the expansion of family drug and alcohol courts in Wales. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Art Wright', '', '12157', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Family Drug and Alcohol Courts', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=10080&l=en', NULL, 44.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies', '', 'homelessness', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.908218', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776838', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The values of the multipliers for next year will be determined as part of our budget preparations for 2027-28, following the UK Government''s autumn budget. I''m proposing to make these changes at the earliest opportunity in this Senedd, to prioritise the improvements that are deliverable and affordable in the short term. The Welsh Government will continue to consider further reforms to the non-domestic rates system that reflect our broader ambitions for Wales. I know that Members across the Chamber are interested in this matter, and ideas for further measures on non-domestic rates will receive a listening ear from me. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=9588&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.023354', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769220', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

