 At a time when many families are facing significant financial pressures, we will take action to make everyday life more affordable. Central to that effort is our transformative new childcare offer, which will help families with the cost of living, support parents into work and training and make a significant contribution to tackling child poverty. We will also tackle one of the biggest pressures on household budgets by improving access to affordable and secure housing, including through our commitment to deliver at least 20,000 additional social homes by 2030.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=8620&l=en', NULL, 82.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'housing_supply; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Planning & place', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government; social homes', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.682656', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770979', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The report of the Independent Water Commission, which was in July of 2025, recommended a reset of water regulation in Wales, including devolving economic regulation, which Ofwat currently does, as well as national water system planning. The Welsh Government is committed to that—is committed to independent economic regulation for Wales—to reflect Welsh law and Welsh environmental priorities.

 The UK Government is expected to introduce a clean water Bill, which will provide the Senedd with new powers. And Welsh Government, we expect to respond to its Green Paper, from February this year, by the end of 2026 to confirm the stages in taking that forward. They were issued to us in the form of a strategic policy steer in February, but we''re expecting more detail. And the new Welsh regulator is expected to be operational by 2032. We are working very closely with Welsh Government as well as with the Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs to ensure a smooth transition to that Welsh regulator and to ensure that there are no gaps in our regulation in the meantime.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=391&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Legislative commitment', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; Green Paper', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:21.794970', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773191', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 First, on keeping the 182-day occupancy threshold for self-catering businesses under review, we absolutely support keeping this policy under review to ensure that it works as fairly and effectively as possible. Secondly, on the visitor levy, I''ve already set out the benefits. It''s common practice, it happens across many parts of the world, and Scotland are now also looking to follow suit. This is ultimately all about choice. It''s about giving choice and power to local people, to local councils, so that decisions that affect their communities can be made in those same communities. So, we will be supporting the Government amendment and we will continue to push for a thriving tourism economy all over Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=12988&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', '', 'self-catering; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.868093', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767095', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
Current stage: Royal Assent (27 April 2026)
Stage history: Royal Assent 27 Apr 2026; Royal Assent 07 Apr 2026', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=46434', '2026-04-27', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd', '', '', '', NULL, 117.0, 'High', 'digest', 'planning_system; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Planning & place', '', 'Bill progress', 'Planning (Wales) Act; Royal Assent', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:20.261641', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=46434', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Could I make it clear? It has been unequivocal in terms of this Government that tackling poverty in all parts of Wales is a priority for us. Or entire programme for government, if you look at it, that cross-Government focus, in every portfolio, is entirely clear that tackling poverty is incredibly important and is something that we prioritise. The Member will also be aware, I''m sure, that there are problems in terms of rural poverty in particular that need to be tackled. Often, that has been something that has been hidden, and it''s very important that the support that we provide in order to ensure that everyone, in all corners of Wales—. We know that, in every constituency, there are people living in poverty who should not be living in poverty, and that the last Labour Government''s record was to increase the number of children living in poverty. That is why it is a priority for this Government to work across Ministers to get to the heart of the problem.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Heledd Fychan', '', '426', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=8316&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.633291', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770989', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We know that care leavers are underrepresented at universities and are on what Crisis describes as a ''predictable route'' into homelessness. According to the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, they are approximately four times more likely to have a mental health need compared to children living with their birth families. Overwhelmingly, professionals and academics have made it clear that corporate parenting has not been achieving for care-experienced children and young people.

 Parents are being failed too. Research shows that parents who go through care proceedings once are more likely to find themselves in proceedings again with subsequent children. Forty-seven per cent of newborn babies subject to care proceedings in England were born to mothers who had previously had children subject to care proceedings. The Nuffield Family Justice Observatory found that mothers and fathers who are frequently involved in care proceedings are dealing with complex and unresolved trauma, which is compounded by the court experience. The adversarial proceedings, formal language and impersonal process compound anxieties and triggers self-protective behaviours, which negatively impact the relationship between the professional and the family unit. Parents feel that they are not being listened to. As the independent review of children''s social care in 2022 found, court proceedings are, by their nature, adversarial and have a high human and economic cost.

 Members, it doesn''t have to be like this. There is another way: family drug and alcohol courts. How are these different? I want to answer by quoting the words of a parent who had previously had children removed, comparing the FDAC process to the standard process. Of the standard process, she said, ''We felt like we had no voice and that we were powerless, and that we were just being accused of things and that there was no opportunity to defend ourselves.'' Speaking of the family drug and alcohol court process, the same parent said, ''It feels like we have the power to actually have our say, get things done, have our side heard, and then they try to work with us to improve things and meet in the middle.''

 Unlike standard proceedings, FDAC uses a trauma-informed approach, which includes listening to parents without judgment, focusing on parents'' strengths and the resources that they currently have available to them, and allowing flexibility. Parents are given a key worker from the FDAC team and a judge who provides them with support and a problem-solving approach. There is weekly substance misuse testing, which involves the parents, and fortnightly non-lawyer reviews with consistent legal monitoring that creates a forum for judges and professionals to problem solve with the parents, set clear goals and delegate tasks for the following two weeks. Parents have a multidisciplinary team to support them, who help them to gain access to services and agencies. They provide transparent information and set up multiple sessions per week to address the intervention plan.

 Clearly, it’s a very different model to the standard process, but how do we get families there? One challenge that arose from the Welsh trial was that the number of referrals was hampered by confusion over the threshold. Professionals were thought to be pre-screening referrals themselves and only referring if parents'' substance misuse was particularly severe. Some legal representatives were under the impression that the local authority was responsible for determining which cases met the threshold, while the judge suggested that there was a general lack of awareness and exposure to the model.

 That lack of awareness can be overcome when we follow the model used in London, which is that the usual threshold for proceedings applies, meaning that if a family is not selected for the model, they will enter the usual family proceedings court instead, and a family will qualify if they have a history of parental drug or alcohol misuse. So, now that we know what these courts are, I want to show you why the FDACs are a more effective model.

 The evaluation of the family drug and alcohol court in Wales found that the number of parents misusing substances decreased from 83 per cent at the start of the process to 54 per cent. The number of parents misusing alcohol, thanks to the proceedings, reduced from 91 per cent to 60 per cent. Not perfect, but a marked improvement. The number of parents recorded as misusing drugs fell from 94 per cent to 51 per cent. Those supported by the experience of family drug and alcohol courts in England and, more recently, the national FDAC impact evaluation, found that a higher proportion of parents stopped misusing drugs or alcohol by the end of the proceedings. The pilot in Wales showed that the number of children in the care of one or both of their parents more than doubled from the start of the proceedings to the end. That is supported, again, by a study in England, which found that 52 per cent of children were returned to their parents, compared to only 13 per cent in the standard process. The family teams almost halved the number of children being placed in local authority care. That''s more family units kept as one.

 There are other proven benefits too. Unsurprisingly, feedback from parents is overwhelmingly positive. The Bedfordshire team evaluation saw parents describe the process as non-judgmental and inclusive, whilst parents in London described the system as transformative. And here in Wales, a parent who was reunited with their child at the end of the proceedings described the service as a lifesaver and said, ''My life''s back on track because of the FDAC team. I can''t recommend them enough.'' ''I can''t recommend them enough''—what a contrast to how parents feel about the standard process.

 It is a serious failure that whilst England has 13 of these specialist teams, we don''t have a single one in Wales. The current situation goes against recommendation 35 of the ''Justice in Wales for the People of Wales'' report. It goes against the recommendation in the evaluation of the Welsh pilot. It goes against the best interests of children, young people and their parents. There is no reason why this injustice cannot be stopped. This is confirmed when also considering the economic case.

 The Centre for Justice Innovation and Mutual Ventures'' economic analysis shows that, for every £1 spent on FDAC operations, £3 is generated in savings to the taxpayer. A reduction in the cost of children''s services needs to be taken extremely seriously, especially when we consider that overspends in Welsh local authorities arising from children''s social care last year alone came to nearly £30 million. Bearing in mind the benefit to the taxpayer, the benefit to some of our most vulnerable children and the benefit to some of our most vulnerable parents, I urge the Senedd to back my motion today. Let us together create a care system that builds, not breaks families. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Tom Montgomery', '', '12183', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Family Drug and Alcohol Courts', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=8759&l=en', NULL, 44.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies', '', 'homelessness', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.808559', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776782', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In addition, our decision too was to start to make some new spending commitments to align with the mandate that we received from the people of Wales through a national election. This budget, therefore, allocates £294 million, which is £164 million in resources and £130 million in capital, to support practical priorities that people can see and feel, as was voted for, such as reducing waiting times in the NHS, expanding funded childcare, ensuring that more pupils receive free school meals, more social homes, supporting transport and crucial maintenance work in our schools and hospitals. In many respects, these were priorities that were seen in the manifestos of other parties during the election period.

 Our intention is to invest £145 million in our health system to speed up access to treatment and to improve outcomes for the people of Wales. We are giving priority to providing additional funding to tackle child poverty and to help people across Wales with increasing costs of living, and are allocating an additional £55 million to start to deliver our commitment to transform childcare. This budget also provides £2 million to start to implement the Cynnal pilot scheme. That''s an important first step in our broader mission to tackle the terrible scourge of child poverty. We are investing an additional £15 million in free school meals. That will enable us to start to extend provision from September onwards to pupils in secondary schools.

 We are also providing £40 million in additional capital to improve the standard of school buildings, and a further capital sum of £5 million will be provided towards the community facilities programme. The budget includes £2 million to help to introduce the new swimming and water safety programme for schools, and another £8 million to continue to provide £1 transport on buses for children and young people, and also £2 million in a bus service between north and south Wales along the western corridor, which doesn''t have a train line. Everyone in Wales deserves a safe home that they can afford. That is why we are investing £20 million in capital to provide over 100 additional homes for social rent.

 The first supplementary budget is a routine part of the annual budgetary process. It provides assurances to our public services. It also ensures that members of Senedd committees in their scrutiny work can see clearly the changes to Government decisions in terms of income and expenditure. At this point, I would like to thank the Finance Committee for considering this supplementary budget in full and for publishing its report overnight. I have read the report this morning and I see value in its recommendations and I will try to respond positively to them, and I look forward to collaborating with the Finance Committee on refining our scrutiny and the budgetary process over the period of this Senedd.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=22863&l=en', NULL, 115.5, 'High', 'digest', 'housing_supply; social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system; Planning & place', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; social homes; social rent; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.460339', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767959', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I''ll start with houses in multiple occupation. HMOs play a crucial role for university students, single individuals, people on low incomes, people who arrive to a new area, for whatever reason, or people who only want short-term housing as an option. The most recent Welsh Government data accounts for under 15,000 HMOs in Wales. A mixture of types of accommodation within communities is important, and the concentration of HMOs in certain areas is something that warrants further action. But that is a separate issue to the misleading motion that''s being debated today.

 The proposals put forward today to restrict the rights of people who live here legally would mean evicting workers, carers, colleagues and friends. That''s the reality. If today''s motion passes, this Senedd would be pitting neighbour against neighbour. It would not represent Wales and Welsh values. The evidence shows that the vast majority—94 per cent—of social housing tenants in Wales were born in the UK, and nearly 80 per cent were born in Wales. Out of almost 0.5 million residents living in social housing in Wales, 463,000 were born in the UK. Of those who are not British citizens, there are people with settled status, refugees who have been granted protection, people granted humanitarian protection, and people with a lawful immigration status, which gives them that entitlement. It''s clear that social housing should continue to be allocated according to housing need and in line with legal obligations.

 We know that there is pressure on local services, including housing, across communities in Wales—nobody is denying that. But the way of alleviating that pressure is to invest in public services, grow our economy, build more homes and deliver effectively across Government, not to rip up legal rights and deny people the services that they''re entitled to.

 Social housing in Wales is allocated based on housing need, and there are strict rules that determine eligibility. That means most people subject to immigration control aren''t eligible for social housing. That is a fact, but one that some people in this Chamber refuse to recognise. There is significant disinformation and misinformation when it comes to social housing and the wider debate about immigration in Wales. It''s important to emphasise that social housing allocations primarily work to serve the people of Wales. The reality is that local authorities across Wales apply tight controls around eligibility, and there''s always a need to follow the correct process to ensure that housing needs are properly assessed.

 Housing associations also play an increasingly important role in supporting people and communities, helping residents sustain their tenancies, improve their well-being, and access opportunities that enable them to thrive. As a result, social landlords are balancing the need to build more homes, invest in existing homes, and deliver services that respond to wider social and economic challenges. This requires strong investment, long-term investment and a shared commitment to addressing Wales''s housing needs. The biggest challenge facing the housing system in Wales isn''t the eligibility criteria but the need to invest in more social homes.

 In the last Senedd term, the Welsh Labour Government invested £2 billion to deliver more than 20,000 homes for rent in the social sector, and that was record levels of investment and record levels of delivery. I''d like to know how the current Government is going to maintain that momentum and increase the supply of social housing across Wales, reducing the social housing waiting list. The focus of the Welsh Government should be on building more homes, bringing empty properties back into use, and making sure councils and housing associations can develop plans to increase the supply of social housing. That is what this Senedd should focus on and scrutinise.

 Finally, Cabinet Minister, fear is being stoked in this Chamber today, so what is this Government doing to challenge the disinformation and misinformation around social housing eligibility? Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Jayne Bryant', '', '5038', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=15648&l=en', NULL, 148.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'hmos_and_standards; taxation_of_property; housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Private rented sector; Tax & finance', 'Opposition housing leads', '', 'HMOs; empty properties; houses in multiple occupation; housing associations; housing waiting list; multiple occupation; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.365238', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776942', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Survivors aren''t a single group, are they? Their experiences are very diverse, their situations are very diverse. So, as I said, we are committed to listening to those lived experiences, to learn from those people who aren''t just one homogenous kind of group, in order to ensure that our policies and our services reflect that diversity and difference.

 In terms of prevention, of course, prevention is one of those guiding principles for all of our work across Government, and I agree entirely with you, again, about the importance of this work. I''m sure you''ll be aware of the programmes that we have—the Sound campaign—where we use male role models, and that has proved to be very successful, so I have continued with that. As I''ve said already, although we are a new Government, I want to continue with those things that are working, and that''s proven to be effective, and we''d like to build on that, to gather further ideas of that kind of activity. We know that the perpetrator programmes are also very important in this regard. So, I can certainly give you an assurance that that element is going to be a core part of the new strategy.

 I don''t know, Andrew, if you want to provide further details on the preventative programmes—the education ones.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=5654&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.144225', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771275', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We have been able to change some policies. So, with the national empty homes grant, for example, community groups can access that. We were lucky enough to have had some land and buildings development funding for some of our groups in the last couple of years, but now that has kind of been stopped and Welsh Government are looking at what the options are. But, yes, we would advocate for a revolving loan fund for the community-led housing sector.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Casey Edwards', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '5. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 3', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=17307&l=en', NULL, 184.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; housing_supply; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty homes; empty homes grant; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:27.028966', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775580', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 For me, this is a very broad portfolio. As you say, it''s difficult to prioritise everything. We''ve been clear in terms of certain aspects of the portfolio where we want to focus particular efforts. One would be responding to the climate and nature emergency, which is, I believe, a societal expectation on us. We are seeing the consequences of climate change being revealed—you know, this summer. We touched on that; we''ll come to it in a minute. So, there''s a lot of work to be done in that space. But also we need to do it in a way that works for communities, and works for farming particularly, which is a big other part of my portfolio. I hope to be an ambitious Minister, but one that''s pragmatic and practical in the way that I approach some of these challenges.

 Food is also important to me. We have a good-news story here in Wales. We have excellent primary produce. We have an opportunity to build not only an economically successful food sector, but one that meets the needs of the people of Wales in terms of providing that nutritious source of affordable food that everybody deserves. You''ll be aware that there is a piece of work particularly around a national food strategy that we''re pursuing, and I very much hope that that''ll be something that we can really deliver on—not just the strategy obviously, but the benefits and the outcomes stemming from that.

 The risk of starting to list things, Chair, is that I forget elements. No doubt you will pick up on those in scrutiny, but, yes, it''s been an interesting few months in role so far, and it''s probably going to get even more interesting as we move forward. But it''s a challenge I relish, it''s a challenge I''m embracing, and hopefully it''s something where I can make sure that, when my time is up, I can say that I did make a difference.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=137&l=en', NULL, 51.7, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; programme for government', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.294168', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771859', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We have made that bid to the Treasury, and we hope that the Treasury will respond relatively rapidly now to allow that piece of work to begin. It can be a long process. Scotland''s most recent review took two years for them to deliver with the Treasury, and so—. A review of this kind is a partnership process. It''s a partnership between the Welsh Government and the Treasury to have that discussion on how we can create a framework that works more effectively and that also reflects need better.

 I would say that I would hope, having heard that the UK Government has—something that this Government welcomes—now committed to seeing how we can make devolution to the nations work more effectively, as well as devolution within England, to look at the fiscal framework that we operate in in the spirit of that ambition of the UK Government, and, hopefully, we will be able to get started on this work as soon as possible.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=9551&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:52.025622', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774626', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The steel industry has, of course, been a major employer for decades in the town of Port Talbot, and thanks to the prompt actions of the UK Labour Government in 2024, although, of course, we recognise that, sadly, the last blast furnace was closed down, there was hope, and there still is hope, in the form of a transition period and funding that has saved jobs. With £500 million of UK Labour Government investment, as part of a £1.3 billion deal into steel transition in Port Talbot, we were able to protect 5,000 jobs and ensure that Welsh steel making has a new future with a new electric arc furnace being commissioned, and, importantly, workers have had a seat at the table during the transition period. Working in social partnership with our unions, the Welsh Government and the UK Government invested in helping workers find new opportunities and new careers, with Port Talbot becoming a model for a worker-centred just transition. Instead of leaving people behind to fend for themselves, unions, the Welsh Government and the UK Government invested in helping workers find new opportunities and new careers.

 Now, as the daughter of a steelworker, I know that when change comes to a steelworks, it affects far more than the payroll. It affects confidence, it affects family life and mental well-being. That''s why the support that''s been offered to workers throughout this transition has been so important in helping people to actually navigate that uncertain period with dignity and hope. Without the support provided through the community support centre and the wider transition board programme, many workers would have faced redundancy with far fewer options on the table. The success of that model has helped people move into retraining, access advice and find new opportunities while remaining rooted in their community. We''ve also not seen any increase in unemployment benefits claimed during the transition, which is testament to its success.

 Now, turning to this LCM, I know there''s been extensive engagement with the Department for Business and Trade, the Bill''s sponsor, the Wales Office and this Welsh Government, and I understand that this Bill is primarily being brought forward to address the current situation at British Steel Limited in Scunthorpe. The UK Government has no plans to use the powers in this Bill more widely, and I know that concerns have rightly been raised about the powers being used in Wales at a future date. However, I''m reassured that the UK Labour Government has listened to these concerns about any potential impact in Wales and has set out a number of commitments in response. Firstly, DBT has confirmed that the UK Government would consult Welsh Ministers in advance of using the powers of this Bill in any undertaking in Wales. So, it''s worth noting that this was accepted, of course, by the Scottish Government as being sufficient assurance to support the LCM in Scotland. Secondly, it goes further by confirming that, should the UK Government ever have to nationalise a Welsh steelworks, the UK Government would identify and ensure an appropriate role for the Welsh Government in the oversight of the works, and it would do this while recognising the statutory role of the board and the Secretary of State in those circumstances. And it also reflects the established practice of the UK Labour Government in terms of steel projects in Wales. For example, the Port Talbot transition board established to administer more than £100 million of UK Government funding to the steelworks and businesses affected by the transition is deputy chaired by a Welsh Government Minister—now, obviously, the Minister, Adam Price.

 So, I am satisfied that the UK Government has now addressed the Welsh Government''s requests, and I now urge Senedd Members to support the LCM on this Bill. I support this LCM today because it''s not about ''if'' and it''s not about ''when'' these powers are ever going to be needed in Wales, because of the assurances I have set out above and, actually, many of the comments as well that have been made in the Chamber so far.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21119&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'LCM; Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.323023', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767919', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Members of the sixth Senedd will remember Luke Fletcher''s calls for the UK Government to bring the Port Talbot site into public ownership and retain primary steel making throughout the transition. At the time, those proposals were ridiculed and dismissed as fanciful. Yet, here we are today, discussing the implications for Cymru of a UK Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill. It''s abundantly clear, regardless of which party is in power at Westminster, what was deemed necessary for Scunthorpe was apparently too much to ask for Port Talbot. What assurances can the Gweinidog give to workers in Port Talbot that this Government will continue to make the case for greater investment at the Port Talbot site under a Burnham-led UK Government?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elyn Stephens', '', '12164', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21006&l=en', NULL, 49.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'LGHP Committee members', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.312556', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767913', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Then, touching on James''s point about the loan scheme, that has been instrumental to us in terms of both returning empties to use and the creation of new residential units from empties. So, the removal of that scheme will be significant in those incentives that we can offer empty home owners.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Rhian Brown', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=5811&l=en', NULL, 184.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; housing_supply; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty homes; empty homes grant; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.610565', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775417', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

This is a committee inquiry. Inquiries take written evidence, and a call for evidence is an opportunity to influence.

Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties Issue Details Issue History Inquiry2 The Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee has agreed to conduct a follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties in Wales. The original inquiry which concluded in December 2019 was conducted by the Fifth Senedd’s Equality, Local Government and Communities (ELGC) Committee. Empty properties includes residential properties, heritage buildings, and commercial or other non-residential spaces. Terms of reference To consider: >>>> >>>progress made towards implementing recommendations made by the ELGC Committee in its October 2019 report ; >>>the impact of actions taken forward by the (then) Welsh Government as outlined in its December 2022 update and identify where further progress is needed; and >>>any new policy developments since the ELGC Committee’s initial inquiry. <<< Evidence gathering: The Committee will hold oral evidence sessions with targeted stakeholders early in the autumn term (dates tbc). Business type: Committee Inquiry Reason considered: Senedd Business; First published: 23/07/2026 Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47957', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 189.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; call for evidence; empty properties; inquiry; oral evidence; terms of reference; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:52.702342', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47957', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In terms of evaluation, it''s likely that there''ll be an ongoing process over the next few years, because there will be new data sets, this work will need to be done continually. It''s not something that will happen just once. People will be reaching 16, 17 years of age, for example. We need to be collecting the data and creating a register that''s as complete and broad as possible. If we then need to look at ways to improve that, and different ways of doing certain elements, well, of course we will be completely open to that. Thanks.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '4. Scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning on the Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=10588&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:26.401160', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774537', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

Published 08/09/2026 | Reading Time minutes

The Welsh Government wants policing to be devolved , transferring legislative and executive responsibility to the Senedd and Welsh Ministers. The UK Government’s policing reforms published in January 2026, do not propose this. Instead, they could lead to Wales-specific administrative arrangements for governing police forces while policing remains reserved.

During his first official visit to Wales as Prime Minister on 27 August, Andy Burnham MP said he was open to discussing policing devolution with the Welsh Government. His comments suggest that wider constitutional change may now be open for discussion , but it is not part of the reforms currently on the table.

Policing is reserved to the UK Parliament , while health, education, housing, social services and local government are devolved. These services affect community safety, crime prevention and demand on the police. Police funding in Wales also reflects this shared landscape. It combines Home Office and Welsh Government support with revenue raised locally through council tax precepts. Police forces therefore operate within an England-and-Wales system while working closely with devolved services in Wales - an interface described as the ‘jagged edge ’ of the justice system.

Policing reform and policing devolution are related, but distinct. The UK Government’s January 2026 White Paper, From local to national: a new model for policing , proposes major structural and administrative changes across England and Wales. These include abolishing Police and Crime Commissioners (PCCs) in 2028, reviewing force numbers and boundaries, creating a National Police Service for serious and complex crime, and strengthening national standards and oversight.

The White Paper does not propose transferring legislative or executive responsibility for policing to Wales. Full devolution would require changes to the Welsh devolution settlement . What is on the table is narrower. Because the White Paper proposes moving local policing governance into local government - and local government is devolved - the UK and Welsh governments must agree replacement arrangements for Wales. These could give Wales greater administrative control and democratic oversight while policing law, powers and ultimate responsibility remain reserved.

This distinction is important. Successive commissions, including the Silk Commission , the Thomas Commission , the Brown Commission and the Independent Commission on the Constitutional Future of Wales , have considered the wider case for devolution. However, the White Paper focuses on how policing is governed, not on fully devolving it.

Wales’s PCCs broadly support the White Paper’s aims, but say its implications for Welsh governance and funding remain unclear. They told the Welsh Affairs Select Committee that reform should include consideration of devolution, rather than simply creating new England-and-Wales arrangements. Their proposed Welsh model combines a national partnership board with four regional boards aligned to the existing force areas.

Other evidence favours stronger community involvement. Wrexham University calls for locally rooted accountability, while Nottingham Trent University warns that boards dominated by local-authority representatives could weaken independent scrutiny.

Three immediate issues will shape the Welsh arrangements:

The UK Government plans to abolish PCCs in 2028 . In parts of England, elected mayors already oversee policing . Wales has no equivalent regional mayors , so it will need different arrangements. The UK Government has said Wales’s devolved local government structure will be taken into account. Scotland and Northern Ireland already have separate policing settlements.

The funding implications of the White Paper for Wales are not yet clear. Police forces are funded through government grants and council tax precepts, while the Welsh Government separately funds police community support officers (PCSOs) and related services.

For 2026-27, the four forces will receive about £512.34 million in core support . However, the White Paper’s changes to governance, force structures and national capabilities raise questions about future costs and how funding would be distributed.

The PCCs have questioned how funding would be allocated , including where money reaches Wales through the Barnett formula. This is separate from the cost of devolving policing, which a 2024 review found was unknown and likely to be significant.', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/has-wales-been-given-a-route-to-devolved-policing/', NULL, '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd Research', '', '', '', NULL, 85.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Tax & finance', '', 'Legislative commitment', 'White Paper; council tax; devolution settlement', 0, '2026-09-08T11:35:17.368088', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I stand today in support of the legislative consent motion for the steel industry Bill. As the Cabinet Minister said, steel remains a vital component of our economy and our communities. I believe that this piece of legislation should be given consent so that steel can play its part in securing Wales''s future. However, as has been mentioned, we cannot ignore the timing. For my home town of Port Talbot, this legislation has come too late. The blast furnaces that defined generations of Welsh steel making have already been extinguished, and the loss of those highly skilled jobs and the impact upon families and the local economy have been profound.

 I support this Bill today because we must do everything possible to protect what''s left of our steel industry in Wales and give our workers hope for the future. Steel is not just another economic sector, it is a foundational part of our construction, manufacturing and infrastructure projects across the country. Losing any more steel capacity would be a serious blow to our industrial base in Wales.

 I am therefore urging the Welsh Government to work with Westminster to ensure that any nationalisation delivers genuine long-term security for the remaining workforce in Wales, invests in modern technology and helps to maintain high-skilled employment in the south Wales Valleys. The people of Port Talbot deserve more than managed decline. They deserve a viable, financially sustainable future in the industry that they helped to build. I''ll be supporting this motion today, but I''m doing so with a clear message: we must learn from what happened in my home town. Future decisions regarding strategic industries must put jobs, skills and community stability first. Diolch yn fawr.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Benjamin Hodge Mckenna', '', '12142', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21589&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.386969', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767882', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I must say that the accusation that somehow this is a rehash of our manifesto, that you have a manifesto, you stand in an election, you get elected, you have a mandate, and somehow then you tear up the manifesto and start again—. That''s not how it works. That''s not how it works. If this feels like our manifesto, as in if this feels like that it reflects what we promised the people of Wales that we would do, with the priorities on health and education and job creation and childcare and child poverty and standing up for Wales, it''s doing its job, so thank you very much for confirming that, Darren.

 You also criticise—. I get it; scrutiny includes criticism. I honestly say, on health, we face an uphill struggle because we inherit some serious issues, not all of which are the fault of the last Government, but that is what we inherited.

 On PISA, don''t criticise us on PISA scores. Those PISA tests were taken in 2025, before we came into power. But it does, of course, reflect again the scale of the challenge that we face.

 The Member focuses, in particular, on health, and, yes, it is something that is important to me, but it''s important to all of us here, I think, getting the NHS right. The Member''s party has often made the case that we should declare a crisis. I do certainly believe that there''s a crisis in many parts of the NHS. I''m not convinced that a declaration of crisis in that kind of way is particularly useful. There are suggestions there that we should have more about beds here. I have made the case for making sure that we maximise the capacity of our health system in the past, but it''s not quite that simple either. Our surgical hubs—most of the people that will go through surgical hubs will be going home the same day. Also, in my local district general hospital, it''s not a lack of beds; it''s the lack of throughput and the number of people. It''s the same wherever we are. The numbers of people who are medically fit for discharge and who are still in our hospitals, those are the issues. Should we have an ambition to absolutely grow where we can, including bed capacity, with the staff to go with them? Yes, absolutely. But we''re also working within constraints. Remember that the plans that we have put in place and that are reflected in this document are clinically led, and they''re not telling us what we need is more beds—they''re saying we need a number of steps to be taken. But I''m not dismissing the point that the Member is making.

 On the shift of resource towards primary—and I include preventative, I guess, in that, though preventative is a little bit different too—the Member''s right that you have to be careful. We''re not suggesting going in one leap. We''re talking about 0.5 percentage point per year of an increase. But it happens in a slow and sustainable way because you don''t want a detrimental impact on secondary care, which, in many ways, is the expensive end of the health service. And getting those waiting lists down, the higher waiting times in particular, takes then the pressure, hopefully, off secondary care, which helps then that transition to primary care.

 The leader of the Conservative group says that there''s a lot of ambition in this document that we share. That''s good. So, let''s work on those areas. The same invitation goes to Members in other parties. This is about ambition, as it says, but, at the end of the day, we''re going to be measured on whether we are able to realise, if not 100 per cent, the thrust of what we say we''re going to try to achieve on behalf of the people of Wales here.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=10485&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.988643', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771005', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Can we just double check that the translation''s okay for you, before we go into those? Is that okay? Just to make sure that everything''s—.

 [Translation: '' Un dau , one two, un dau , one two, un dau one two—if you can give me a thumb''s up if you can hear me. Yes. Thank you very much.'']

 Give us a thumbs up if we''re all good. Okay. Great. Diolch yn fawr iawn. Lovely. Thank you so much.

 I guess the first question is in relation to the report that''s been published. Clearly, that was signed off by the previous auditor general, and we have a new auditor general with you here today, Catherine, and I was wondering—you''re the one who''s going to be scrutinised on the performance of the previous auditor general—how you feel about that and how you''ve sought some assurances to ensure that you''re assured that the signing off process for that was appropriate.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Sam Rowlands', '', '8734', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=93&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs; Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:50.711255', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774089', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
