 We are already making sure that we are providing crisis support, but I know that the level of the price hike is already unaffordable. It is something that I worked closely with your former colleague Mark Isherwood on in the last Senedd. As I said, the Prif Weinidog has called on the UK Government—. Many of the levers around this are things like the social tariff, which is something that this Government would really like to see, which would really help those families in need who are finding their fuel bills unaffordable. He''s asked for a four-nation summit to ensure robust plans are developed to support low-income households with those unavoidably high energy needs and who will be most impacted by higher energy prices when winter approaches.

 As regards the Warm Homes plan, that sits in the portfolio of my colleague the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning, if I''ve got that right. But, of course, fuel poverty is very much in my portfolio, so we''ll be working together on those plans, and I know there''s already action afoot as regards the Warm Homes plan.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=1606&l=en', NULL, 132.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'energy_efficiency; welfare_and_support', 'Housing system; Property & energy', 'Sector bodies; Housing minister; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Warm Homes; discretionary assistance fund; fuel poverty', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:15.605043', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766843', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Turning to the Building Safety (Wales) Act 2026, I''m pleased to say that we are making steady progress with implementation. The Act marks a major step forward in delivering lasting building safety reform in Wales. The Act strengthens the regulatory framework for multi-occupied residential buildings and it introduces new competence requirements for fire risk assessors. The Act also creates stronger mechanisms to hold building owners to account, and it gives residents a stronger voice in matters affecting their safety.

 Everyone knows what my view is now, which is that leaseholders should not be expected to pay for remediation costs that they did not cause. And I do believe that, wherever possible, the people who developed, commissioned or profited from unsafe buildings should be responsible. And that''s why I worked hard to ensure that the Act did include provisions that allow interested parties to apply to the residential property tribunal for remediation orders and remediation contribution orders covering fire and structural safety defects.

 The consultation on the regulations that are needed to support this element of the regime closed earlier this month, and I hope to the lay regulations before the Christmas recess. Over the coming year, we’ll continue consulting on the regulations that are required to underpin the wider regime as part of our planned implementation schedule. And we anticipate that the registration of category 1 buildings will begin in December 2027. I''ll keep Members updated as the implementation programme progresses.

 The Act complements the extensive programme of reform already delivered through the UK Building Safety Act 2022, as well as the subsequent secondary legislation, and that underpins the new building control regime that came into force in July. The new regime strengthens accountability for duty holders and it embeds the enhanced competence requirements for registered building inspectors and registered building control approvers. Amendments were also made to approved document B of the building regulations last year.

 Members will be very well aware the Grenfell tower tragedy is the backdrop to our entire building safety programme. As we remember the 72 people who lost their lives, I would like to provide an update on the progress being made to implement the recommendations of the Grenfell tower inquiry. Over a quarter of the recommendations have now been implemented in Wales, and I expect that number to double by the end of the year. We are addressing many of the recommendations through the legislative changes I have already spoken about. Additionally, we''ve taken action to strengthen emergency preparedness and to embed the lessons from Grenfell across the system.

 Finally, I''ll return to our remediation programme. Last week, I met with developers who have signed up to the Welsh Government contract. That was the previous Government''s contract. I emphasised that all obligations under the contract must be met or I will consider all appropriate enforcement avenues in relation to any failures. I am committed to accelerating the pace of remediation and to holding developers to account. I will continue to update colleagues as the programme progresses, and I welcome the scrutiny work from Members, which will help us to get this right for the people of Wales. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: Building Safety Programme Update', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=16502&l=en', NULL, 148.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'building_safety_and_leasehold; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Property & energy', 'Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'Building Safety (Wales) Act; Residential Property Tribunal; building safety; consultation; inquiry; leaseholders; remediation contribution; remediation orders', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.778745', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771123', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I''m also concerned about the unintended consequences of today''s announcements. Few in this Chamber would, I think, challenge the notion of raising business rates on large out-of-town retailers, but these proposals will likely also impact valuable sites employing hundreds of workers each in places like the city centres of Cardiff, Swansea, Newport and Wrexham.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=10117&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates; non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.100957', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769222', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

