 And on community right to buy, I appreciate the support there for that principle. There''s a lot of work to be done in defining what exactly, for example, should be included. Some of the work has begun already, but we will get down to that work in earnest. I should have answered the question earlier on the question of funding. There is already a range of grant and loan funding arrangements available that community groups can access. I''m always interested in seeing how those can be developed further, but I think the important thing here is laying down the legal framework within which we can identify who can be the responsible bodies locally; which community assets need to be protected; and how we put those protections in place. Let''s get that work done first.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=10789&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'community assets; community right to buy; legislative programme; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.231582', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767790', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 There''s no referendum being pushed for by this Government now, but we absolutely believe that the people of Wales should be allowed to decide. I will use the influence that I have, hopefully, as Wales''s leader, to try to persuade people to think about the challenges, the opportunities and what we might be able to do in future that we aren''t able to do now. These paths are challenging and full of opportunity at the same time. The Commission on the Constitutional Future of Wales isn''t an independence commission. It is about, ''How do we arm ourselves to make us, as a nation, fit for the future?''

 Now, Reform, we know—we''ve heard it again today from your leader, and again, you''re being honest about this—don''t believe in giving us any more powers. You think we should settle as we are. You think that we should, at best, put what we have in aspic and just hope that somehow, through our actions as Governments, that we can make things better. I just don''t see it in that way. I think, where there are things that all the evidence points to it making sense for us to have control over—policing, where we pay for most of it directly in Wales now, but have no accountability for it; justice, so that we can serve people better in constituencies all across Wales; so that we can make decisions about our natural resources—I just think that''s common sense.

 What the commission does is provide us a platform through which you can say you don''t want it to happen, and I can say, and others, ''Do you know what? We should look at this and see how we can deliver this.''', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=10965&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.061628', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771012', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

