 One thing that we''ve said in our churches report is that we are losing lots of opportunities to pick up these properties before they become empty, because the church needs to sell it on because it''s insuring it, and the cost of mothballing that property and holding on to it. We talk about some sort of holding agency or role within the Welsh Government, whether that sits in Unnos, which is talked about a lot, of actually recognising, ''This is a community asset that can''t be knocked down''—particularly in terms of a church that might be listed—''we have to find a reason, we have to find a way to do something with it—a building needs a use''. We''re going to miss that. Quite often the private sector will be able to move fast to take on that asset, but then will go and sit on it for a long period of time. If we''re going to have a strategy at getting better at that sort of thing, that would be really powerful, I think.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Wendy Dearden', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '5. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 3', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=15744&l=en', NULL, 230.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; housing_supply; planning_system; social_housing; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; Unnos; community asset; empty properties; evidence session; housing association; inquiry; planning permission; social housing', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:26.745466', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775586', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 How do you incorporate that increase in the workload within existing resources, and is there any work that''s been done regarding the reasons behind the increase in the complaint numbers? I think we''ve heard about the reasons previously, but, in terms of the current resources, and the estimate before us today, how have you incorporated that into that work?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Sera Evans', '', '330', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=10555&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.247248', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777549', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 What is causing that barrier, do you feel, for farmers to not take up that vaccination? And how are you as a Government going about trying to tackle those barriers as well? Because, of course, we need to see more of those vaccinations if we want to ensure that we don''t see what happened in south-west England happening here in Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Niamh Salkeld', '', '12143', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6254&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.140389', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772112', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Council tax is set, of course, by local authorities and it''s important that it is. I would remind the Member that, in a recent budget deal before we came into power, we made sure that there was a baseline for an increase in funding for councils, and a big part of that was to protect council tax payers, which I agree is something that''s very, very important indeed.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=1496&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'council tax', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:18.802853', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770761', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 This programme is built around the issues that matter most to people’s everyday lives: the sustainability of the health and care service; the cost of living; high-skilled, well-paid jobs; driving up standards in education; tackling child poverty and opening doors for young people; and standing up for Wales at every turn.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=8414&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.649707', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770961', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The Welsh Conservatives have proposed such measures by extending free childcare, reducing the tax burden on working households to ensure that they keep more of what they earn, whilst also calling for a dedicated poverty action plan, focusing on children and fuel poverty. Given the continued rise in reliance on the welfare state amongst many Welsh households, can you, Cabinet Minister, explain how your Government''s approach differs from treating poverty after it occurs, instead of addressing the actual cause that keeps families trapped with it in the first place?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Natasha Asghar', '', '336', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=1793&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency', 'Property & energy', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'fuel poverty', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:47.887577', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773522', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Alongside this, as is usual in supplementary budgets, the budget makes formal adaptations to the general level of funds available to Wales in light of the spring statement and the main objectives of the UK Government for this financial year. It also includes a restatement of this year''s final budget to reflect the new ministerial portfolios. Moving a first supplementary budget is an early mechanism in the financial year to set out formally changes to the annual budget. The second supplementary budget is the final opportunity to finally formalise the allocations towards the end of a financial year.

 This first supplementary budget therefore makes £433 million of additional allocations. This includes £294 million for Welsh Government priorities, and £139 million for budgetary responsibility transfers. The balance of £246 million in unallocated resources and £29,000 in general capital has been held in reserves in the first supplementary budget in order to manage in-year pressures and to respond to priorities. This unallocated funding is our protection against the financial position that we have inherited and against the significant financial pressures that are unfunded within the year, including a total of over £300 million of overspend that the health service is currently reporting. Managing these in-year pressures is a significant challenge, and that challenge is made more difficult because of the tight funding framework that we work within. The unallocated resources funding is there as reserves to be allocated later to help us manage the current pressures in the system, if necessary. We cannot allocate that funding in its entirety so early in the financial year. The previous Government made similar allocations to the NHS in supplementary budgets at the end of the financial year: £200 million in 2025-26, £424 million in 2023-24.

 The Welsh Government''s settlement is directly linked, as we know, to decisions made by the UK Government. It can change, even in-year, up and down. Only a few days after I appeared before the Finance Committee, the UK Government announced its defence investment plan. That is expected to lead to a direct negative consequence for our capital budget this year. If the cut of 1 per cent anticipated hits all departments of Government equally, that will mean the loss of between £25 million and £30 million immediately during this financial year alone, and there is more to come.

 This is a supplementary budget.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', 'Cabinet Minister for Finance', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=22645&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; final budget; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.441935', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767932', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 To get back to the very good point raised by Gwyn in relation to the community energy issues, I was lucky enough to visit Hwb y Gors in the Amman valley, where community-owned wind turbines have paid for the renovation of the community centre there. But it took 10 years to secure planning permission, and we need to make it much easier for our communities to be able to own community energy. So, Prif Weinidog, what will you and the Government do to make sure that we accelerate this, so that, actually, communities can own that community energy and ensure that they see the benefits and we stop the world being on fire? Diolch yn fawr iawn.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Jane Dodds', '', '4983', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=300&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'planning permission', 0, '2026-09-16T11:53:07.066494', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768965', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But the important thing—just in looking at the broader point that you make—is that, through this skills audit, I very much hope that we will then be able to align future apprenticeships to those growth sectors. We know in Gwynedd, for example, that there is a shortage of people with different construction skills in order to build the social housing that we want to see built, and also, the improvement of the current housing stock through retrofitting. We need particular skills to do that and we need to ensure that that skills pipeline is provided through our skills and apprenticeship providers.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16242', '2026-09-30', 'Cefin Campbell', '', '8643', '', '', 'Early Years, Children, Young People and Education Committee', '2. Welsh Government General Scrutiny Session', '16242', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16242?startPos=5080&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency; social_housing', 'Housing system; Property & energy', '', '', 'retrofitting; social housing', 0, '2026-10-02T06:30:34.426442', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16242&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773457', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
