 As I also mentioned, towards the last part of the summer, as part of developing ministerial budgets and allocations, we held sessions with each individual Minister to challenge on their thinking on their budgets and outcomes. The Minister for government efficiency was part of that with me and the Deputy First Minister, in order to provide that cross-Government analysis of individual ministerial budgets and the challenge that he would bring into that conversation on outcomes, not just budget lines.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=8955&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.930232', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774274', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I''m talking specifically here of homes again, because I do think that social housing is one of those issues that you''re referring to, which is not only the right thing to do, because we want to accelerate the process of providing more social homes, but there is also a consistent income flow coming in through rent from social housing. Therefore, it makes sense in general terms too for this investment here in Wales. I''m very pleased to hear that contribution from you supporting what we''re doing within Government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3398&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.009900', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768139', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 It''s not a large amount of money in terms of marketing, considering other budgets. But I just didn''t feel that this told us a lot, to be quite honest, and I''d just like to see how we measure engagement. I think that we have quite a bit of work to do to promote the work of the Senedd amongst the public, in order to show how important the work that happens here is, and this didn''t give me the confidence that we''re measuring how we go about doing that, and the success of it.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Sera Evans', '', '330', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=1839&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.206484', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777338', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, we''ve looked at what''s happened in the last couple of years. It''s been 3.2 per cent—the low 3 per cents—3.2 per cent and 3.3 per cent, I think. We''ve been pretty close in our estimates in previous years. So, we think 3 per cent is a reasonable, realistic, best-guess estimate, really, of where we''ll be next year. Obviously, if there''s any differentiation in that, which there has been this year—we were able to find the additional 0.3 per cent from our budget to fund that. In other years previous to that, the office has been in the position of having to come back for a supplementary budget midyear if the increase was significantly beyond where we estimated it to be. So, we think that''s a reasonable estimate based on previous years.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=10728&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.272160', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777633', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, I''m open to this. This is something that sits more within the economy portfolio, but I''m very open to ensuring—and I support those calls—that we receive fairness for parents, mothers and fathers, and for other carers.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=4660&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.032923', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771286', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, looking back at that plan, we''ve said that we want to deliver justice with a positive impact for people; it''s our core service. So, in practice, what that looks like, what we want to do is we want to be able to intervene in more cases. We continue to increase year-on-year, but we recognise that we still have to say ''no'' to a lot of people, so we want to intervene in more in the future. And the other thing is that we want to be able to do that in a more timely manner. We''ve had feedback from our stakeholders, from public bodies and complainants here, about the length of time that some of our investigations take. So, we recognise that we''ve got to focus on timeliness, so that''s another area for us, and to reduce the time it takes us to intervene, doing more early resolution work where that''s appropriate. 

 We''ve set ourselves targets for this year, and we''ve recently published our KPI targets for the three years of the plan. And we have set ourselves ambitious and stretched targets to improve all those things, and we have been listening to our complainants and our stakeholders in doing that. So, that has very much informed the estimate and what we want to do in the future. 

 Influencing positive change—it is about more use of those powers that Katrin''s been talking about, our own-initiative powers, and about how we can be more proactive in our work in driving improvements in public service, not just reacting to the complaints that we receive.

 And then I think the other point to make is just about continuing to strengthen access to our service, so that we are doing work to make sure people know about us, can access us easily and in the language and in the way that they wish to do so, as we''ve already discussed.

 So, that''s a very—. I''m trying to keep it succinct here, but that''s really the thinking that''s informed the estimate, and also what we want to achieve in the coming year.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Michelle Morris', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=10399&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.227030', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777655', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 ''We are with Europe but not of it. We are linked, not combined. We are interested and associated, not absorbed.''

 First Minister, at your conference you spoke about a new deal for Wales. Well, the public aren''t daft. What we''re actually getting is a raw deal. You''re giving Wales a raw deal by failing to grow our economy. Unemployment is up, and not a week goes by without a negative story of businesses in Wales shutting up shop and cutting jobs. And the education sector is in such a dire state that teaching unions are threatening industrial action against this Government. What have you done to turn the dire situation around? Nothing. Instead of making substantive cuts to business taxes and energy costs by scrapping net stupid zero, you''re marching for separatism in Aberystwyth. Your programme for government was thin and without measurable targets. When are you going to ditch the dodgy ideology and start putting people first? Because so far, all you''ve done is make things worse.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16313', '2026-10-06', 'Helen Jenner', '', '12153', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16313', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16313?startPos=1263&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-10-09T13:35:09.506046', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16313&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775844', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The specifics—I might go to Manon in a second to talk about some of the ways that some of the projects are managed to make sure that we are setting the right envelope for those budgets so that we don''t overspend, because we can''t overspend. We don''t have a reserve, so we have to set enough of a budget to make sure that we don''t fall foul of any penalties or anything like that. So, the process that we go through is quite intricate to be able to make sure that we only ask for what we need. Of course, as we have seen in the last Senedd and in the last budget, if a windfall comes our way, like the rebate that we got on rates, we actually return that to the block grant. Also, a very important principle within the budget-setting process is that ring-fenced budgets don''t go from one to the other. So, if there''s an underspend, say, on the remuneration board budget, that goes back, as a matter of principle, to the block grant, it doesn''t come to the Commission to be spent outside that. But maybe I''ll ask Manon just to talk a little bit more about how we strategically go around checking that we''re having value for money when it comes to spending public funds.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=955&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.041977', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777227', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In terms of the systems themselves, Manon or Ed, do you want to talk about those systems?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=4598&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.618322', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777357', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In many of our communities in the Valleys and in post-industrial areas, people can be impacted directly by major planning decisions, but perhaps won''t respond to formal consultations and won''t attend public meetings and won''t feel that the planning system has been planned necessarily for them to take part. Could you talk a little bit about how the Government is going to reach out to those communities proactively, and how will you assess then whether the national conversation has succeeded in extending participation beyond those who already engage with planning?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Elyn Stephens', '', '12164', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=10056&l=en', NULL, 137.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister; LGHP Committee members', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; consultations; planning system', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:59.213653', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773010', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We are also making changes. We have been asked to make changes by the UK Parliament''s Public Accounts Committee to the support that all companies, including Dŵr Cymru, give to their customers. So, part of the challenge is companies and the regulators not having communicated clearly enough about what''s going to change and when it''s going to change, so that people can see what they''re getting for their money. The Public Accounts Committee made five recommendations to us about improving how water companies communicate with their customers. We will have six months to describe how we''re going to implement those.

 In addition to that, in February of 2024 we introduced a new condition in companies'' licences, which we call the customer focus licence condition, requiring them to support and communicate better with customers than ever before. In a different context, we''ve taken our first enforcement action under that licence—again, that wasn''t in Wales; that was against South East Water in England for supply outages around Tunbridge Wells at the start of the year, which were very well publicised. We found that South East Water''s communication with its customers wasn''t where it should be. So, we''ve already established how we''re going to implement that customer focused licence condition in practice, and that applies to all companies, including Dŵr Cymru Welsh Water.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=2857&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'local_government_enforcement; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; enforcement action', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:22.103727', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773224', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
