 Moving on to item P-07-1599, ''Abolish 24 hours 20MPH default speed limit—if it is necessary keep it during certain times''.

 ''24 hours 20MPH speed limit on restricted roads doesn’t make any sense. It can be timed if it is mandatory. For example almost no body would be running or playing or walking at 2:00AM who can be in danger of getting hit by a 30MPH vehicle.

 ''Suggestion is to go back to old pre Covid speed limits. But if new limits are deemed mandatory then they can be timed.''

 The submitter is M. Saleem, with 280 signatures, 274 of which are from Wales. I invite Sara to discuss the petition and any actions you would wish the committee to take.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16259', '2026-09-24', 'Jason O''Connell', '', '12158', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. New Petitions', '16259', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16259?startPos=1741&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T12:02:31.149018', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16259&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772203', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Resilience—I talked about this last week—is an issue that I have tried to focus a great deal on since I came into the role of First Minister, understanding the various elements that contribute to the resilience, or lack of, in some ways, that we have as a society. Wildfires happens to be the one that has grabbed our attention, which is why we need to make sure that we''re tightening up those resilience elements around that in the summit on 22 October. But the Member is right, flooding is something that many communities will be concerned about. There are other concerns, too—the effects of heat, the effects of cold and so on.

 It is something that we work across the UK on. I want to ensure that Wales gets its fair share of resilience funding, civil contingency funding, across the UK, and that''s something I''m eager to pursue. But the Member has my word that I will absolutely be looking at how we work between Welsh Government and UK Government to get the support that Wales needs to prepare for this wide range of threats that we face in future.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=1833&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-23T11:54:34.456097', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770730', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We set the national planning policy framework that local planning authorities apply when determining applications, and Natural Resources Wales provide specialist technical advice on groundwater risks as a statutory consultee. Planning authorities can draw on their hydrogeological expertise when they consider whether further investigation or any mitigation or monitoring is needed before a decision is taken. 

 Where evidence identifies a specific local risk, local planning authorities and NRW can use their respective planning and permitting functions to make sure that proportionate monitoring and controls are in place during construction and operation. But, ultimately, we will work closely with those bodies to support the consistent and effective application of groundwater protections as a number of different development pressures—and environmental risks, no doubt—evolve.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=4559&l=en', NULL, 27.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', '', '', 'planning authorities; planning policy', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:48.311109', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773611', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Indeed, this current 2026-27 budget was secured through an agreement between Labour and Plaid Cymru, and when Plaid extended to Government, it chose to honour the spending commitments in that budget. So, this debate should not simply be about who spends the money. It should be about something much more fundamental: how Government raises it, how it spends it, whether taxpayers receive value for it, and whether Government itself is prepared to reform, because fiscal reform cannot simply mean changing the name above the door whilst maintaining the same approach underneath it. That is why fiscal reform is ultimately about a choice: a choice between a Government that believes the answer to almost every problem is more spending and more bureaucracy, where Reform UK believes taxpayers have a right to demand value and accountability and results, because Government has no money of its own. [ Interruption .] No, thank you.

 Every pound spent in this place ultimately comes from taxpayers or borrowing that taxpayers will eventually have to service, and Reform UK starts from a very simple principle: it is taxpayers'' money before it is Government''s money. That is where I believe the fundamental difference between Reform and Plaid Cymru lies. Plaid Cymru came into Government promising change. Then came its first financial test, the supplementary budget. And what happened? It failed. On 14 July, Plaid Cymru''s first supplementary budget was rejected by this Senedd by 49 to 44 votes. Now, Plaid will undoubtedly blame opposition parties, but Government is about more than announcing how much money you want to spend. Government is about leadership. It is all about building confidence in your priorities, demonstrating value for money, and convincing Parliament your financial plans deserve its support. That is precisely why fiscal reform matters, and Plaid cannot pretend that they have nothing to do with the financial settlement that they inherited. Only months earlier, Plaid Cymru negotiated a budget agreement with the previous Labour Government and then abstained to allow the budget to pass. They did not endorse every line of it, but they helped provide the parliamentary route for that financial settlement to take effect.

 So, Reform''s message on fiscal reform is different. Fiscal reform cannot simply mean spending more, taxing more, creating another scheme. This isn''t reform; it''s more bureaucracy. Genuine fiscal reforms mean asking the harder questions. What can Government stop doing? What can Government do better? Where is taxpayers'' money going and being wasted? And what bureaucracy can be removed? That is what fiscal reform should be about: prioritising expenditure, eliminating waste and ensuring that Government lives within the resources available to it. It commits us to cutting 1p from every £1 across all rates of Welsh income tax by the end of this Senedd term, funded through reductions in expenditure elsewhere without cuts to front-line services, putting more of people''s own money back in their pockets. We will review the quango state, merging or abolishing bodies whose functions are duplicated or unnecessary. And we will introduce a tax lock. Reform will not introduce a new Welsh-controlled tax or levy that has not been set out in our manifesto and approved by voters.

 That is fiscal reform—lower taxation where it can be afforded, disciplined spending, less waste, and protecting the front-line services upon which people depend, because economic growth does not come from Government''s endless moving of taxpayers'' money around Cardiff Bay. Prosperity comes from people building businesses, entrepreneurs taking risks, companies investing, and people earning and spending their own money. And Government should create the conditions that allow them to do it. That is the Reform alternative—lower taxes, less waste, less bureaucracy, better infrastructure, stronger front-line services, and a Government that understands that economic growth ultimately pays for public services upon which we all depend.

 So, when Plaid Cymru return to this Chamber with further spending plans, Reform should ask a very different question: not simply, ''How much are you spending, and where can savings be redirected towards front-line services in—?'' Sorry, bear with me. Apologies. So, this includes politically fashionable projects that Governments are often reluctant to challenge, because fiscal reform requires us to confront spending driven more by ideology than outcomes. The test should not be whether a policy makes a Minister feel virtuous. The test should be: does it work, does it improve people''s lives, and is it worth what taxpayers are being charged for it? If the answer is ''no'', then Reform should have absolutely no hesitation in challenging that expenditure. There should be no sacred cows here when it comes to taxpayers'' money.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16313', '2026-10-06', 'Paul Marr', '', '12180', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Debate: Fiscal Reform', '16313', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16313?startPos=15033&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-10-09T13:35:10.914148', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16313&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776202', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Just to go back to the reasons, most have been covered. I think it''s important to say that the empty homes number isn''t static. Empty homes are being brought back into use regularly, but then these new ones are constantly joining the end. On top of what''s been said already, we see the cost of maintenance is a big thing. People can''t afford the ongoing cost, which leads to the property becoming empty in the end, awaiting for the house prices to rise, waiting for the costs to fall. We see that a great deal in terms of reasons for houses arriving at the state they become when they''re empty. 

 There''s a point about the carrot and stick. We''ve got to be careful the stick doesn''t hit everyone. We have issues with the council tax premium. We purchase properties and we carry out community projects within these properties, but we''re hit by these 300 per cent council tax premiums. I know work is been done in this area, but we''re footing this council tax premium bill whilst we''re trying to bring it back to use in an effective way. There''s something to note there.

 In terms of homes and chapels, I think with that case you mentioned—that real community love of these facilities—so much community engagement is needed, which comes at a cost, and a lot of time needs to go into that. But communities do buy into these processes. It just takes time. It has to be done in the right way, and that does take time, before you turn the local pub into houses. There''s a lot of negativity that comes from that in the first instance. So, that community engagement piece, which is a long piece, is super important. I''m just rattling through the questions that Mike was asking there. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Trystan Jones', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '5. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 3', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=15631&l=en', NULL, 155.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; council tax; council tax premium; council tax premiums; empty homes; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:26.727928', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775577', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Research by Wrexham.com shows that, whilst the Plaid Government was questioning the need and rationale for an extra £100 million to be spent on additional learning needs, it was at the very same time asking councils how much more they''d be spending on additional learning needs this year. Now, the data was published just before the supplementary budget vote on your own website. And that figure, in total, for all councils and all schools in Wales was an extra £96.3 million—almost exactly the £100 million that Welsh Labour was saying was required to stabilise the system across Wales, and for which you received a major UK Labour Government windfall. Now, does this revelation, uncovered as I say by Wrexham.com, demonstrate that disadvantaged children risk coming second to this Plaid Cymru Government, behind free childcare for millionaires?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Ken Skates', 'Leader of Welsh Labour', '267', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=1142&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:18.756833', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770739', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I think it is worth noting that my colleagues in the civil service have been working very closely with the UK Government on this Bill, to ensure that the provisions in the Bill appropriately reflect the devolved settlement and allow us to be fully and appropriately within the compass of the new Bill, which is, of course, a really important Bill to ensure public accountability.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16266', '2026-09-14', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Public Accounts and Public Administration Committee', '3. Introductory evidence session with the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16266', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16266?startPos=2427&l=en', NULL, 126.5, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', 'Bill progress; Opportunity to influence', 'LCM; Legislation Committee; Legislative Consent Motion; Royal Assent; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-15T11:57:29.633217', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16266&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768587', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, the bilaterals have been really good to kick that process off in terms of just seeing where we can work together. For example, you mentioned food and a discussion with the Cabinet Minister for rural resilience. That''s not just a bilateral between myself and that Cabinet Minister; we''re talking about education, we''re talking about our planning system, we''re talking about the economy and supporting our food businesses. So, that''s what this approach is absolutely about, to make sure we don''t miss those opportunities. And if we look at the approach to food during the last Government, it was quite narrowly focused, and this is absolutely building on the work that you mentioned by your previous colleague on the food Bill, to make sure that it is a whole-system approach.

 I absolutely agree with you in terms of the quality of food in schools, but also teaching our young people about food skills and making sure that we understand where our food comes from, supporting our brilliant agricultural industry, and looking at our procurement rules. So, this is not just something for food; this is absolutely health prevention, looking at the economy. So, those bilaterals have kicked off that discussion while we work out how we make sure that these are embedded discussions early on in every policy development—that we look at preventing ill health and reducing the health inequality that we''ve got and making sure it''s a cross-Government approach. And that''s my role—to make sure that we don''t miss those opportunities while we work out the mechanics, and that that is replicated on the official level as well. These conversations, potentially, have never happened before in these policy areas, so it''s making sure that that''s embedded so that it''s systematic and strategic that we really do look at health inequality and look at preventative health measures. 

 In terms of the budgets and the costs, you raise some interesting points there in terms of how that will be developed. You''re right in terms of a new budget line. I think the first step that we''re grappling with now is the definition of ''prevention'', especially if we are committed to increased spend on prevention over this Senedd term. How do we define that is really important—how do we define current activity within health and social care, but also in the other portfolio areas as we move to shift resources towards prevention. Getting that definition of prevention is absolutely key. There are different mechanisms then of identifying that spend. The Scottish Government are currently looking at how you tag some spend as prevention—so, not necessarily creating a budget line but tagging it as ''preventative'' and those definitions within preventative spend. We are exploring those options, with the key fundamental principle that transparency is key. Without us knowing what we''re spending, how we''re spending it, and, ultimately, what the outcomes of that spend are in terms of population health, it''s not as impactful as it should be, and that''s why I''m in this role. 

 In terms of the weight loss medication, absolutely—it''s creating that system of support around individuals for those lifestyle choices. They are right in some instances: preparing people for treatment in the NHS, for example, or certain body mass indexes. But making sure we''ve got that whole-system approach around the individual, to make sure there''s a sustained improvement, is really important, and we''re keeping that under review. Public Health Wales have been successful recently in co-ordinating a Wales-wide bid for funding through the obesity pathway innovation programme. That''s £8 million of funding in order to look at these issues, to develop and test approaches that could form part of that delivery model going forward. We want to be looking at treating obesity as a chronic condition, and, obviously, the changes that would mean in terms of how the NHS would respond to that would be key. 

 I''ve mentioned the food issue. On substance misuse, absolutely. I''m pleased to hear your approach in terms of the person-centred approach—not just dealing with the substance misuse issue, but, absolutely, mental health and making sure that all the services around that individual are there. Following on from the last Government''s harm reduction method, we will absolutely be taking that forward and working closely with the Deputy Minister for mental health. If I''ve missed anything, as I know time is tight—[ Interruption .] I''ll come back to you on the COPD screening specifically. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Nerys Evans', '', '261', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Statement by the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health: Priorities for Public and Preventative Health', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=15205&l=en', NULL, 27.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', '', '', 'planning system', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.730268', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767911', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We know that customers'' bills have gone up, and customers accept those bill rises only when they see what they''re getting for their money when they see those improvements. We are scrutinising Dŵr Cymru incredibly closely. We have put it into an enhanced regime of monitoring and support when we meet it on a very regular basis; I''m meeting the chief executive later on today, for example. We have regular meetings—our whole team—with the company. The last one was in June. We do those jointly with the Welsh Government, NRW and DWI. In addition to the money I spoke about earlier being ring-fenced and clawed back if it''s not delivered, we have put very stretching performance commitments on Dŵr Cymru, and attached financial incentives that will ensure that customers do get what they''re paying for. 

 I would love to be able to say that this problem will be solved overnight, but it will not be. This is only year 1 of a five-year programme, which is very early to tell if the tide is turning. But you will know the significant changes the company has made at executive and leadership level to address this problem. We are overseeing that closely and supporting the company to the extent that we can.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=1038&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:21.867403', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773147', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Now, there is no doubt that people across Wales are concerned about pressure on housing. They see growing waiting lists, rising rents and increasing difficulty for local people to access affordable homes. They expect politicians to take those concerns seriously and to offer practical solutions. Unfortunately, what we have before us from Reform today is not a serious attempt to solve a problem. As usual, it is a headline-grabbing motion that falls apart the moment you examine how it would work in actual fact. We believe—the Conservative Party believes—that social housing must prioritise those with a genuine and established connection to our country. We also agree that accommodation should not be provided to people who are here unlawfully. That is why we have tabled an amendment that takes a practical and deliverable approach and a position that is shared by the UK Conservative Party. What we do not support is a policy so carelessly drafted that it would sweep up people who are legally entitled to be here, legally entitled to work here and legally entitled to housing support under existing arrangements.

 The reality is that Reform''s motion would not just affect the groups they want to talk about. It would affect foreign students renting rooms in HMOs while studying at Welsh universities, it would affect workers who have come here legally, and it would affect Irish citizens whose rights are protected under the common travel area. Most significantly, it would also affect EU citizens who were given settled status after Brexit. These are people who were explicitly told that there would be no change to their rights if they were already lawfully resident in the United Kingdom. Those commitments were written into the withdrawal agreement and form part of our international obligations. So, Reform need to answer a simple question: are they really proposing that the United Kingdom should tear up agreements it freely entered into, creating uncertainty for British citizens living abroad and risk retaliatory action from our European neighbours, all to pursue a policy they have no realistic route in implementing? That is the logical consequence of what they are proposing.

 Now, many of the very people now championing this motion were enthusiastic supporters of the ''leave'' campaign, which repeatedly assured EU citizens already living here that their rights would be protected. Back then, they said one thing; today, they are saying another. So, which position is right? We believe in controlled immigration, secure borders and fairness in the allocation of public services. But we also believe that promises made should be promises kept. That is why our amendment takes a different path. We call for reform of social housing eligibility rules where needed, while protecting the rights of British and Irish citizens and those covered by the withdrawal agreement. We call for action against the use of HMOs to accommodate those who are unlawfully present in the United Kingdom, including those who have arrived illegally. Those are measures that can actually be implemented—they are legally sound, they are proportionate and they address legitimate public concerns without descending into gesture politics.

 We should also be clear that Welsh Government cannot escape responsibility. Labour have overseen years of pressure on housing supply, years of failure to deliver the homes that Wales needs, and years of inaction when local authorities and communities have raised concerns about housing availability. So, while Reform offer slogans, Labour try to defend their record and Plaid set out their next 100 days of uncosted aims, the Welsh Conservatives are offering solutions—solutions that recognise the pressure on housing, uphold the rule of law and can actually be delivered. For those reasons, Members should reject Reform''s flawed motion and support ours. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=14932&l=en', NULL, 110.0, 'High', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Private rented sector', 'Opposition housing leads', '', 'HMOs; housing supply; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.295243', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776955', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In Pembrokeshire and Anglesey, more than one in five jobs sits within tourism. This is not a supplementary income stream for those communities; it is the backbone of their local economy. Four in five tourism enterprises in Wales employ fewer than nine people. This is a sector of family businesses and sole traders, not international hotel chains that can absorb bad policy and move on. When this Chamber legislates without understanding that reality, it is these people who pay the price. So, point one of this motion shouldn''t be controversial, it simply asks the Senedd to recognise what is already true.

 My colleagues were with the First Minister last Friday on a luxurious cruise ship in Holyhead port, and we were hearing about the good work that Cruise Wales and Holyhead Town Council have done to get more cruise ships to stop there on their British isles tours. There are around 60 a year at the moment, bringing close to £0.25 million to the immediate local economy, not to mention the wider benefits of showcasing north Wales to returning visitors. This is an excellent example of what can be achieved when Government works together with the sector, but these examples are few and far between.

 Wales is currently the only UK nation yet to recover pre-pandemic visitor spend. Scotland''s international spend was up 41 per cent on 2019, ours is still down. If we''re serious about Wales being world class, this Chamber must ask why we''re the exception and stop making decisions that make the answer worse.

 The first bad decision: tourism tax. A tax on visitors is a tax on the jobs that serve them, a blunt instrument that hits small, rural and coastal operators hardest. And remember that Wales, as part of the UK, already charges 20 per cent value added tax on hospitality accommodation, more than double Germany''s 7 per cent or France, Italy and Spain''s 10 per cent. Layer a visitor levy on top of this and we''re pricing Wales out of the market when we''re trying to grow the sector. It''s not even been implemented yet, but the perception of Wales taxing visitors, especially from England, I believe, has already had a negative impact on visitor numbers, and I''ll let my colleagues expand on this further.

 The second bad decision: introducing the 182-day rule. Nearly 40 per cent of genuine holiday lets are not making this unrealistic high bar of letting days. Independent survey evidence shows 47 per cent of owners are losing money as they''re being classed wrongly as second homes. England''s equivalent threshold is 70 nights, less than half ours. And there is no evidence so far that the higher Welsh bar achieves its aim of increasing the number of family homes in local communities. Protecting housing supply and community sustainability can be done through the planning system, not through an unfair fiscal tool, and I''ll let my colleagues set out why that number needs to change.

 We talk about sustainable tourism. What does that mean? Well to me, in my opinion, it means the visitor should bring maximum cash to the local economy for jobs and businesses, with minimum disturbance to daily life and no lasting change to the fabric of a community. But sometimes it feels there are winners and losers in our busy summers. The tourism sector makes the majority of their revenue over summer, but for those who can''t park outside their house or find their bins full of visitors'' litter, it can feel that they are not benefiting from this surge in numbers, and we totally get that. But this need not be the case.

 We can have winners and winners if we ease up on regulation. Let the farmer open his field to cars on busy weekends in certain hotspots; let residents associations negotiate a residents'' permit scheme, so locals get parking while visitors pay for theirs; let a village hall become the ticket office or shuttle bus pick-up point for a nearby attraction, taking a fee for the privilege. On this side of the Chamber, I have a whole bucketful of ideas to make sure that local communities benefit from this sector, but this doesn''t include taxing visitors, leading them to go somewhere cheaper, which could literally be a few miles away in a different county that hasn''t implemented the tax. Although I do not agree with the policy, if the previous Welsh Government wanted to implement the tourism tax, they should have had the guts to insist it was a Wales-wide policy rather than passing the buck to local authorities.

 Sustainability also means a wide range of accommodation, so people stay rather than just visit for a day in tourism-specific places. This means relaxing regulations to support more hotels, bed and breakfasts, caravan parks and campsites. For example, we recently changed the law in Wales. We only allow 28 days of permitted development for a landowner to run a summer campsite. In England it''s 60. Nobody will invest in water supply or temporary toilets for just 28 days of business. So, let''s change that.

 Sustainability means better transport and co-ordination around our tourism hotspots. I know, from a number of occasions, freezing cold at the base of yr Wyddfa waiting for the Sherpa bus that never arrived, that this system isn''t good enough. I know there''s a transport plan specifically for the Eryri National Park, but it currently remains a plan. Where is the Welsh explorer pass linking public transport to discounted attractions? Where''s the Cadw castle pass, where you can visit as many castles as you can in a week and take a selfie at each one and get a discount in the shop? This is not a lack-of-resources issue. These things are often policy and partnership problems. We can do so much more to support the sector without taxing it.

 And finally, I want to make this point about how the tourism and hospitality industry can make a vital contribution to stopping our rising youth unemployment. Almost everyone''s first job was in hospitality. It teaches communication, quick thinking, hard work. Major hotel chains and tourism attractions employ lawyers, accountants, engineers, sales and marketing. It''s not just making beds and serving tables, but for many of those best paid professionals, that''s where they started. And I''ve seen first-hand how hospitality careers are valued across Europe.

 I''ve visited Swiss hospitality schools, which are world class, drawing students from across the globe to prestigious courses. I recently set up a hospitality excellence fund with my local college, privately funded to help our students compete nationally to raise their game. We talk about ambition for Wales. So, why can''t we compete with those Swiss colleges? Why can''t we say proudly from the very top that hospitality in Wales is one of the best starts to a career? Where is the drive? Where is the ambition? Well, maybe it starts now.

 In this Chamber, by supporting this motion and removing those two barriers—one fiscal, one regulatory—that the previous Government has put in the way of that sector''s success, we need to be working cross-party to create an environment that encourages private business to invest in our tourism offer, high-value accommodation, world-class visitor attractions. Let''s liberate the tourism sector, not strangle it with taxes and regulation. Llywydd, I commend the motion to the Senedd.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Louise Emery', '', '12182', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=11702&l=en', NULL, 154.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets; housing_supply; planning_system', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'holiday lets; housing supply; permitted development; planning system; second homes; tourism tax; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.688195', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767063', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
