Forward work programme – Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee Forward work programme – Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee Consultation: Priorities Consultation: Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee Issue Details Related Meetings The forward work programme of the Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee sets out the work which the Committee is intending to carry out in the following weeks and months. Any documents published indicate the current intentions of the Committee at the time of publishing, however the programme may change at any time to respond to external events. Type: For information First published: 15/06/2026 Consultations Priorities Consultation: Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee (running until 18/09/2026) Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47634', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 98.0, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Consultation open', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee; consultation; consultations', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:47.583055', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47634', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Yes, it''s going to take a while, because we are talking about something that is hugely important—the future of our nation. We can''t just do it in a week or two; we do have to have those conversations and we need to bring people with us on that journey. But that doesn''t mean that we aren''t doing other things too. Just tomorrow, I will be meeting the town-centre taskforce, which will be gathering for the first time and that will bring clear recommendations forward by the beginning of next year on some of the challenges facing our town centres, with recommendations on ways of taking action in order to improve our high streets. And there are a number of other, similar issues that are ongoing as we speak.

 I''ve mentioned the taskforce, I''ve mentioned social housing, there is work happening on retrofitting homes and so on—and that''s just in my portfolio. Every Minister can talk to you about at least 10 other things that they are working on today in order to deliver change. But we do have to think of the destination and the future too and not forget, in the midst of those day-to-day important things that we''re doing to improve people''s lives, that we have to also have a clear vision for building our nation so that it is a nation that is fit for the future, and that brings together the aspirations of everyone here so that we can be far more ambitious in how we go about planning for the future. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16264', '2026-09-29', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: The Revision of the National Development Framework and National Conversation', '16264', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16264?startPos=10336&l=en', NULL, 88.0, 'High', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency; social_housing', 'Housing system; Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'retrofitting; social housing', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:59.254461', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16264&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773023', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Every resident in Wales deserves to know their home is safe, that identified risks are being addressed swiftly, and that they will not be left facing years of uncertainty while Governments debate responsibility. Progress must be measured by outcomes. For too long, many people across Wales have faced uncertainty about the safety of their homes, the costs they may face and the timescales for remediation.

 We must ensure that those responsible for building failures contribute towards putting them right. Residents who bought or rented homes in good faith should not be left carrying the burden of mistakes made by others. I note the announcement of the Wales interim measures alarm grant, which will provide support for leaseholders facing the costs of interim alarm systems. Now, while any assistance for affected residents will be welcome, leaseholders will want clarity on how many people are expected to benefit, how quickly support will be available, and whether the scheme will fully address the costs many residents continue to face as a consequence of failures for which they bear no responsibility.

 Looking now at remediation orders, as well as the need to hold developers to account, the key test is not how many powers are available, but how quickly unsafe buildings are fixed and how many families are able to move on with their lives. There are concerns about the pace of progress. Clearly, we''ve heard them all today, and you acknowledge those yourself. But people need confidence that meaningful action is being taken now, and that remediation will not be delayed. As always in Wales, we need action, not more bureaucracy.

 Minister, there is also a wider challenge here. You have made it clear that increasing housing supply is one of your priorities, with ambitions for 20,000 new social homes by 2030. Now, while increasing supply is important, quantity must never come at the expense of quality. And some of you may have heard Radio Wales this morning, where they were talking about snagging—so many new houses that have got so many faults. Well, we can''t have poorly built houses in the first place, and the lessons of past building failures must be fully embedded in future development, so that every new home is built to the highest standard of safety, quality and resilience.

 At the same time, Wales faces a housing crisis. We need more homes, but we need safe homes. We need faster development, but we also need public confidence that safety standards are being rigorously upheld. These objectives are not in conflict. They must go hand in hand. We also recognise that public confidence depends on ensuring that all significant safety risks are addressed, whether they relate to cladding, asbestos, reinforced autoclaved aerated concrete or wider structural concerns. People deserve safe homes, clear information and swift action when problems are identified. Therefore, can you, Minister, confirm how many leaseholders are expected to benefit from the Wales interim measures alarm grant? What assessment has been made of whether the scheme will meet the costs faced by affected residents? And what assurances can you provide that remediation work and developer compliance will accelerate well before the planned registration of the category 1 buildings, as you mentioned, in December 2027?

 Ultimately, success will not be judged by the number of strategies, working groups or announcements; it will be judged by whether families can sleep safely in their homes and whether the public can have confidence that the failures of the past are not repeated. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning: Building Safety Programme Update', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=18121&l=en', NULL, 143.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'building_safety_and_leasehold; housing_supply', 'Planning & place; Property & energy', 'Sector bodies; Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', '', 'building safety; cladding; housing supply; leaseholders; remediation orders; social homes', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.967178', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771117', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, I think setting the target, as we''ve done as a Government in the programme for government, of increasing the share of Welsh public sector procurement that is retained within Wales, that''s an important contribution to meeting the productivity mission, giving those opportunities to Welsh firms at all levels, and making sure they''re available to Welsh small and medium-sized enterprises in particular, through the way that we structure procurement and by giving them support to enable them to access those opportunities. And if they''re able to access them in the public sector, of course, that builds up their capability to go for contracts in the private sector as well, and also to secure contracts outside of Wales. So, there are many, many benefits, I think, to having a procurement policy that is aligned with delivering your wider economic and social goals.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16245', '2026-09-30', 'Adam Price', '', '5030', '', '', 'Economy, Energy and Connectivity Committee', '2. General Ministerial Scrutiny: Enterprise, Connectivity and Energy', '16245', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16245?startPos=800&l=en', NULL, 17.2, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-10-02T06:30:29.663481', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16245&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773982', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 And last week, there was an appearance of going even further, with Government Ministers appearing to cast doubts on the future of Flying Start. Whether or not that suggestion proves accurate, it has caused significant concern among those people delivering and benefiting from the programme. Will the First Minister take the opportunity right now to provide clarity and guarantee that funding for Flying Start will continue, as well as the prioritisation of those most in need? Children in some of Wales''s most deprived communities should not pay the price for the Government''s failure to properly cost its election promises.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=11133&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:20.090741', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771010', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

