 In terms of the MOU, that is a paper that will come from the UK Government; they lead on that, because it''s they who have committed to doing that. That probably won''t provide a legislative status for Sewel, but the intention is to improve the way that they collaborate with us, to make the process clearer and to ensure that we have clear timelines as to when information is shared. And that, in turn, should help the Government to analyse what''s in the legislation, and then help the Senedd to come to a conclusion—to have sufficient time to come to a conclusion—on whether they want to pass an LCM or not.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16278', '2026-10-01', 'Gareth Morgan', '', '', '', '', 'Constitution, Justice and External Affairs Committee', '2. Ministerial scrutiny session: Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16278', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16278?startPos=501&l=en', NULL, 17.2, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'LCM', 0, '2026-10-05T15:05:42.674340', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16278&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774938', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 It''s the cumulative effect of doing absent voters, overseas voters, of doing people who''ve got high churn, in a quick timescale, I think, which is probably the biggest area of risk to manage in this particular time frame. Over a longer time frame, in terms of future elections, I think that all becomes more manageable. So, I think the large risk rise is in the current situation, with the implementation for 2027, with all of those factors that are sitting with electoral registration teams. They are manageable, but they need really tight management, and reviewing how that''s gone is probably critical because of the fact that it''s the first time and, also, those delivery risks are not insignificant.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Ian Bancroft', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=5266&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.392077', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769962', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 My concern is that, obviously, the empty homes loan is a fantastic tool in our toolbox, but it is coming to an end in 2030. So, something similar needs to replace it. If, in Flintshire, the grant''s not working or it hasn''t taken off, then ideally we would like to continue with the loan scheme. Because I''d say that, in the last six months, I''ve had about £0.5 million-worth of requests, which I''ve had to decline because they''re not getting the five years interest free. So, it''s something that we need to consider.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'James Bolton', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=5743&l=en', NULL, 184.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; housing_supply; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty homes; empty homes grant; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.598620', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775415', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But the point—. You asked about targets; we''re reviewing the targets system as a whole at the moment. Health boards have several priorities and targets, and there is a view that there are too many of them, and we have to see whether it''s possible to decrease the number of targets and to make clear what our expectations are, instead of there being too many targets in the system. So, we are reviewing that at the moment.

 We would welcome and appreciate, truth be told, any recommendations that could be made, be they from the cancer community or from you here, on where we should look, because so much work is being done by the Government at the moment. The programme for government is going to be published; the 100 days has passed. A little bit of work like this, which would provide added value, is something that we would very much appreciate, and, I think, something that would be welcomed, because it''s not necessarily something that''s been factored in, in terms of cancer targets specifically, by us at the moment, because we are going to be developing a cancer strategy in due course and we''re going to try to improve quality and outcomes when it comes to cancer care. So, that''s something that should be looked at, and we would welcome it.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16251', '2026-09-17', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', '', '269', '', '', 'Health and Social Care Committee', '2. General scrutiny session with the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care, the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health and the Deputy Minister for Social Care, Mental Health and Women’s Health', '16251', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16251?startPos=2128&l=en', NULL, 17.2, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:14.172154', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16251&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770047', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The target audience, I believe, was to approach people who are in ownership of properties to get them to work on their properties and bring them back into use. But what we''ve found, especially in my area, is that a lot of people purchasing the properties have enquired about the scheme and, of course, they''re not in control, or they''re unaware of the status of the property. The owners may not have adequately informed the council of what the status of the property is, which has led to a lot of cases being declined. But if you look at the amount of people who have enquired about the scheme and applied initially, it gives you a good indication that there is interest there.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Osian Jones', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=5624&l=en', NULL, 155.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.578988', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775488', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 What these questions demonstrate, I think, Minister, is that what''s actually needed is wholesale reform of the business rates system in its totality. There was a commitment to that effect in Welsh Labour''s manifesto, just as there was in Plaid Cymru''s manifesto. I know that this new Government loves a review, it loves a commission. I wonder, Minister, if you could explain why your announcement today—welcome though it broadly is—is simply making tweaks at the margins and not getting into the more comprehensive changes that your party promised before the election. Our offer, as Welsh Labour, is to work with you so that we can make the system fair and so that we can incentivise businesses and local authorities to grow the value of NDR receipts and to drive wider economic growth. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=10175&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates; non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.112504', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769224', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I know from my unsuccessful attempt to purchase a ticket to see Oasis how much visitors are prepared to pay to purchase these golden tickets. If you''ve travelled from North America to see Taylor Swift, my guess is you''re not going to worry about paying a visitor levy of £1.30.

 Here''s the mad thing, for all the visitors that come to Cardiff, often as part of events that the council is delivering, for all the money they spend in local bars, restaurants, hotels, for all the overnight clean-up operations required after 70,000 people have partied in the Principality, the council receives not one penny of income, thanks to the completely centralised taxation system we have in this country. 

 A visitor levy, estimated to raise about £3.5 million a year for Cardiff, starts to change that calculation so that the council raises revenue from major events to in turn fund a better experience for visitors and residents alike. And it will also allow Cardiff to attract new events to the city and also better market ourselves internationally. That was the calculation in proposing a levy.

 In the extensive consultation carried out with key stakeholders and the general public, of the 2,000 responses received, 62 per cent supported those proposals, recognising how the money would be ring-fenced to support the visitor economy. It''s not a tourism tax, it''s an investment in tourism. It''s a modest contribution from visitors towards services, infrastructure and attractions that make Wales such a desirable place to visit in the first place.

 Elwyn Vaughan references—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=14092&l=en', NULL, 82.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; tourism tax; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.987798', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767117', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 There are two approaches to that. There''s the operational stuff—fire and rescue, the Welsh Government''s role, military support, all that kind of thing, and we can talk about that. But then we do have the longer term strategic interventions that we need to plan now, such as encouraging land management in a way that helps mitigate some of these risks. We already have certain sources of funding available to farmers—for example, when you think of drought, to look at sinking additional boreholes, creating lagoons for water et cetera. The sustainable agricultural loan scheme as well is out there to help farms become more resilient in the face of climate change, and maybe help some of these. We''re looking at doing more to encourage drought-resistant crops, which retain more moisture in the ground, through the sustainable farming scheme. There''s work ongoing on peatlands, for example, and rewetting, which can all help avoid finding ourselves in those situations. But when it does happen, then clearly there are public health consequences.

 Again, I''ll defer to Rhodri in relation to the Act, because I think those are circumstances or situations that arise that maybe hadn''t been considered at the time of the Act particularly, but obviously they would be relevant in terms of protecting people''s health.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=489&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.343379', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771763', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Now, when it comes to the bursary scheme, once again, may I just remind you that this wasn''t part of any negotiations that you brought forward or discussions that you brought forward for the supplementary budget, which you voted against as a party, in order to improve health outcomes in Wales. I am absolutely more than happy to discuss any options that you might have and consider their impact and assess how they might be included, but I just remind you that, as we have been informed by the Cabinet Minister for Finance, there are massive in-year pressures currently in the health service, and those pressures are only going to get worse as time goes on because of settlements coming down from London and the fact that we don''t have a fair funding formula for Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', '', '269', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=1214&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:04.785911', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768044', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 This year, I don''t think there will be an underspend at all. In fact, we''re probably going to be in a position where, internally, we have to try and work out how we''re going to invest more money in this area, because the costs of the access to the tool have risen considerably, above inflation. So, we''re in a position, really, where—. And we''ve got more bodies who want to join the national fraud initiative as well, which is great, great for Wales. But what that does mean, of course, is that costs are going up, and so, at some point in the future, we may need to talk to you about that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Ann-Marie Harkin', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Audit Wales - Scrutiny of Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26 and the Annual Plan 2026-27: Evidence Session 1', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=2076&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Delivery bodies', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.137501', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774352', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The big thing for me, in and amongst everything that I''ve heard from everybody, particularly the younger Members of the Senedd, is that we need to understand that this is about the social and economic issues and bringing them together. If we are not going to be doing anything very real in relation to building more homes that are affordable, addressing the issues around renters'' rights, for example, if we are not going to be tackling the big stuff around climate change and so forth, there isn''t going to be a future for anyone. And I worry, more often than not, about my own children, and even when I was first even considering having children, I was thinking, ''Am I doing the right thing?'' And now I look at my kids, one’s 15 and one’s 13, and I''m thinking, blimey—. Now I get to be in this building. And my daughter keeps reminding me, ''Mum, it''s because of you lot that we''re in this situation.'' So, I get it from home on a daily basis, so when I walk in—. Look, for me, what I would say, and I''ll ask the Minister, is: can you please report back to the Senedd after the discussion that you have with Milburn? Many people that I have spoken to, from lots of different organisations, including the TUC themselves, have said that it''s good that we''ve had that review, but there were still gaps even there. And I think that there''s clearly more work to be done.

 I just want to say there have been lots of comments made about immigration, and we can talk about these things all day long, but the data doesn''t stack up. And I think that I very much do welcome the fact that we have also heard, of course, from Janet, who made it very clear it''s about business rates. And I agree. We need to review business rates. There''s more work that needs to be done there. Actually, immigration has been a good thing for both Wales and for the country as a whole.

 What I would say is that, in terms of public procurement, specifically—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=11645&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'business rates', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.848706', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768304', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 You told me that specific performance measures were something you were planning to raise with the UK Government, that the UK veterans strategy offered a potential way forward, and that further measures would be explored. We are now several months further on, so I''m asking you again: can you give this Senedd one measurable outcome, just one measurable outcome, showing that veterans in Wales are materially better off because of the armed forces covenant on houses, homelessness, healthcare or employment? And will you now commit to publishing Welsh performance measures against which you, as the Minister responsible, can actually be held accountable?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Cristiana Emsley', '', '12185', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=1200&l=en', NULL, 49.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'homelessness', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:20.202642', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773486', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We''re not coming across those properties until we''re getting complaints about them. You speak to neighbours, you go out on site and you can''t even get to the front door, let alone live in the property. So, it is an issue. How vast that issue is, we don''t know; I guess that would need further investigation. But, for us on the ground, it is a problem.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Georgina Wayman', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=5948&l=en', NULL, 155.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; council tax; council tax premium; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.631714', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775514', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
