10 August 2026', 'https://www.gov.wales/regulations-designating-building-safety-authorities', NULL, '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', '10 August 2026 Open consultation Building and planning', '', '', '2026-10-16', 147.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'building_safety_and_leasehold; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Property & energy', '', 'Consultation open', 'Building Safety (Wales) Act; building safety; consultation; we want your views', 0, '2026-09-11T15:28:44.915123', 'consultations:regulations-designating-building-safety-authorities', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I am aware that there are several pieces of legislation that have already been passed and we''ll have to look on a case-by-case basis at each one of those and go through them. Of course, it isn''t the kind of thing where you say, ''Well, the previous Government has said this; we therefore say something different.'' Not at all. It''s purely possible—. For example, the seed of the tribunal Bill was sown by the previous Government, and it''s entirely acceptable to me and others, and I''m sure—hopefully—to the Senedd as a whole. By the same token, we will be looking on a case-by-case basis at every single one of them, and where it is current, where it does what we need it to do, we will accept it. Perhaps there''ll be minor changes in regulations, I don''t know. But as a whole, certainly, that''s the way forward: to look first on a case-by-case basis.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=2032&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.407683', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770379', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 And the follow-up from that then is, in safeguarding cases, domestic violence cases, where people do want that anonymity, if the 28 days have been exceeded, what flexibility is there then to ensure that people can take their names off after the 28 days? Because there''ll be issues arising within the 28 days for some people—they''ll be fleeing domestic violence or what have you. So, we have to have some guarantees of flexibility, I think. Otherwise, I''m concerned, if we have a very hard 28-day cut-off, that we will see people slipping through the net. So, I''m looking for reassurance, really, that those issues are on the radar and there is some flexibility.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Marc Jones', '', '350', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=1289&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.883276', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769868', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 One of the things that this Government really wants to do is work cross-portfolio. I see many, many opportunities within culture and sport to support the social justice agenda, specifically, as you mentioned, on cohesion, but in many other ways, too. I see it, again, as essential—being able to provide children who grow up in poverty with access to those opportunities. We know, very often, when you haven''t got a lift to get to that away game, then that can cause you problems, and if you can''t afford the kit, et cetera. My Cabinet colleague Heledd Fychan and I are having those conversations, and I would very much want to make sure that sport is something that we address within cohesion.

 One of the things that we do within cohesion—I was talking about volunteering in the third sector—is the community facilities programme, and that''s really important in this. We provide capital grants to community organisations, to enhance their facilities, of all types, which includes sporting organisations, because we recognise, although sport isn''t—. When I was first looking at it, I was thinking, ''Oh, why am I giving money to sports clubs?'' I completely understand why I''m giving money to sports clubs, because they are absolutely essential to this agenda. I don''t know if, Andrew, you just want to give a little bit of detail around the community facilities programme, because it does come to that broader point on cohesion as well.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6597&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.248508', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771317', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 The ongoing costs, the recurring costs, will be those that fall to local authorities. So, in the first year, we''ve done that as a specific grant because it''s a one-off new process that''s being met. Then, in subsequent years, which is—. The Cabinet Minister recently re-signed the strategic partnership agreement with the Welsh Local Government Association. That commits to meeting new burdens and putting it into the revenue support grant, where we should. So, we shouldn''t be sending lots of small pockets of money to local authorities to do individual bits of work. So, the RSG—. Obviously, local authorities need to meet their statutory duties. The new way of registering people through automatic registration will be a new statutory duty, so they''ll need to meet that through funding that we''re allocating through the RSG in future years. As the Cabinet Minister says, we expect that to go down as the register is more complete year on year.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Michael Kay', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '4. Scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning on the Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=8744&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies; Housing minister', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.998019', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774365', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 One of the fastest growing pressures on councils is additional learning needs. Wales received additional funding for ALN on the back of increased spending in England, but your supplementary budget kept that money in Cardiff Bay, rather than sending it to councils and schools that actually support these children. So, when you bring your revised plans forward this autumn, Minister, will you commit to passing that ALN funding directly to local authorities so councils aren''t forced to drain their reserves to fill that spending gap?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16263', '2026-09-23', 'Francesca O''Brien', '', '12190', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Education and Welsh Language', '16263', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16263?startPos=6167&l=en', NULL, 44.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Opposition housing leads', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:12.108143', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16263&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771509', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Steel is no abstraction to Wales, it is in our bridges and our railways, in the turbines that will power our future and in the history of generations of families from Shotton to Llanwern, from Trostre to Cardiff. It''s a strategic national asset, and a country that cannot make its own steel is a country living at the mercy of others. That is why this Bill matters. It builds on the UK Government''s steel strategy and it provides the power to transfer the shares or property of a steel undertaking into public ownership where a public interest test is met: in simple terms, a safety net beneath an industry too important to lose. The need is real and urgent. Energy costs are among the highest in Europe, global overcapacity is squeezing prices, trade barriers are closing markets and the short-term cost of the transition to the low-carbon steel of the future weighs heavily on the industry of today.

 But I must also be honest with the Senedd: welcoming this Bill is a bittersweet experience. When the blast furnaces at Port Talbot hung in the balance in 2024, we were told by one Government in Westminster and then the next that nationalisation was not on the table. Roughly 2,600 people lost their livelihoods. A year later, when Scunthorpe''s furnaces came under the same threat, Parliament was recalled on a Saturday to keep them burning. Suddenly, every option was on the table. That was the difference, and Wales felt it keenly. I deeply regret that the purpose at the heart of this legislation before us today was not embraced in time to save the blast furnaces at Port Talbot. The belief is wide and deep in Wales that our steel industry was not treated equally. We do not ask for preferential treatment for Wales, but we do expect to be treated with the same fairness and the same dignity.

 Even so, yesterday''s regret must not stop us from protecting tomorrow. I welcome the fact that this tool will now be firmly in the toolbox for Wales, as for every other part of the United Kingdom. And let''s also be clear about what this Bill does not do. It does not guarantee the future of the Welsh steel industry. No statute can do that. What it guarantees is that, if the hour of danger comes again, Government can act within days rather than despair for months, so long as these powers remain on the statute book where they''re needed. We will oppose any attempt to let them quietly slip from it. A sunset clause on these powers contained in the Bill must never become the sunset of an industry.

 We also must be equally clear about something else: a safety net for one producer must not pull the floor from under the others. If British Steel in Scunthorpe is nationalised, a state-owned undertaking must not undermine the commercial position of 7 Steel or Tata Steel. A company relying on the public purse should not price below other producers, nor enjoy an unfair advantage in public procurement, nor draw investment and energy support away from Welsh sites. The Welsh Government will hold the UK Government to this principle at every stage.

 The Bill deals with both reserved and devolved matters, and it will affect all four nations of the United Kingdom. I told the UK Government clearly that the lack of time given to this Government and to the Senedd to scrutinise the Bill was unacceptable. Urgency is never an excuse for disregarding devolution. We did insist on clear commitments to protect Welsh interests and we secured important undertakings as a result, and I will return to them in closing.

 While there is currently no intention or need to use these powers in Wales, it is right that they exist, and it is the UK Government that holds the resources to act at this scale. Let''s remind ourselves what is at stake. Once the electric arc furnace at Port Talbot is operational—the second largest anywhere in the world—nearly half of the UK''s steel-making capacity will be here in Wales. The future of steel in these islands is a Welsh future. The Bill does not secure that future by itself, but it ensures that no Government can ever again claim that the means to defend it, if necessary, is missing. It''s on this basis, clear-eyed and with our expectations on the record, that I recommend the Senedd gives its consent.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Adam Price', 'Cabinet Minister for Enterprise, Connectivity and Energy', '5030', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=20149&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.220284', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767876', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Another specific point: the baseline on the total Senedd Commission budget for last year shows £12.7 million, and then next year we''re looking at £109 million. Of course, last year''s baseline includes £4.5 million of election-related expenditure, which is outside of your control, and it is a one-off every four years, we hope. I wonder if you think a more accurate reflection of a baseline figure shouldn''t necessarily—. It has to be accounted for, it has to be reflected, but, actually, perhaps your baseline from last year was actually closer to £98 million as usual spend rather than £102 million. Is that a fair comment, you think, or not?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Sam Rowlands', '', '8734', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=220&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:36.925200', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777381', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, it is a new period for us as a Senedd, and it is a period of time with a lot of new people here, and to understand, possibly, the nuances of how all of the budgets engage with each other—that will take a little bit of time to settle and for people to understand that. Hopefully, Members will have patience while things settle in, but, of course, I am available to speak with any individual Member who has an interest in this area and wants to know a little bit more about the processes. I have had a few such discussions with individuals around this table and in other places as well. So, we are trying to be as open as possible, in particular in order to be able to manage a budget that is different this time, because of Bay 32, and for people to understand what the different moving parts are within that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '3. Scrutiny of the Senedd Commission Draft Budget 2027-28: Evidence Session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=835&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; draft budget; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:37.027251', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777180', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
