 But, we will continue with our plans to address those waiting lists, and this will directly benefit the people of Powys. We are aware of people who have been on waiting lists for far too long in Powys. They will benefit from this. But, we will also ensure that there is a rapid diagnostic centre for cancer introduced in Powys too so that people are seen earlier, rather than people going to hospitals that are long distances away when they get to stage 4 in their cancer journey, which will be far too late for many of them. We will see them more quickly and ensure that they get an earlier diagnosis too.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', '', '269', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=619&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', 'Bill progress', 'Stage 4; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:04.729565', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768025', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, I can see the benefits of that and I think that is something that could be explored. It may encourage more local authorities to take action in that way because it minimises the risk for the authorities.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Georgina Wayman', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=6388&l=en', NULL, 178.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; local_government_enforcement; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; compulsory purchase; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.705327', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775534', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But planning can also assist us in tackling conflict between policies, where those issues arise. Therefore, I am convinced that we need to give particular attention to this. I did announce last week that we would be consulting on the national development framework. ''Planning Policy Wales'' sits under that. I will also be making a call for evidence around how we can make meaningful progress with what we all agree on, namely the need to further reform the planning system. I would be delighted if you were to bring some of your own ideas forward, around 106 issues, certainly, but on other issues too. I encourage everyone in this Chamber who has ideas as to how we can make the system more accountable to bring their ideas forward.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=6617&l=en', NULL, 121.0, 'High', 'digest', 'planning_system; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'Planning Policy Wales; call for evidence; planning policy; planning system; programme for government', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:12.609409', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776747', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 As I say, it''s disappointing to see yet again more divisive rhetoric from the usual suspects in this Siambr, who are using—[ Interruption .]—I can shout louder than you, believe me—what is a basic human right to stoke fear in our communities and cause division. Shame on you. When we say HMOs, the image that might come to mind may not be particularly pretty. But this image of what can be some of the worst quality, unsafe, overcrowded homes is now being used by Reform once again to scaremonger people about perceived dangers in their communities.

 The situation in some HMOs isn''t ideal. That doesn''t take anything away from the fact that everyone deserves a place to live—a safe, secure and warm place to live. Due to the lack of social homes that so many of our colleagues have mentioned, people are stuck in the cheapest, inadequate private housing options. And Reform are disgracefully arguing again that we need to look after our own—what do the words even mean—while ignoring the fundamental problem that there are not enough good-quality, affordable homes for everyone who needs one. And I would like to pay credit again to the housing Minister for making that point so clearly earlier on today.

 Foreign nationals or not, everyone needs somewhere to live. It is their human right. We need more social housing that meets the needs of everyone in our communities. Scarcity must not be used to pit communities against one another.

 This motion is nonsensical, hateful and inflammatory. And words have real consequences. I have said this time and time again in this Siambr, but, sadly, it bears repeating. [ Interruption .] No. Divisive rhetoric, such as we''ve heard today, only stands to cause division in our communities. And this fearmongering is the sort of thing that leads people to feel even more unsafe in their homes and communities than they already do. People across Wales see this for what it is. They see you for what you''re saying.

 I represent the most diverse constituency in Wales, and its diversity makes Cardiff what it is today. People from around the world have chosen to make Cardiff their home, and the people of Cardiff will not allow you to divide our communities. People, time and time again, have stood up to this divisive rhetoric and shown solidarity with their neighbours.

 Reform are not interested in community cohesion. Reform are not interested in finding housing solutions. Reform are only interested in dividing our communities to distract from the fact that you have no answers to the problems facing the people of Wales. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'Anthony Slaughter', '', '5025', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=16334&l=en', NULL, 137.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'hmos_and_standards; housing_supply; homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place; Private rented sector', 'Opposition housing leads', '', 'HMOs; adequate housing; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.438924', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776958', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 What we''ve also found is, recently, across SRS, we''ve implemented a better communications strategy, so we''re now promoting the work that we do. So, any enforcement action that we take, we''re promoting that via social media and we''re getting people engaging with us then, because they can see, actually, what the end point is. So, I do think it is a very positive thing, but it''s key to promote the work that we''re doing to show people that we are actually going to do it.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Georgina Wayman', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=5081&l=en', NULL, 178.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; local_government_enforcement; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; compulsory purchase; empty properties; enforcement action; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.485591', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775102', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Therefore, given how this has happened in the past and how we would expect it to happen in the future, there would be an assessment of the powers in that Wales Act, what would be considered for devolution—justice, policing, the Crown Estate, whatever those exact powers are. Then we would need a clear assessment, of course, of the costs associated with those powers, and what those costs are, which means how they are administered now in Wales by the Westminster Government, and what the Welsh Government would require to exercise them. And then, as part of that discussion process, we would need a clear statement of what would come to Wales as a new settlement in light of the devolution of those powers.

 There''s quite a bit of detailed work needed to evaluate clearly what those costs are, because we don''t want to be in a situation—and I would say this as Minister for finance—where there is devolution of powers, but none of the money that is even spent today on those powers in Wales is transferred to Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=9721&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:52.044087', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774397', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, going a long way back to 2012, there was a UK Government report on economic regulation across different sectors that was part of what you might remember as the bonfire of the quangos, as it was called, of the UK Government back then, when economic regulation was steered away from being more intrusive towards something that was more arm''s length. The final report of the Independent Water Commission, to my mind, swings the pendulum back the other way.

 In advance of that, we recognised that we need to have more grip on this, which is why we introduced the controls that we did in PR24. But I''m not going to sit here and say that in the period before then, our arm''s-length economic regulation was as much a success as it should have been, because the Independent Water Commission report sets out that it was not.

 What we have done is hold Dŵr Cymru to account through enforcement activity. I think that''s a very poor substitute. Fixing a problem afterwards is a poor substitute for fixing it first. I wish that were not the case, but we have held the company to account, and in ensuring the investment, the record levels of investment that it''s now charging its customers for, does deliver improvements, we have much more of a grip than we had in the past. I recognise I''m giving a ‘jam tomorrow’ answer, but within the framework that we had, we''ve done what we could. The Independent Water Commission report set out that that was not the right framework, and we were at too great an arm''s length from this. We accept that, and that''s why we''re working with Welsh Government for a reform, which is a generational opportunity to improve economic regulation in Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=2717&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:22.084342', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773215', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In terms of PR29 planning more generally, in order to have a PR29 at the end of 2029, we will need to start scrutinising proposals probably towards the end of 2028, which means companies, including Dŵr Cymru, will need to have submitted their business plans well in advance of that. Planning on that has already started and will need to continue. We will be publishing a methodology about how we''re going to approach PR29, including what will be different and Wales-specific for Wales, in spring next year.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16234', '2026-09-30', 'Chris Walters', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '3. Ofwat’s role in the Welsh context - scene setting session', '16234', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16234?startPos=654&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-10-01T06:30:21.823473', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16234&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773134', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I''m just wondering if you had any thoughts on the proposals in Scotland allowing some measures that might be brought forward in terms of allowing local authorities to take temporary possession and bringing the CPO procedures and compensation together. So, just grateful for your thoughts on those. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Rebeca Phillips', '', '12172', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=6348&l=en', NULL, 189.7, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.699278', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775527', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I am very aware of a great number of community groups that are keen to buy and to procure buildings that become empty in their communities, in order to turn them into assets that are of use to the community, to bring life to the community and to the high street quite often as well. I am very aware as well that there are barriers quite often to doing this, and sometimes they lose the opportunity to buy an asset and to transform it for community use. So, I do hope that we all will be able to agree on new legislation that will empower community groups, so that they can identify, in the first place, assets that are in their community that they might want to buy for the future, that we create a register identifying all of those, and that then, when they do come on the market, they get first offer on those properties, and then have an opportunity to make a business case and to continue with the process. That is not happening at the moment and opportunities are being missed.

 I do intend to arrange a series of workshops over the summer now with partners, with communities, in order for us to have more detail in terms of this new legislation. I encourage you all to take part in that and encourage community groups in your constituencies to be part of this new important legislation.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3675&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system', 'Housing minister', '', 'community right to buy; legislative programme; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.032842', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768143', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 One of the issues that I''d emphasise in response to the issue around the fiscal framework is our own borrowing capacity in this context. That''s an issue that I want to take into the fiscal framework review with the UK Government to provide that flexibility. And then there is, as you''ve outlined, again more work that we need to do with those that do have more borrowing powers—local authorities and Welsh Government, even—to think about how we look at that work, look at the ability of other aspects of work as well, when we think about pension holdings in the public sector as well.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=10129&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:52.098325', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774630', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But we do expect all developments planned in Wales to have regard to all relevant planning policy, to be sensitively designed and implemented to avoid and to definitely minimise impacts, at least on communities and the environment, but also to maximise the benefits, because I think there are two sides to this coin. When you get it right, it releases a lot of opportunities economically, et cetera, but when you get it wrong, then those are the risks that sometimes appear.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16265', '2026-09-30', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16265', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16265?startPos=3513&l=en', NULL, 27.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', '', '', 'planning policy', 0, '2026-10-03T13:38:48.151671', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16265&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#773583', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

