 Now, as noted after the first 100 days, the Government is investing £100 million in tackling waiting lists in the NHS. It''s vital for people across Wales, and, of course, in Gŵyr Abertawe, that the Government succeeds in tackling this huge problem, which has faced Wales for far too long. How will the Government ensure that these investments will provide the most effective, and, more importantly, continuous benefit, while delivering the programme for government? Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16313', '2026-10-06', 'Gwyn Williams', '', '5083', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16313', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16313?startPos=5375&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-10-09T13:35:09.938316', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16313&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776031', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In terms of wider engagement, I suppose that''s, potentially, going to be something for the Welsh Government to answer and to look at, in terms of the implementation. So, we''ll be running our usual post-poll evaluation of the elections next year, and we intend for AVR to be a very significant portion of that reporting process. So, it''s something we''re planning on, essentially looking at how we evaluate the pilots and doing the same thing again on a national level, to try and get some data behind opt-out rates, reasons for opt-outs, and hopefully that can help inform some of these conversations and any changes that need to happen, moving forward.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Davies', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=1209&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.866640', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769851', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 I would also encourage Members to look at the work done during the fifth Senedd by the committee that you''re now a member of, which, on a cross-party basis, not from Government, was scrutiny work carried out by previous Members of this Senedd that confirmed that broadcasting isn''t working for Wales as it currently stands, and encouraged Government to take action. So, what I''m doing as a Minister is acting on independent recommendations—listening and acting—and that''s what I hope that any Minister would do in my position.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16261', '2026-09-16', 'Heledd Fychan', '', '426', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Trefnydd, Chief Whip and Cabinet Minister for Culture and Sport', '16261', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16261?startPos=737&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'co-operation agreement', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:16.589926', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16261&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769461', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Members will be aware that the Government is introducing a phased expansion of funded universal childcare, which, at full roll-out, will be the most generous offer in the UK, providing 20 hours of funded childcare each week, for 48 weeks of the year, for all children aged from nine months to four years old.

 In my view, and the view of the Government, and in the view of every expert on poverty and child development, the expansion of childcare is not merely a ''nice to have'', but it''s absolutely essential. Whether or not to expand funded childcare is not a question to discuss. The lack of affordable childcare is a societal problem to solve, and in solving it, we will deliver benefits that span across gender equality, early child development, tackling poverty, supporting our economy, and improving our children''s health and educational outcomes.

 Having stated with pride our aim of delivering a universal childcare offer, and with us now 57 days into this Government term, we have progressed with this and made excellent progress. We have been working on our plans with local government and sector representatives through the newly established expert group, and we have already announced an additional £55 million of funding through the supplementary budget to support the initial phase of the expansion programme.

 As part of the process of developing our manifesto, we carefully costed an expanded childcare offer, and we identified the additional funding required to deliver the full expansion. And now in Government, officials have undertaken further economic modelling.

 On that basis, we are developing clearer costs, considering the assumptions behind them. The modelling undertaken so far suggests that, at today''s prices, the core costs of the full expansion of our offer will require an additional £375 million a year. This is a mid-point estimate, and work is ongoing with our economists and analysts to test and model a range of variables, which includes the costs at each phase of expansion and potential take-up rates.

 As advised by our expansion of childcare expert steering group, we will also take into account not only workforce costs, but a range of factors, including training and development, additional learning needs, local authority support and capital. The level of additional funding required, indicated by Welsh Government economists to date, aligns with the detailed work undertaken by Plaid Cymru in preparation to make this policy commitment.

 Our clear aim is that families of all children from nine months to four years across Wales should be able to access a more affordable, simpler, more consistent childcare offer by the end of this Senedd term. Achieving this aim will require careful sequencing, along with partnership working and ongoing funding decisions, but we are determined to maintain the momentum.

 The latest data, published on 1 July, shows that we are now offering funded childcare to nearly 75 per cent of all two-year-olds across Wales in 2025-26. However, we want to go further and ensure that what was promised by the previous Labour Government, but not delivered, is fully implemented. I want us to reach all our two-year-old children as quickly as possible and I am urging local authorities to complete this first phase by the end of this financial year. This is what families want and deserve.

 At the start of this Government term, three local authorities had succeeded in completing the roll-out of 12.5 hours of funded childcare to all two-year olds. One other local authority has reached every two-year-old child last month. And 16 more local authorities have already confirmed that, with the additional funding that we will invest through the supplementary budget, they''ll be able to move to a full roll-out by the end of this financial year, which demonstrates our commitment to deliver our new offer to families across Wales. Officials are working closely with the remaining two local authorities to understand how they can be supported to deliver this phase at this point.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Sioned Williams', 'Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality', '8670', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality: Update on Early Years Childcare and Play', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=11243&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.274923', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767777', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 ''introduces a more outward-facing, proactive and impact-led approach.''

 Could you expand on how this will be achieved and how the strategic plan for 2026-29 has affected the preparation of the estimate for 2027-28?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Sera Evans', '', '330', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=10371&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.222983', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777654', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Now, governing, as I believe you recognise, is hard, and we do not intend to make your task unnecessarily harder, but we do represent in our party a great Welsh tradition with deep bonds of solidarity across Britain on the basis that we achieve more together than we can achieve alone. And our role in this Chamber is to stand up for the most vulnerable and to be a voice for those who don''t have inherited wealth and privilege, but who graft and work for a better tomorrow. We were therefore disappointed that one of the first things this Plaid Cymru Government did was to water down its offer on free school meals. Plaid Cymru promised in its first 100 days document to commit to expanding free school meals to all secondary school pupils in households claiming universal benefit from September of this year. But by June, of course, just weeks after the election, you stated that this year it would only be available to parents of children in years 7 and 8. No date, indeed, has been given for extending this to years 9, 10 and 11.

 Now, Bevan said that socialism is the language of priorities, and one of your first decisions was that you decided that keeping an election promise to some of our poorest children was not a priority. When over £300 million of ALN consequentials came to Wales, again you decided that the most vulnerable children were not a priority and not a penny was allocated to them. Welsh Labour had published plans in Government and in our manifesto for a negotiating body for school support staff, and despite the Senedd voting in favour of it, your Government has refused to commit to supporting school support staff on this.

 Now, First Minister, you told traditional Labour voters in your election campaign that you were a safe pair of hands for progressive voters, and yet your group is now welcoming right-wing Reformers. And in your first decisions on education and opportunity for everyone, you''ve prioritised expanding Welsh Labour''s childcare offer to millionaires. First Minister, can I ask you today to commit that your policies in this programme for government will not result in the removal or reduction in support aimed at working class or vulnerable children, such as Flying Start?

 Turning to healthcare, under this Plaid Cymru Government, both overall waiting lists and two-year waiting lists are rising again, having fallen for 10 months in a row under Welsh Labour. Before the election, you promised that, within months, the number of people waiting for more than two years would be zero. Can you confirm that the priorities that you set out in this programme for government will not see you renege on another promise—a promise, not just an ambition, that we will see two-year waits reduced to zero within months? And can you confirm how many months that will be?

 Weeks before the election—in an attempt, obviously, to get Wales''s trade union movement on side—you also wrote to Cwm Taf health visitors supporting their call to upgrade all band 6 health visitors to band 7. Ministers, again, have reneged on this promise, and health visitors rightly feel betrayed. So, can I ask you, First Minister, will you now make good on your promise to these workers and to Unite the Union?

 Others who will feel ignored and betrayed by this programme for government are the people of north Wales, as Helen Jenner has already identified, specifically those in Flintshire and Wrexham, because as Wrexham.com have observed, for north-east Wales, a notable feature is absence. It''s as though parts of Wales are already being left behind. Standing up for Wales means uniting the people of Wales, not seeking a course of action that will divide Wales, divide the nation, divide our families and divide our communities, as Brexit did. Standing up for Wales means taking action to improve Wales, not producing a plethora of plans.

 It''s not too late, though. It''s not too late. I ask you, finally, First Minister, to work with us in the spirit that you campaigned, in the spirit not only of constructive politics, but also progressive politics, because when the people of Wales are prioritised, I can assure you that you will find in us a willing and constructive partner.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Ken Skates', 'Leader of Welsh Labour', '267', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=9526&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.843105', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770996', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Wales is dangerously unprepared for the predicted onslaught of extreme weather conditions. Climate response must be integrated as our first line of defence. Welsh Government allocated in its supplementary budget just £1 million for emergency preparedness. Establishing a climate shock fund would be a vital step forward in demonstrating that the Welsh Government is serious about protecting communities this winter, with or without UK Government support. What discussions has the First Minister had with the UK Government on funding these emergency measures? Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Anthony Slaughter', '', '5025', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=1752&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:18.844963', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770746', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 We''ll move on to P-07-1611, ''Allocate the full consequential funding to Additional Learning Needs (ALN)''.

 ''We call on the Welsh Government to allocate and ring-fence the £500m in consequential funding from English SEND spending directly to local authorities and schools for Additional Learning Needs (ALN).

 ''While not legally bound to the same portfolio, this funding exists because of systemic education pressures that do not stop at the border. Ahead of the supplementary budget, this money must go straight to the frontline to support Wales’ most vulnerable learners and stabilise our schools.''

 The submitter is Laura Tams, on behalf of NAHT Cymru. It has 11,451 signatures, with 11,228 from Wales. I invite not Janet but—checks notes—Vikki to discuss the petition and any actions you wish the committee to take.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16259', '2026-09-24', 'Jason O''Connell', '', '12158', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. New Petitions', '16259', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16259?startPos=684&l=en', NULL, 17.2, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-25T12:02:31.018608', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16259&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772177', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But it also means, of course, working across every party and every part of the country, and your collaboration and co-operation as Members will be a crucial part of that work. I''m eager to have a constructive relationship with you, contributing to your inquiries over the coming years, and to consider them carefully as we act as a Government.

 The Welsh language is at the heart of the kind of Wales that we as a Government want to create—an inclusive, creative, confident Wales that''s proud of its own identity. You will see from the paper that we''ve already started this work by improving access to Welsh-medium education at every step of the journey, from the early years to statutory education and further education and higher education as well.

 But the Welsh language is not just a language of the classroom, it''s alive in our communities, and we are determined to empower the areas where it flourishes as a social language. The work of examining how best to do this has already started, and we''ve already started work to extend the Welsh language standards so that more and more sectors offer services through the medium of both languages. Alongside this, it''s crucial that we, throughout the whole of Wales, take the Welsh language to people, wherever they are and in doing whatever they do already—on the playing field, in the workplace, in clubs, using services and in digital spaces as well, very importantly.

 Opportunities to use the Welsh language must be easy to access, must be timely and must be fun. That''s why our response as a Government to the second stage of the Commission for Welsh-speaking Communities report will be announced very soon. I want to do more to enable the Welsh language to take root in all of our policies. The work on health, culture, the economy and others that are outlined in the paper and in our programme for government provides a clear direction in terms of where we want to go, and there is much more to come in that regard.

 Our new strategy for the Welsh language will set out clearly how we intend to do more to create more opportunities to use the Welsh language in all aspects of our lives today, now, not just by 2050. Alongside the strategy, we will also be launching a consultation on the national framework for Welsh-medium education and the code to describe ability and skills in the Welsh language, all of which to implement the Welsh Language and Education (Wales) Act 2025 in full. That Act provides an opportunity to transform Welsh-medium education and to light the way towards the million speakers and beyond. So, I thank all of the parties for their unanimous support for that Act in the last Senedd, and I also thank the parties of this new Senedd for the positive response to this work in the Chamber.

 There is a great deal to do, therefore: taking the Welsh language to people, making it fun and easy to use the Welsh language; improving access to Welsh-medium education in every part of the nation and increasing the proportion of young people and children who receive Welsh-medium education; and, through the new strategy, placing the Welsh language at the heart of every public policy and the portfolio of every Minister, including health, housing, planning, transport, education, culture and the economy. And as we proceed with that work, I''ll be depending on your scrutiny, and I look forward to working with you to ensure that the Welsh language genuinely does belong to every one of us. 

 In the meantime, I very much hope that the paper that we submitted provides a comprehensive picture of our work to date, and I very much hope that it shows how we are taking steps immediately now to increase how much Welsh is part of people''s lives today. We are acting more swiftly and explicitly for the Welsh language in a co-ordinated manner, a strategic manner, so that we can make progress powerfully together to place the Welsh language at the heart of daily life for everyone in Wales. Thank you very much.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16239', '2026-09-28', 'Anna Brychan', '', '12145', '', '', 'Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee', '2. Ministerial scrutiny session: Cabinet Minister for Education and the Welsh Language', '16239', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16239?startPos=307&l=en', NULL, 63.2, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; programme for government', 0, '2026-09-29T13:11:13.826678', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16239&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772308', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

