 The organisation sees AI, obviously, as a tool that can help improve accessibility, efficiency and service delivery, but not replace the human judgment in cases. As I said, the focus on it has been to use it responsibly, supporting both staff and complainants, ensuring that fairness, accountability and public trust remain in what we do. I think, as has been said previously, we do recognise that there''s an increasing number of people using AI to help prepare and present their complaints in a more structured way. That''s important as an accessibility tool that can help people engage with our service.

 At the same time, again, I think, as we''ve mentioned previously, any information submitted to us, sometimes the AI-assisted content may not always be complete. So, there are some issues raised around that. That, I think, is why we''re continuing to assess the substance and evidence of every complaint, as Katrin said, on its merits, regardless of how it''s received or how it''s written.

 As AI becomes more widely used, we''ll continue to adapt our services and digital tools to ensure that they remain accessible and responsive to the needs of complainants, but also trying to help our staff be more efficient. An example is we are already using approved tools such as Microsoft Copilot to support tasks like research, summarising information and administrative work, again trying to help our staff be more efficient while keeping that human judgment at the centre of everything that we try and do. It does not make complaint decisions and cannot determine any complaint outcomes. I don''t know whether anyone wants to add to that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Sarah King', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=11811&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.428409', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777724', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
