 Before the closure of Tata Steel''s blast furnace at Port Talbot, Wales''s steel accounted for 60 per cent of total UK crude steel output and primary iron, it employed over 4,000 people—a figure that has now sadly halved—it contributed around £3.2 billion per year to the Welsh economy, and it represented approximately 3 per cent of Wales''s total economic output. These figures demonstrate just how important the steel industry is to Wales, not only to our economy, but to thousands of families and communities that have depended on it for generations. Any decisions affecting the future of this sector will have lasting consequences.

 Of course, the Conservatives have always recognised the vital importance of our Port Talbot Tata Steel site and, to help secure its long-term future, it was, in fact, the UK Conservative Government that agreed a joint investment package with Tata Steel. Rather than taking the company into public ownership, it chose to work in partnership with the private sector to deliver a sustainable future for steel making in south Wales. That package included a £500 million Government grant to help fund a £1.25 billion transformation to a modern, greener electric arc furnace. This was a substantial commitment by the UK Conservative Government, demonstrating that Government can play an active role in supporting strategic industry whilst also encouraging private investment and innovation. Whilst it is important that Governments are able to step in and support our vital steel industry when circumstances demand it, we must never forget the benefits that come from Governments working constructively alongside the private sector. Partnership can deliver investment, safeguard jobs, modernise production, help secure the long-term future of our steel industry, and without making nationalisation the default option. Our focus should actually here remain on creating the conditions that encourage investment, protect skilled jobs, and ensure that Welsh steel continues to have a strong future.

 Whilst I do understand the intentions behind this Bill, my Welsh Conservative colleagues and I cannot and will not support this LCM.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Janet Finch-Saunders', '', '212', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21393&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'LCM; Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.364377', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767880', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Moving on, if there''s time, I''d like to come back to some of the points on specific legislation that you mentioned, Dafydd, but just a question, perhaps, for the Counsel General on the legislation passed during the sixth Senedd that has implications for the Welsh Government during the seventh Senedd, which would require further legislation if the Government didn''t want to pursue those implications. For example, this is a possibility for the legislation passed with regard to council tax and the reforms that are to come in the legislation in 2028. Is that a conversation that the Government is having? What needs to change? Or are we to take it that anything that was passed by the previous Government is going to be carried forward and delivered by this Government?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=1916&l=en', NULL, 115.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee; council tax; legislative programme', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.377326', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770395', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, we have a number of initiatives in train at the moment to help to foster positive connections, to prevent hate and to provide support to those people who experience those kinds of hate crimes. They include the community cohesion programme, Hate hurts Wales—that campaign—and, of course, the Wales Hate Support Centre, and wider initiatives that try to foster resilience in face of some of these damaging and harmful narratives. I spoke earlier about social media. Of course, those aren''t powers that we have to regulate at the moment, but we are looking at ways of identifying false and fake narratives that are damaging and harmful, and then working with partners to stand against those narratives.

 We also have to think about those broader matters within the portfolio. Spaces—I believe that we need to have spaces where people can come together, where people can see each other as human beings and not place them in silos or boxes where people feel that they can''t identify with each other''s experiences. It''s very important that we do identify with each other''s experiences. So, I think that the voluntary sector has a major role to play in this—bringing communities together, strengthening those connections between people from different backgrounds and different experiences to foster that sense of cohesion, of belonging and of empathy. I think that that will help, perhaps, to decrease some of those divisions, to decrease those feelings of disconnection from each other, which I think is very harmful to our society. Andrew, I don''t know if you wanted to come in briefly on that policy.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=5927&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.178881', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771305', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
