 The questions that I have are: would you as a Cabinet Minister possibly agree to look at alternative models for how we''re going to fund this sector? More permanent contracts. The tender system is fine for ordering toilet roll or for road improvements and so on, but I don''t think that it''s fit for purpose for this particular sector. Will you look at how the sector, when there are contracts, if that is the ongoing situation, how an uplift can be made on an annual basis? I know of organisations that haven''t had an uplift in 10, 15 years. And, to provide greater clarity, the advocacy sector that I worked within have tried to contact a Minister for that Minister to come in and discuss how the situation is impacting them. Would you commit to meeting with them? That would give clarity not only on this sector, but also how it''s affecting the sector more broadly.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Elfed Williams', '', '12165', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6990&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.310994', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771272', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In addition to that, there is a Government commitment to carry out an analysis of all of the sports assets, including swimming pools, that we have across Wales to truly understand (1) what''s been lost and (2) what is at risk of being lost, because many of our swimming pools are very old, in order to put a long-term plan into place. Everyone should have access to swimming lessons. Everyone in Wales should have access to swimming pools. But, unfortunately, there have been cuts, and that''s why this work must proceed. I would encourage any Member here today who is passionate, as am I and as is my party, about ensuring that every child in primary school in Wales has a right to access swimming lessons free of charge, in order to learn such an important skill, to support the supplementary budget today.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Heledd Fychan', '', '426', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=6562&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.800071', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767718', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Like I say, we have done it; we''re pleased to have done it. There have been many challenges, and I think anything that can be done to streamline that process, make it easier, quicker and more cost-effective to reduce the risk to local authorities, we would welcome, to enable us to take more. I think the easier it is to get through the process, the more local authorities will use that power. Financially, it''s been very costly to the local authority, and it''s reducing those risks to the authority to enable us to pursue it.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Georgina Wayman', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 2', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=4672&l=en', NULL, 178.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; local_government_enforcement; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; compulsory purchase; empty homes; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.418954', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774777', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Today''s session marks the committee''s first general scrutiny session with the Minister and provides an opportunity for Members to discuss the Welsh Government''s priorities, challenges and objectives within the portfolio. We''re very grateful for the information provided in advance, but before we get into the questions, would you mind introducing yourselves for the committee?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=52&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.551741', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771218', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 ''A Plaid Cymru government will address key challenges in the Additional Learning Needs (ALN) system in Wales''.

 But today, Plaid are asking us to vote for a budget that rejects an opportunity to give a huge boost to ALN children, and that is what this Chamber is asking for. It''s about time that ALN and education had a significant boost given the educational outcomes in Wales. They are way behind the rest of the UK. Education, quite frankly, has always been a poor relation to the NHS, the budget of which now takes up half the Welsh Government''s expenditure, so this would''ve been an opportunity to rebalance the funding across services in the two main spending areas in Wales. But the truth is that Plaid are using a windfall of consequential funding to plug a financial black hole hidden in its manifesto. It''s being used to help fund the first phases of implementation, and without this windfall, Plaid would''ve been lost.

 We aren''t expecting the entire Government budget to be rewritten, although it is not unusual for new UK Governments—indeed, Governments across the world—to issue wide-ranging budgets a few months after they''ve been elected and taken office. But to limit this supplementary budget to the lines table demonstrates a very low appetite to do things differently. Plaid will say that the allocation of moneys reflects their manifesto priorities, but funding their commitments largely from a one-off windfall is not sustainable, and there is a significant lack of detail as to how that funding will make a real impact on the day-to-day lives of the Welsh people. How will it make a difference? What improvements will we see and when?

 Llywydd, it is not just the budget numbers that don''t add up; the votes are not adding up for Plaid either this evening. The Tories and Labour have said they''ll oppose this budget, so assuming that there''s not a secret deal struck at the eleventh hour, this budget will fall. And it will fall because Plaid are being incredibly stubborn over an amount of money that its First Minister has previously labelled ''small change''. Reform is willing to offer Plaid Cymru a lifeline, and it will only cost you some small change, as you call it—the small change needed to guarantee student nurses a job; some small change taken back from overseas ventures and ploughed back into Welsh public services; and some serious small change to make a huge investment into ALN, which is badly needed. And after all, that is why we have this consequential funding coming to Wales.

 If Plaid can commit to those commonsense priorities, priorities that have a huge amount of support across Wales, I''m sure that we can go into recess on a high note. But if you lose this vote, you will have tripped at the very first, very low hurdle. We will be heading into the summer questioning whether this Government has the ability and the support to continue leading Wales. Plaid will need to have a long, hard think over the summer and come back willing to compromise. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Dan Thomas', '', '12147', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=24832&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.670648', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767905', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But my real question was about the line on ''proper management of our budget'', which is, I think, amber, because as opposed to being unqualified accounts and ''substantial'' assurance, it is ''reasonable'' assurance, and I wonder what''s going on there.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16295', '2026-10-08', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Public Services Ombudsman for Wales- Annual Report and Accounts 2025-26, and Estimate 2027-28: Evidence session', '16295', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16295?startPos=10143&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-10-09T13:34:38.171946', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16295&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777612', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 This is not a housing policy; it''s a choice to socialise the cost of a failed system, a transfer of the cost from the people who made the decision to the people who live next door to it. HMOs for foreign nationals have become the mechanism by which hard political choices are avoided. Wales does not have a spare stock of family homes. We have long waiting lists. We have young couples forced to live with their parents into their 30s. We have veterans and key workers who can''t get housing in the town where they were born. And we have a licensing regime for HMOs that''s treating it as a growth industry.

 Every family home converted into a HMO is a home taken out of the market for a local family and put into a market that pays better, where the tenants are transient, the houses are crowded, and rental income is backed by a public contract. Landlords did not invent this incentive—Government did. So, let us be precise about what this motion does and does not do.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', 'John Clark', '', '12169', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Reform UK Debate: Access to HMOs and social housing for foreign nationals', '16314', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16314?startPos=15962&l=en', NULL, 99.0, 'High', 'digest', 'private_rented_sector; hmos_and_standards; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', '', '', 'HMO; HMOs; landlords; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.392752', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#776936', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Reform went into the election supporting greater surgical and diagnostic capacity, stronger literacy and numeracy in schools, reform of the additional learning needs system, more vocational opportunities for young people and a stronger future for family farms up and down the country. But the real test of this programme is not how many promises are made today, it''s whether we can come back to this Chamber in a year, two years and four years from now and measure whether anything has actually improved.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Helen Jenner', '', '12153', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: ''New Ambition for Wales: Programme for Government 2026-2030''', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=8975&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-26T13:20:19.746272', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770998', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But, this is my first time here as Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning, and I look forward to working with you today, but also over the period to come, and I look forward to the scrutiny sessions that you’ll be undertaking with me with regard to all of the different commitments and all of the different work taking place within my portfolio.

 I''m really grateful to this committee and to the Legislation Committee for scrutinising these regulations in particular. Can I just, first of all, in general, say how important I think it is that we have these regulations in place? We are leading the way in the United Kingdom in terms of rolling out automatic voter registration, which is such an important step in expanding our democracy, and it''s a very positive step to ensure that as many eligible people as possible can take part in the local government elections in 2027.

 To turn specifically to the question you asked with regard to the timetable, it''s very important, I think, that we reach a situation where we have as complete a register as possible before 2027, and we''re working to take advantage of that opportunity. The timetables, or the deadlines, are ambitious, but we''re working closely with key stakeholders and local departments in order to achieve these objectives, drawing on their significant experience of working in challenging conditions to implement electoral reform. This is the first time that we''ve asked local government officials to do the work of rolling out such significant changes, and our thanks go to them for doing that work. That work is ongoing.

 You mentioned the Gould principle. The Gould principle says that we should not change legislation within six months of an election where that change would affect that election. The timetable is tight, but we are within that limit, so all is good.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '4. Scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning on the Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=7899&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister', '', 'Legislation Committee; Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.836044', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774416', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 But, as I see it, and as the Government sees it, we are clearly in favour of devolution for Wales, but we also believe in decentralisation—that is, devolution within Wales too. That is part of our mission as a Government: we want to empower communities and authorities at a local level to take decisions too. So, there is a risk, yes, and we are alive to that risk, but I think that the point of principle is that it''s the Welsh Government and the Welsh Parliament that should set the framework. I would be eager to work with the UK Government and others to ensure that decentralisation within Wales develops too.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16278', '2026-10-01', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Constitution, Justice and External Affairs Committee', '2. Ministerial scrutiny session: Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16278', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16278?startPos=2157&l=en', NULL, 17.2, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'devolution settlement', 0, '2026-10-05T15:05:42.822858', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16278&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774867', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 It can also be helpful in other areas as well, because sometimes things people think of as empty homes turn out not to be; they turn out to have an isolated older person in who really needs some assistance. So, I think it''s a really valuable overall part of the package, really.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Chris Bailey', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '5. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 3', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=14625&l=en', NULL, 155.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; empty homes; empty properties; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:26.556441', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775463', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Amendment has been agreed

 Click to see vote results', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16314', '2026-10-07', '', '', '', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Voting Time', '16314', '', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'HMOs; social housing', 0, '2026-10-10T14:39:13.604519', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16314&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#777002', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In the next few weeks, there''ll be a lot of training, a lot of raising awareness, and perhaps they haven''t engaged as they should have because they''ve been so busy with the Senedd elections, by-elections, the annual electoral registration canvas, that it''s not been something that has been on their horizon or ''We didn''t know if it was going to happen or not.'' So, it is possible. As all things, if the right resources are there, we will turn it around, but it''s obviously now at risk.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Sandra Matthews', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=6403&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.592884', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769967', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 What you''re using is a proportion of what is available for the manpower across Wales in order to develop the cases to use some of these more almost draconian enforcement measures in order to bring them back into use. In these blight cases, which require a lot of co-ordination between departments in councils, the reason I think you had a lot of your money not spent is because you don''t have enough coal-face people that are actually doing the spending and doing the operational side of things, so you''ve got a bottleneck there, which needs to be invested in and supported.

 The central expertise and support that''s provided and the funding has been essential for the work that has been done across local authorities, because it oils the wheels and makes everything work with regard to taking this legal action, both through training and providing support, not just for empty property staff, but also for their associated legal teams, and briefing appropriate councillors, who may be antagonistic or supportive of the action. And so that needs to continue, but what we''re seeing here is a resource constraint, which is then limiting the available drawdowns on some of that funding, in addition to the fact that these are long, slow processes that periodically require a big chunk of funding, but only periodically.

 You have to speculate to accumulate, so the more of these you get going, as you start moving along the process, you''ll gradually have more of them using up that funding, but it will take some time for that action to increase, and that will always be bottlenecked if you minimise the funding being put in, in individual local authorities, to empty property officers, which brings me back to the points you made about redirection of council funding and council tax premiums. Where that''s been redirected into the empty properties team, we''re seeing it growing and flourishing, whereas where we don''t see that, we can see the reverse occurring.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Dr Henry Dawson', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 1', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=2238&l=en', NULL, 155.2, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; council tax; council tax premiums; empty properties; empty property; evidence session; inquiry', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.119707', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775282', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 So, on that and on the progress we have made, of course, it came into force just before the end of the previous Senedd, at the end of April, and since then, since it''s come into force and gone onto the statute book, officials have started work on the national strategy on BSL and, of course, the statutory guidance that were a part of what the Act was calling for, and those two things have to be published within 18 months of the passing of the Bill. So, the work has truly started on that. We''ve had a session with the BSL stakeholder group to get their initial views on what that strategy should look like, what the guidance should look like, and we are also now recruiting the BSL adviser. You''ll recall that that was a very important part of the legislation, and we were very clear that we needed to get that adviser in place as soon as possible so that they could lead the work, then, of ensuring that we facilitate and support the use of BSL in Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=4958&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.067320', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771307', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
The Welsh Government has published its new Programme for Government today, which sets out its priorities and plans over the four-year Senedd term, following the Senedd election on 7 th May.

The Welsh Government has published its new Programme for Government today, which sets out its priorities and plans over the four-year Senedd term, following the Senedd election on 7 th May.

A Programme for Government is published by a new government at the start of each Senedd term. It outlines what Welsh Government ministers want to achieve before the next election.
Launched by First Minister Rhun ap Iorwerth today, the document focuses on the new Government’s key priorities - bringing down waiting lists, supporting families with the cost of living, creating good, well-paid jobs, raising educational standards, tackling child poverty, and standing up for Wales.
The First Minister said:
“This Programme for Government is our blueprint for change. It sets a new ambition for Wales to realise our vision of a modern, thriving, ambitious and confident nation.
“For too long, silo working has stood in the way of progress. This new government is taking a different approach. By bringing imagination and innovation to the way decisions are made and priorities are set, we will work across government departments – not just within them – to address the greatest challenges facing our society today.
“Our focus is on turning our early action into lasting results - by getting NHS waiting lists down, ensuring a more sustainable future for our NHS, helping families with the cost of living, creating good jobs, raising standards in our schools and tackling child poverty.
“We will be uncompromising in standing up for Wales and demanding fairness from Westminster, making the case for empowering the Senedd with the tools that will enable us to be bolder and more radical in how we change our citizens’ lives for the better.
“We were elected to get Wales moving after years of drift and stagnation. That remains our mission.”', 'https://media.service.gov.wales/news/programme-for-government-sets-a-new-ambition-for-wales', '2026-09-21', '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', 'First Minister; First Minister - Rhun ap Iorwerth', '', '', NULL, 31.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-22T11:59:04.698051', 'newsroom:programme-for-government-sets-a-new-ambition-for-wales', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 You asked what support the Welsh Government can provide. Well, of course, we are working with local partners. The county council is in all likelihood the first port of call in terms of understanding the problems that may continue now because of what''s happened, and also NRW in terms of restoring the land and any work that needs to happen on the nature side of things. But you''re right, this is shocking and makes us realise the severity of the situation in terms of the climate, the nature emergency and climate emergency that we''re facing here. I don''t think anybody could start to deny that that''s very real. Between these fires and this incredible heat that we''ve had, which is also affecting people in very negative ways, we genuinely have to tackle this crisis facing us in Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Topical Questions', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=6284&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies; Housing minister', '', 'temporary accommodation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.302236', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768218', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 And in terms of the housing associations bringing old stock up to standard, there is another grant available for that, the optimised retrofit scheme, which is very successful in terms of improving the condition of our social housing. We need to accelerate that, and we also need to see the private sector stock progressing as well, so that the quality of our housing everywhere and across all tenacies improves.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5677&l=en', NULL, 88.0, 'High', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency; social_housing', 'Housing system; Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'Optimised Retrofit; housing associations; retrofit; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.240470', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768222', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 In terms of the NHS and the local health boards, there is a very real and frequent interaction between the Minister for health and all local health boards, and any local health boards that are particularly struggling in the delivery of services. You will have heard him speak of the framework of accountability this week, or last week, so those are regular interactions and challenges within this system.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16294', '2026-10-01', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '6. Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 - Pre-budget scrutiny: Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16294', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16294?startPos=8856&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; draft budget', 0, '2026-10-02T12:57:51.915531', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16294&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#774272', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 One of the things that I want to see is fairness for council tax payers in Wales. You often talk about the need for fair funding between the England and Wales settlement—I get that, I completely get that and we support you on fair funding—but we need fair funding in Wales and we need fairness in council tax bills for people who have to struggle to pay them each and every year. The average band D council tax bill in Wales has risen by more than £600 over the past five years, and some residents have faced hikes of over 50 per cent over that period. It''s totally unacceptable. We need urgent action to protect people from the sorts of excessive rises that some people have been having. So, can I urge you again: introduce a cap, legislate so that people have to have referendums for anything above that cap and do one more thing too, let''s have a review—an independent review, truly independent—of the local government funding formula to make sure that there''s fair funding to all parts of Wales, not just places favoured by the previous Labour Government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16262', '2026-09-22', 'Darren Millar', '', '171', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16262', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16262?startPos=1540&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'council tax', 0, '2026-09-23T11:54:34.413169', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16262&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770721', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

 Just to note that, in partnership with housing associations, we have had some real progress with that historically, but with particularly problematic and particularly pre-1920s properties that are harder to insulate and suchlike, the Welsh housing quality standard is actually a bit of an obstacle to housing associations and council landlords coming on board with these bodies. So, having a separate outfit that lets them use that in a community-based initiative, but something that doesn''t require WHQS, might be a solution for unlocking some more of those problem properties.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16305', '2026-10-01', 'Dr Henry Dawson', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties: Evidence session 1', '16305', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16305?startPos=3398&l=en', NULL, 258.8, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'private_rented_sector; energy_efficiency; taxation_of_property; social_housing; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Private rented sector; Property & energy; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; WHQS; Welsh housing quality standard; community asset; empty properties; evidence session; housing associations; inquiry; landlords', 0, '2026-10-07T06:31:25.293456', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16305&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#775315', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

